HT_DATA.load("detail", {"LkH2r-sNjQs":{"wy":"HN treats it as the go-to layperson-friendly argument against e-voting and links it in nearly every voting-technology thread.","q":{"x":"Tom Scott still has the best argument against e-voting IMO [1].","i":24993684,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":44657184,"author":"david-gpu","month":"2025-07","story":"US companies, consumers are paying for tariffs, not foreign firms","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent explanation of why electronic voting is terrible.","quote":"Tom Scott provided an excellent explanation of why electronic voting is a terrible idea.","text":"Tom Scott provided an excellent explanation of why electronic voting is a terrible idea. Original: [LINK] Follow-up: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28963528,"author":"space_fountain","month":"2021-10","story":"Blockchain voting is overrated among uninformed but underrated among informed","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Digital exploits scale far better than attacks on paper elections.","quote":"remains the most convincing argument to me that it's a bad idea","text":"I think the Tom Scott video on electronic voting from years ago remains the most convincing argument to me that it's a bad idea ( [VIDEO] ). To summarize, even assuming you can solve properties like correctness, censorship resistance, privacy and coercion resistance. The fundamental problem with digital is that any exploit of any of these properties you ever have scales really well. Messing with a paper election requires a lot of people working together across the entire country. A digital hack just requires one smart person"},{"id":25924688,"author":"Qub3d","month":"2021-01","story":"Dominion Voting Systems Sues Rudy Giuliani","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers open source and blockchain as proposed e-voting fixes.","quote":"Tom Scott made an excellent summary of why electronic voting is a bad idea","text":"Tom Scott made an excellent summary of why electronic voting is a bad idea, and explicitly covers both open source and blockchain as proposed solutions (hint: they don't solve the problems that make e-voting bad) [VIDEO]"},{"id":24993684,"author":"greenduck","month":"2020-11","story":"Let's put e-voting where it belongs: on the trash-heap of bad ideas (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Elections only count if everybody can believe the results.","quote":"Tom Scott still has the best argument against e-voting IMO [1].","text":"Tom Scott still has the best argument against e-voting IMO [1]. Briefly: an election only counts if everybody can believe the results. Making an expert level understanding of CS a requirement to verify your voting system means that Joe Q. Average who doesn't hold a PhD (or maybe even a college degree) has to rely on spooky experts telling him what to believe. If I were in his shoes then I would have no confidence that I participated in a fair and valid election. We kind of live in a bubble here on HN where most people are sort of in the tech space and could take a weekend or two to understand blockchain. I think its easy to forget that most people don't have the required background to learn it easily (or would want to use up their time to understand it). I almost have a PhD in the hard sciences and I don't fully understand the finer details of block chain. I think I would have to write my own implementation to fully appreciate it. Simplicity and the ability to explain the system to every American is a requirement of any voting system. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37220573,"author":"teddyh","month":"2023-08","story":"Brazilian hacker claims Bolsonaro asked him to hack into the 2022 voting system","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"","text":"Please explain how a “digital election system” could ever be a sane idea? • Why Electronic Voting is a BAD Idea < [LINK] > • Why Electronic Voting Is Still A Bad Idea < [VIDEO] >"},{"id":34914330,"author":"ChickenNugger","month":"2023-02","story":"Bankman-Fried, others made 300 illegal political donations, new indictment says","stance":"rec","why":"Second video explaining why electronic voting is a terrible idea.","quote":"Here's Tom Scott's second video explaining why electronic voting is a terrible idea","text":"Here's Tom Scott's second video explaining why electronic voting is a terrible idea: [VIDEO] Electronic voting machines and companies that manufacture them should both be illegal. Yet their use is widespread in America. The primary reason they're a bad idea is they make widespread election fraud easy. There is no good faith reason to use them."},{"id":30840651,"author":"danuker","month":"2022-03","story":"Debian decides to allow secret votes","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"Relevant watching: Tom Scott: Why Electronic Voting Is Still A Bad Idea","text":"I am not familiar with Debian's practices, but electronic voting can't work for a nation because: - centralization (there must be a central, corruptible place where the voters are authenticated or the votes are counted) - software is untrustable: [LINK] The USA has had a Diebold voting scandal. Other countries are using a paper-based voting process which can be supervised by third parties. [LINK] Relevant watching: Tom Scott: Why Electronic Voting Is Still A Bad Idea [VIDEO] One possible solution could be ZCash ( [LINK] ) but it has had its own problems ( [LINK] )."},{"id":29946019,"author":"V__","month":"2022-01","story":"It’s not still the early days of blockchain","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"Obligatory Tom Scott videos","text":"Obligatory Tom Scott videos: Why Electronic Voting is a BAD Idea - Computerphile | [LINK] Why Electronic Voting Is Still A Bad Idea | [VIDEO]"},{"id":26150187,"author":"lukifer","month":"2021-02","story":"YouTubers have to declare ads. Why doesn't anyone else? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Compelling, layperson-accessible argument against electronic voting.","quote":"a compelling, layperson-accessible argument against electronic voting","text":"Relevant to HN's interests: a compelling, layperson-accessible argument against electronic voting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24176271,"author":"kfrzcode","month":"2020-08","story":"USPS Files Patent for a Blockchain-Based Voting System","stance":"rec","why":"Great explanation of the basics against electronic voting.","quote":"Here's Tom Scott with a great explanation of the basics.","text":"Electronic voting is a terrible idea. Attacks on paper & pen ballot systems are much, much harder to scale. Here's Tom Scott with a great explanation of the basics. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22641007,"author":"roywiggins","month":"2020-03","story":"Detailed audit of Voatz' voting app confirms security flaws","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why elections differ from reversible bank transactions.","quote":"I'll let Tom Scott take it","text":"Many bank transactions can be reversed, and the ones that can't can be covered by insurance or self-insurance. You can't practically speaking reverse a tainted election. Anyway, I'll let Tom Scott take it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46715082,"author":"jcynix","month":"2026-01","story":"Internet voting is insecure and should not be used in public elections","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Electronic processes are way to easy to rig one way or the other.: Tom Scott: Why Electronic Voting Is Still A Bad Idea [VIDEO] Sure, there are ways to cheat with paper votes too. But counting paper ballots should always be open to watch for voters interested in observing the process. And voting should be done in secret, disallowing photos, to make it hard to \"prove\" the vote to possible buyers."},{"id":35280759,"author":"logifail","month":"2023-03","story":"The drama in trying to convert election PDFs to Spreadsheets","stance":"reference","why":"Attacks against paper voting do not scale well.","quote":"attacks against it don't scale well","text":"> By looking at the receipts printed by the ballot machines. Let's the clear, you're not really \"recounting\" the ballots at that point. If the machine is compromised - and we're discussing a situation in which we know CCTV was removed and people were then shot - you have no real idea if the receipt corresponds to the voter's original intent. Or, indeed, if all the receipts from all the voters make it as far as the recount (?) > Ballot machines print either a final tally at the end of the day, or print every single vote and automatically drop it into a physical ballot, depending on the threat model of the country in question. How is reprinting the final automated tally supposed to represent a \"recount\" of the original automated tally? > Either way the you have partial or total recount. You really don't. Bits of paper and Mark I eyeballs all the way. As Tom Scott puts it, \"The key point is not is that paper voting is perfect - it isn't - but attacks against it don't scale well\"[0]. [0] Why Electronic Voting Is Still A Bad Idea: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30918204,"author":"AussieWog93","month":"2022-04","story":"Man hacked random-number generator to rig lotteries, investigators say (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Secure systems must also be understandable and trusted by the public.","quote":"it's not just enough for a system to be truly, cryptographically secure. The public must also be able to understand and trust the chain","text":"Tom Scott once made the very good point in one of is videos[1] that it's not just enough for a system to be truly, cryptographically secure. The public must also be able to understand and trust the chain that keeps the system safe. By this measure, perhaps the best RNG is still Bev from the local RSL hand-cranking her bingo cage. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26918889,"author":"kosievdmerwe","month":"2021-04","story":"UK court clears post office staff convicted due to ‘corrupt data’","stance":"reference","why":"Voter trust matters more than actual machine security.","quote":"","text":"Yeah, electronic voting is essentially like having a person in the voting booth that you have to tell your vote and trust that they will tally it correctly. [1] It doesn't matter whether voting machines are actually secure, they probably mostly are right now, but whether a layperson can have faith in the system. Paper voting is very secure if you involve people from opposing parties in the process and attacks are not very scalable. Most people can think of and understand mitigations for certain kinds of attacks. And if paper voting is too expensive for your country, you have bigger issues. [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] That said, I don't see how secure electronic voting can possibly be cheaper than paper voting. For voting machines to be secure, you have to manufacture them in a very audited manner, with little to no foreign sourcing of parts, you can't leave the machines unattended for long periods of time (aka, reusing them between elections is probably a no-go) and you have to build them in manner that is secure against voters tampering with them in their private booth."},{"id":26223713,"author":"irjustin","month":"2021-02","story":"Ethereum Isn't Fun Anymore","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm against doing electronic voting and tracking [0]. Others have commented on the tracking/anonymous issues, so I'll leave that. Make the whole system as hard as possible to manipulate by involving a lot of people who distrust the person they're working with. There is a tiny chance your personal vote will get lost in all the paper handling, but you can be assured that the system is a lot harder to manipulate simply because of the sheer number of people involved. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24993501,"author":"SahAssar","month":"2020-11","story":"Let's put e-voting where it belongs: on the trash-heap of bad ideas (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Related (and amusing) links","text":"I think you're missing a few things: Votes should never be identifiable and the count should be verifiable. First of all: using somewhat public things like SSN, \"numbers in your street address\" and a credit card number is a terrible idea. All of those have been leaked and are present on things you present to identify yourself or pay. Also requiring a credit card or home to vote would almost certainly be unconstitutional. Getting a \"receipt\" is also problematic: You should never be able to prove you voted A over B or vice versa since that opens up ways to intimidate people to vote one way and coerce them to prove it. I'm not saying it's impossible but there are so many problems with electronic voting that I don't even know where to start. At least with physical ballots we can manually recount if we need. And that's before we even start talking about how current systems are basically swiss cheese for hacks, just look at the voting village for the last couple of defcons. Related (and amusing) links: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":23716463,"author":"bragh","month":"2020-07","story":"Estonian Electronic Identity Card: Security Flaws in Key Management","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Tom Scott's video about e-voting.","text":"It's not about it being compulsory, but the system being unverifiable end-to-end and any criticism of that being laughed at. If you put it into business terms, would you trust an employee or vendor who told you that everything was alright, did not allow you to perform checks and audits and mocked both your and external partners concerns [0] about it? I don't think so. If the government is indeed for the people and not vice versa, then this is not acceptable. [0] [VIDEO] Tom Scott's video about e-voting. Funniest rebuttal I saw on Estonian social media was that we are secure, since he is talking about e-voting, but we have i-voting. So I guess once we will call it c-voting, it will be even better...?"},{"id":22233649,"author":"Lycake","month":"2020-02","story":"Iowa Caucus App Error","stance":"reference","why":"Follow-up on why electronic voting remains a bad idea.","quote":"He also made \"Why Electronic Voting Is Still A Bad Idea\" last month","text":"This was 2014. You might think with newer technologies this isn't a problem anymore. He also made \"Why Electronic Voting Is Still A Bad Idea\" last month: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22252169,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2020-02","story":"A Link to the Shadow Inc. App That Blew Up the Iowa Caucus","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's electronic voting. \"Votes in transit\" (Scott 2019): Why Electronic Voting Is Still A Bad Idea: [VIDEO] It's also a standard incrementalism tatic."}],"r":[["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["wfYbgdo8e-8",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["snAhsXyO3Ck",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["uHQ4WCU1WQc",1],["2SuvO4Gi7uY",1],["XfQUOHlAocY",1],["Y5K9HBiJpuk",1],["lG4VkPoG3ko",1],["esQyYGezS7c",1],["r3snVCRo_bI",1],["Erp8IAUouus",1],["NGFhc8R_uO4",1],["kH5APw_SLUU",1],["tIuj-oMN-Fk",1],["pdmODVYPDLA",1],["VPBH1eW28mo",1],["-6BsiVyC1kM",1],["-iCIZQX9i1A",1]]},"30jNsCVLpAE":{"wy":"A perennial HN recommendation for outage and postmortem threads, praised as funny, educational and one of the best postmortems anyone has seen.","q":{"x":"The best postmortem I've seen was actually a conference talk","i":35036065,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35036065,"author":"KronisLV","month":"2023-03","story":"Ask HN: What is the best postmortem you've seen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed datacenter outage postmortem covering human factors, automation and architecture","quote":"The best postmortem I've seen was actually a conference talk","text":"The best postmortem I've seen was actually a conference talk: \"Debugging Under Fire: Keep your Head when Systems have Lost their Mind\" by Bryan Cantrill (in the GOTO conference, 2017) Here's a YouTube video of it: [VIDEO] Here's the slides: [LINK] It goes into detail about a pretty bad outage (when an entire data center was brought down), the human aspects, automation, how they handled it, the various risks, architectures, how things fail and about software development in general."},{"id":34584940,"author":"int0x2e","month":"2023-01","story":"WAN router IP address change blamed for global Microsoft 365 outage","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A favorite talk by a favorite speaker on handling outages","quote":"Time to share one of my favorite talks (and speakers) ever","text":"Time to share one of my favorite talks (and speakers) ever - \"Debugging Under Fire: Keep your Head when Systems have Lost their Mind\" (Bryan Cantrill, GOTO 2017) [VIDEO]"},{"id":30052356,"author":"OmarAssadi","month":"2022-01","story":"Famous outages along with deep postmortems?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Interesting, funny talk about a Joyent outage","quote":"Bryan Cantrill, who gives my favorite talks, has an interesting and funny talk on one of the Joyent outages","text":"Perhaps not famous, but Bryan Cantrill, who gives my favorite talks, has an interesting and funny talk on one of the Joyent outages: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36854124,"author":"cls59","month":"2023-07","story":"40 years ago yesterday Air Canada Flight 143 ran out of fuel mid-flight","stance":"rec","why":"Parallels between Air Canada 143 and semi-automated software systems","quote":"Bryan Cantrill draws some excellent parallels between this incident and software systems","text":"Bryan Cantrill draws some excellent parallels between this incident and software systems in his \"Debugging Under Fire\" talk (beware of semi-automated systems): [VIDEO]"},{"id":32396616,"author":"OmarAssadi","month":"2022-08","story":"The Story of Mel (1983)","stance":"rec","why":"One of Cantrill's recommended talks on debugging production systems","quote":"All of his other presentations are great too and definitely worth a listen","text":"I’m surprised I haven’t seen this linked yet, but Bryan Cantrill of dtrace, Sun, lawnmower, Joyent, etc, fame gave an amazing talk for Monktoberfest 2016, titled “Oral Tradition in Software Engineering”, which features The Story of Mel [1]. Highly recommend checking it out — there are loads of little gems and stories like this throughout. All of his other presentations are great too and definitely worth a listen if you like this sort of thing [2]. A couple of my favorites are “Fork Yeah! The Rise and Development of Illumos” [3] and “Debugging Under Fire: Keep your Head when Systems have Lost their Mind” [4]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30763371,"author":"wingmanjd","month":"2022-03","story":"Hackers claim to have breached Okta systems","stance":"rec","why":"Managing a long outage, including sleep management for the team","quote":"Bryan Cantrill gives a great presentation about managing an outage","text":"I suppose it all depends on how much infra needs to be stood up for the absolute necessities of the business to operate. Does the company need that internal ticketing system in place to process external client transactions? Probably not, but it'll need it eventually (so maybe that moves to 2nd tier restore process?). My company's RTO is 24hrs to processing new client transactions. Restoring old ones will definitely take longer, but at least new ones can proceed. If your own company's RTO is 2w, that sounds like a lot needs to be in place. Part of the business continuity/ disaster recovery is getting management to sign off on those types of numbers, big or small. Make sure they're realistic. You're right that this type of recovery is not fun. Bryan Cantrill gives a great presentation about managing an outage ( [VIDEO] ). One of my biggest takeaways, if you're looking at a sweeping outage and a long haul of a recovery, do sleep management asap with your team. Dead tired people are more likely to make brain dead decisions."},{"id":28904180,"author":"KronisLV","month":"2021-10","story":"Is it time to rewrite the Operating System in Rust? (2018) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Enjoyable, humorous Cantrill talk on production debugging","quote":"i rather enjoyed Bryan Cantrill's talk from 2017","text":"I'll give it a watch and will summarize, because while i'm not always on board with the Rust hype (and therefore want to know more, to help eliminate my biases, one way or the other). That said, i rather enjoyed Bryan Cantrill's talk from 2017, \"Debugging Under Fire: Keep your Head when Systems have Lost their Mind • Bryan Cantrill • GOTO 2017\": [VIDEO] So i wouldn't necessarily turn away from any of his videos just because of the sometimes humorous or not awfully serious tone."},{"id":28235824,"author":"e12e","month":"2021-08","story":"Harbormaster: Anti-Kubernetes for your personal server","stance":"rec","why":"Cantrill talk on debugging production systems","quote":"worth watching IMNHO","text":"Not (I think) the exacttalk/blog post gp was thinking of - but worth watching IMNHO: \"Debugging Under Fire: Keep your Head when Systems have Lost their Mind • Bryan Cantrill • GOTO 2017\" [VIDEO] Ed: oh, here we go I think? > Running Aground: Debugging Docker in Production Bryan Cantrill19,102 views16 Jan 2018 Talk originally given at DockerCon '15, which (despite being a popular presentation and still broadly current) Docker Inc. has elected to delist. [LINK]"},{"id":26273161,"author":"KronisLV","month":"2021-02","story":"Google admits Kubernetes container tech is too complex","stance":"rec","why":"Funny, educational account of Joyent's region-wide reboot incident","quote":"a pretty funny and educational talk by Bryan Cantrill about that particular incident","text":"> Joyent famously took down their whole region by rebooting wrong nodes. In case anyone's interested, here's a pretty funny and educational talk by Bryan Cantrill about that particular incident: GOTO 2017 • Debugging Under Fire: Keep your Head when Systems have Lost their Mind [VIDEO]"},{"id":24573866,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2020-09","story":"They're deleting my channel, but they don't know why? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Operator mistyped command rebooted an entire datacenter","quote":"I highly recommend Bryan Cantrill's talk[1] about the time an operator accidentally rebooted an entire datacenter","text":"> I run a YouTube channel that is over 10 years old , has over 770,000 subscribers , almost 600 public videos , 120,000,000 views When writing software involved in managing a live, public, massively multi-user system, the traditional unix-style commands that are immediate, often silent, and capable of damaging effects become a really easy way to shoot yourself in the foot. Worse, some commands might let you accidentally shoot everyone's foot on a typo. The traditional example is accidentally typing something like \"rm -f * .bak\" (note the extra space after the star). For a good discussion of this type of problem, I highly recommend Bryan Cantrill's talk[1] about the time an operator accidentally rebooted an entire datacenter with a single miss-typed command. The general solution to this is building sanity checks into the software. The user just asked to reformat 500 hosts, but almost all previous uses olf the 'reformat' command affected less than 10 hosts. Maybe we should ask for verification from an actual human if they really intended to run this unusually destructive command. Why doesn't YouTube have this kind of sanity check in their automated takedown/strike/channel-deletion tools? Google wrote automation that can decide to delete a channel with a long history and many successful videos. Why doesn't that automation have basic sanity checks that ask for operator input when asked to do an unusually destructive action like deleting a 10 year old channel with a huge history? "},{"id":20423630,"author":"merlincorey","month":"2019-07","story":"Root cause analysis: significantly elevated error rates on 2019‑07‑10","stance":"rec","why":"Dev versus ops mindsets when handling live fires","quote":"Bryan Cantrill has a great talk[0] about dealing with fires","text":"> In my experience customers deeply detest the idea of waiting around for a failure case to re-occur so that you can understand it better. Bryan Cantrill has a great talk[0] about dealing with fires where he says something to the effect of: > Now you will find out if you are more operations or development - developers will want to leave things be to gather data and understand, while operations will want to rollback and fix things as quickly as possible [0] Debugging Under Fire: Keep your Head when Systems have Lost their Mind - Bryan Cantrill: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42685364,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2025-01","story":"Debugging: Indispensable rules for finding even the most elusive problems (2004)","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker's own talk pulling together his debugging methodology","quote":"A talk that pulls many of these together is my GOTO Chicago talk in 2017","text":"I was! (Along with co-author Dave Pacheco.) And I still have the dream that we'll finish it one day: we had written probably a third of it, but then life intervened in various dimensions. And indeed, as part of our preparation to write our book (which we titled The Joy of Debugging ), we read Wheeler's Debugging . On the one hand, I think it's great to have anything written about debugging, as it's a subject that has not been treated with the weight that it deserves. But on the other, the \"methodology\" here is really more of a collection of aphorisms; if folks find it helpful, great -- but I came away from Debugging thinking that the canonical book on debugging has yet to be written. Fortunately, my efforts with Dave weren't for naught: as part of testing our own ideas on the subject, I gave a series of presentations from ~2015 to ~2017 that described our thinking. A talk that pulls many of these together is my GOTO Chicago talk in 2017, on debugging production systems.[0] That talk doesn't incorporate all of our thinking, but I think it gets to a lot of it -- and I do think it stands at a bit of contrast to Wheeler's work. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36793801,"author":"drbawb","month":"2023-07","story":"Cold restart whole system after total outage","stance":"reference","why":"Retrospective on an entire datacenter rebooting","quote":"includes a retrospective of sorts about an entire datacenter rebooting","text":"I'm reminded of Bryan Cantrill's talk \"Debugging Under Fire\"[1], which includes a retrospective of sorts about an entire datacenter rebooting.[2] That is a pretty large-scale disaster, but even that is a rung below a continent-wide outage. Poor \"Bill\" must have saw the proverbial light when he heard some folks wanted to trash the DIASTER script. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":33629038,"author":"vaidhy","month":"2022-11","story":"Amazon confirms corporate staff cuts that could hit 10k employees","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as another expert take on system design","quote":"Here is another take from Bryan Cantrill","text":"If it were so obvious, I am wondering why you need to worry about doing it wrong. You talk about trade-offs as if they are fixed in time. That is not the case at all in anything but toy systems. Maybe you need to find someone who can design a quality micro-service? You have no idea what I do and what my competencies are, yet, you are assuming you know a lot more about system design than I do and I am missing something simple. Here is another take from Bryan Cantrill [ [VIDEO] ]. Very likely, he does not know anything about services either, I guess."},{"id":31942894,"author":"KronisLV","month":"2022-07","story":"Upptime/upptime: Uptime monitor and status page powered by GitHub","stance":"reference","why":"Restarting a whole data center and its implications","quote":"I recall Bryan Cantrill doing an interesting presentation","text":"> All I say is that you must relay on externals if A-E are all on the same network as it may go down. Thankfully, it's not too hard to take advantage of multiple networks in a hybrid/multi-cloud setup nowadays! Though, depending on the necessary access controls and auditing, such a setup might require slightly more work. You do bring up an excellent point, though, about how it's a serious single point of failure in many systems out there, because personally I've also seen many setups like that (the majority of them, actually): I do suspect that in many cases that is indeed done for ease of use/convenience, even if it may lead to downtime. Of course, in some cases downtime is acceptable, so I cannot argue that it can also make sense to choose such a simpler setup - for example, for having your own company's applications/monitoring for development environments all on the same network. Though if this topology is retained at scale, things can get a bit interesting. On a similar note, I recall Bryan Cantrill doing an interesting presentation \"Debugging Under Fire: Keep your Head when Systems have Lost their Mind\" that talked about restarting their whole data center and the implications of that: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25418103,"author":"znpy","month":"2020-12","story":"Google outage – resolved","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as a related talk during an outage thread","quote":"","text":"It reminds me of this: [VIDEO] -- \"GOTO 2017 • Debugging Under Fire: Keep your Head when Systems have Lost their Mind • Bryan Cantrill\""},{"id":17098814,"author":"tonyarkles","month":"2018-05","story":"The sad state of sysadmin in the age of containers (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill's line on dev versus ops instincts during outages","quote":"Bryan Cantrill had a good line about DevOps in one of his talks","text":"> I'm not sure you do, since you admitted to really being at least part sysadmin, earlier :) Hah! I wish I could remember which talk it was. Bryan Cantrill had a good line about DevOps in one of his talks (I think it was this one: [VIDEO] ). The gist was \"you can say you're DevOps, but when the shit hits the fan, you're either going to be Dev or Ops. If you're a Dev, you're going to want to debug the problem before rebooting the failed machine. If you're Ops, you're going to want to reboot the machine as fast as you can to get it back up.\" Through that lens, I'm definitely pretty far over on the Dev spectrum; when something goes catastrophically wrong and someone reboots a box to \"solve the problem\", my first reaction is \"YOU FUCKER YOU BURNED THE CORPSE\" It's such a tricky thing all around. Looking back at what I wrote earlier, I also realize that in some ways I'm facilitating the ignorance. I've got a really nice Consul and Nomad setup for the team, so they can pretty much just toss .wars and Docker containers at the cluster and they'll automatically get scheduled somewhere with spare capacity. The load balancer, the database cluster, all of the service discovery and job scheduling stuff... they've never had to get in and set any of that up. Maybe it's time to do more mentoring... Anyway, thanks for getting me thinking. I've been a little bit grouchy lately about all of the recent experiences of people not knowing how the stuff they build actually runs. You've been a great "}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"JjDsP5n2kSM":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a talk whose advice on simplicity and avoiding unnecessary work applies well beyond games.","q":{"x":"It's about much more than games. To me, it's about identifying and not doing unnecessary work.","i":12639113,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":26246740,"author":"tompark","month":"2021-02","story":"Making Games on Your Own as an Engineer","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Q&A on finishing Braid by keeping the project technically simple.","quote":"For many years one of my favorite talks about indie games development was Jonathan Blow's speech","text":"TFA has some good points. I'd go even further on the situation where you quit your job to work on an indie game -- if you ever find yourself doing this, and you've never built a game by yourself before, you'd better have several years of runway. For many years one of my favorite talks about indie games development was Jonathan Blow's speech at UC Berkeley CSUA (2011) ( [LINK] ) Initially I loved his main message during the talk: \"You have to be brutally effective to finish a project in a reasonable amount of time.\" But there's a part in the Q&A that later grew to be even more poignant to me: [VIDEO] > Q: You said you worked on many projects before Braid which didn't quite get completed. What was special about Braid which made you complete it? > A: What was special was it had been a long time since I'd finished anything on my own, and I sat down and decided that \"Dammit I'm going to finish something this time\". And, \"To that end, so I can finish this, I'm gonna make something that is technically pretty simple\"... For some of us, it's REALLY HARD to complete a side-project while working full-time job. For most of my career I had completed a real game only when it was for a job (or for a client) and I worked on it full-time. The single reason I hadn't made a game as a side project was because I'd quit before I finished. I finally figured out something that worked for me, and it was counter to all the advice I'd heard. I'm sure this is different for other people, but for me the k"},{"id":16839792,"author":"Stwerner","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk with huge impact on how commenter thinks about software.","quote":"Three talks that I tend to reference a lot and have had a huge impact on the way I think about software","text":"Really excited to go through all the other talks listed here I'm not familiar with! Three talks that I tend to reference a lot and have had a huge impact on the way I think about software: The Soul of Software: Avdi Grimm - [LINK] The Humane Representation of Thought: Bret Victor - [LINK] How to Program Independent Games: Jonathan Blow - [VIDEO]"},{"id":13521783,"author":"psyc","month":"2017-01","story":"Ask HN: Which developers do you closely follow?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shipping working software before complexity collapses it; optimize developer effort.","quote":"Every time I get stuck on a project, I watch this video.","text":"Every time I get stuck on a project, I watch this video. It's about games, but IMO a lot of it applies to software projects in general, especially MVPs. How to ship something that works before it collapses under its own complexity. I think the bit about remembering to optimize for developer effort is gold. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12639113,"author":"bajsejohannes","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"About avoiding unnecessary work; applies beyond games; skip Q&A half.","quote":"It's about much more than games. To me, it's about identifying and not doing unnecessary work.","text":"Jon Blow's \"How to program independent games\": [VIDEO] It's about much more than games. To me, it's about identifying and not doing unnecessary work. The second half of this video is a Q&A session, which I would skip."},{"id":47427687,"author":"nlawalker","month":"2026-03","story":"Rob Pike's 5 Rules of Programming","stance":"rec","why":"Blow explains using simple arrays first to ship, optimizing for years of life.","quote":"This reminds me of a portion of a talk Jonathan Blow gave[1]","text":"This reminds me of a portion of a talk Jonathan Blow gave[1], where he justifies this from a productivity angle. He explains how his initial implementation for virtually everything in Braid used arrays of records, and only after finding bottlenecks did he make changes, because if he had approached every technical challenge by trying to find the optimal data structure and algorithm he would never have shipped. \"There's a third thing [beyond speed and memory] that you might want to optimize for which is much more important than either of these, which is years of your life required per program implementation.\" This is of course from the perspective of a solo indie game developer, but it's a good and interesting perspective to consider. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25663210,"author":"lackbeard","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: What does mastery look like in software engineering?","stance":"rec","why":"Get good by shipping many programs, not specializing narrowly.","quote":"A long time ago I saw a good talk by Jonathan Blow wherein he made a point that really resonated with me.","text":"Example masters: John Carmack, Jeff Dean, Bram Cohen. What do they do? I think they're all extremely effective at getting things done. So be smart, hard working, and laser focused on shipping. A long time ago I saw a good talk by Jonathan Blow wherein he made a point that really resonated with me. The point was that to get good at programming you need the experience of shipping a lot of programs and thus you should optimize your career around shipping as many good programs as you can. Contrast this with the common advice of being an expert in databases, or distributed systems, or OOP, or testing, or something like that... Edit: found the talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24921896,"author":"arriu","month":"2020-10","story":"Ask HN: Good C++ code bases to read?","stance":"rec","why":"Why simple, messy-looking code can be optimal given other factors.","quote":"I've always encouraged people to check out Jonathan Blow's talk on this topic","text":"I've always encouraged people to check out Jonathan Blow's talk on this topic ( [VIDEO] ). He points out many reasons why this type of code would actually be the optimal version when weighing other factors. Simple code is easy to write, maintain, and optimize later. Despite the code looking messy, I find it easy to understand and navigate. A counter example of truly confusing code, which kind of does similar things, would be something like this: [LINK]"},{"id":10236148,"author":"nulltype","month":"2015-09","story":"The Witness: the creator of Braid talks about his fiendishly difficult new game","stance":"rec","why":"Great programming talk with advice applicable beyond games.","quote":"he has a great talk here: about \"how to program independent games\". Much of the advice seems to apply to other programming projects too.","text":"For anyone interested in just programming, he has a great talk here: [VIDEO] about \"how to program independent games\". Much of the advice seems to apply to other programming projects too."},{"id":9011409,"author":"kelukelugames","month":"2015-02","story":"‘Braid’ creator sacrifices his fortune to build his next game","stance":"rec","why":"Jonathan Blow talk among his amazing talks.","quote":"But he has given some amazing talks.","text":"I've followed Jonathan Blow for a while. He has a reputation for being condescending. If you follow him a Twitter then you might come to the same conclusion. But he has given some amazing talks. [LINK] <-compares games to televison [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27067871,"author":"arduinomancer","month":"2021-05","story":"Crazy New Ideas","stance":"reference","why":"Talk argues against premature optimization.","quote":"I watched a talk with Jonathan Blow recently and he went on for quite a while railing against premature optimization.","text":"> Namely you should write code that is fast and efficient I don't think this is accurate. I watched a talk with Jonathan Blow recently and he went on for quite a while railing against premature optimization. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21111216,"author":"anaphor","month":"2019-09","story":"How to Succeed as a Poor Programmer","stance":"reference","why":"Advice to start with arrays as first data structure.","quote":"","text":"I don't think most of this applies only to \"poor programmers\". The advice about KISS is something many \"good\" programmers would benefit from following. Likewise, I've seen good programmers recommend using arrays as your first attempt at a data structure as well (e.g. Jonathan Blow, [VIDEO] )"},{"id":15469287,"author":"skadamat","month":"2017-10","story":"Handmade Hero – A complete game live-coded from scratch","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why game development differs from web development.","quote":"","text":"This is not uncommon in game development. Jonathan Blow and others have spoken at length about why game development is different than web development or other types of software engineering (not in a condescending way but in a \"it's different, here's why\" way): - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AxFzf6yIfcc",1],["SqFu5O-oPmU",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["0SARbwvhupQ",1]]},"36myc8wQhLo":{"wy":"Repeatedly recommended as a revelatory talk on how little control the main OS has over modern SoCs and PCs.","q":{"x":"There's this great USENIX talk by Timothy Roscoe [1]","i":33791375,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":46922393,"author":"jcgl","month":"2026-02","story":"C Isn't a Language","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Modern hardware is an amalgam of processors, not a Von Neumann machine.","quote":"I found this talk both riveting and compelling","text":"This isn’t my space to opine on, but I found this talk both riveting and compelling: [VIDEO] In a nutshell, the useful fiction of computer-as-Von-Neumann-meaning doesn’t adequately reflect the reality of modern hardware. Not only does the CPU itself not fit that model (with things like speculative execution, sophisticated power and load management…), but the system as a whole is increasingly an amalgamation of different processors and address spaces."},{"id":33791375,"author":"dist1ll","month":"2022-11","story":"Tales of the M1 GPU","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Critique of unholistic OS design and the many hidden RTOSes.","quote":"There's this great USENIX talk by Timothy Roscoe [1]","text":"There's this great USENIX talk by Timothy Roscoe [1], which is part of the Enzian Team at ETH Zürich. It's about the dominant unholistic approach to modern operating system design, which is reflected in the vast number of independent, proprietary, under-documented RTOSes running in tandem on a single system, and eventually leading to uninspiring and lackluster OS research (e.g. Linux monoculture). I'm guessing that hardware and software industries just don't have well-aligned interests, which unfortunately leaks into OS R&D. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28801915,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2021-10","story":"Rust, Wright's Law, and the Future of Low-Latency Systems","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Roscoe's OSDI keynote on OSes and modern heterogeneous hardware.","quote":"I highly recommend Timothy Roscoe's OSDI keynote[0]","text":"I know that I recommended it in the talk, but I highly recommend Timothy Roscoe's OSDI keynote[0] -- and may also be worth catching the Twitter Space we did a few weeks ago discussing it.[1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":41261551,"author":"RealityVoid","month":"2024-08","story":"Programmers Don't Read Books – But You Should (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Modern SoCs break the simple computer model.","quote":"I recently listened to this","text":"I'd say the computer architecture can vary _greatly_ and there are a bunch of cores in modern SOC's that kind of break the assumption of how a computer works. I recently listened to this: [VIDEO] Cache coherence, accelerators, various takes on pipelining, ISA extensions. There are many many things that change all the time. Not even touching the SW glue stuff that changes. I don't know the history of react and what is going on in modern browsers, but I suspect there are OS constraints, evolving web standards, extra capabilities, evolving browsers that shape and force the framework to adapt. I am doubtful people change it just for the sake of it."},{"id":35230222,"author":"jiveturkey","month":"2023-03","story":"Ask HN: Where can I find a primer on how computers boot?","stance":"rec","why":"High-level overview of modern boot, management engines before the OS.","quote":"Watch from about 10:00 to about 20:00 for a decent super high level overview.","text":"Plenty of good links here already. I'll just add that booting is enormously complex, and starts well before any of the dozen comments so far. I skimmed many of them (not all) and they all tell pretty much the same story whether on real hardware or virtual. But modern real hardware first boots a Management Engine (or BMC, or T2, or similar), which prepares things and starts subsystems like hardware controllers, before releasing the CPU where the \"OS\" finally boots, blissfully unaware that it is running on an abstraction. Watch [VIDEO] from about 10:00 to about 20:00 for a decent super high level overview. These two are also good for a deeper understanding. [LINK] [LINK] They both cover UEFI, even though the focus is on T2. I know that's well beyond what you were asking for, but the boot process goes well beyond GRUB."},{"id":34050190,"author":"Sirened","month":"2022-12","story":"How Many Computers Are in Your Computer? (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Kernels ignore massively heterogeneous SoCs.","quote":"A favorite related to talk is Roscoe's keynote titled \"It's Time for Operating Systems to Rediscover Hardware","text":"A favorite related to talk is Roscoe's keynote titled \"It's Time for Operating Systems to Rediscover Hardware\" [1] in which he discusses some of the outcomes/consequences of kernels (like Linux) largely sticking their heads in the sand on the issue of these massively heterogeneous SoCs. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33134252,"author":"zerohp","month":"2022-10","story":"How to tell a mainframe from a minicomputer from a micro","stance":"rec","why":"Modern computers are clusters of heterogeneous processors.","quote":"If you find this fact surprising, you'll probably want to take a look at this talk from USENIX ATC '21","text":"> So as someone else commented, a mainframe isn't really a computer, it's a whole cluster of closely-coupled computers, many with dedicated functionality. Quite possibly all implemented with different architectures, instruction sets, programming languages, bit widths, everything. Nearly all computers more powerful than a simple microcontroller look like this in 2022. If you find this fact surprising, you'll probably want to take a look at this talk from USENIX ATC '21: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33146105,"author":"mlindner","month":"2022-10","story":"I have come to bury the BIOS, not to open it [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the many separate OSes running inside a modern PC.","quote":"I suggest taking a look at the talk Bryan mentioned","text":"> I would really like to know if my x86 'linux PC', is actually running another 'hypervisor' OS that runs Linux - it seems like a recipe for security vulnerabilities. There's not so much a \"hypervisor\" OS, but instead a congealed set of many different OSes, some realtime OSes, some possibly old Linux variants, some possibly closed source proprietary one-off OSes. I suggest taking a look at the talk Bryan mentioned: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33346302,"author":"CrLf","month":"2022-10","story":"Disabling the Intel Management Engine","stance":"rec","why":"Main OS runs sandboxed beneath higher-privilege coprocessors.","quote":"A nice talk on this","text":"The IME gets a lot of hate around here, but let's not get distracted by it: higher-privilege co-processors running code outside the main OS' control is becoming (or already is) the norm everywhere. Intel-based PCs are just one instance of it (and perhaps not even the most egregious one). Most hardware has evolved to effectively run the main OS under a sandbox where it \"thinks\" it is in control, but isn't. A nice talk on this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30421679,"author":"the_duke","month":"2022-02","story":"I tested four NVMe SSDs from four vendors – half lose FLUSH’d data on power loss","stance":"rec","why":"Modern systems are cooperating devices each with own OS, yet main OS pretends control.","quote":"I can recommend the related talk \"It's Time for Operating Systems to Rediscover Hardware\".","text":"I can recommend the related talk \"It's Time for Operating Systems to Rediscover Hardware\". [1] It explores how modern systems are a set of cooperating devices (each with their own OS) while our main operating systems still pretend to be fully in charge. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29446354,"author":"lostdog","month":"2021-12","story":"On Hubris and Humility: developing an OS for robustness in Rust [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Raises the question of OS design across heterogeneous chips.","quote":"After watching the OS talk that Cantrill references [0], I really want to know the answer","text":"Very interesting. This reminds me a ton of L4, with its message passing style. I'm curious if it was inspired by L4. It's a great talk, and my only disappointment was that I was hoping to hear about what comes after. After watching the OS talk that Cantrill references [0], I really want to know the answer to \"what does an OS that runs across a bunch of heterogeneous chips look like?\" Having Hubris be just a single-node OS was a bit of a let down, so I will be staying tuned for the sequels. [0] [VIDEO] EDIT: Went to go read the docs and: \"The operating system that originally made this point was L4, and Hubris’s IPC mechanism is directly inspired by L4’s\""},{"id":29664672,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: \"Know Your Hardware”: best books/resources to learn?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the layers of hardware and firmware below Linux.","quote":"First of all, watch this, and learn that there's a whole stack of layers below what Linux can even touch these days.","text":"First of all, watch this, and learn that there's a whole stack of layers below what Linux can even touch these days. [VIDEO] For a theoretical understanding, with a much simpler model, I suggest the Nand2Tetris course."},{"id":45310875,"author":"intermerda","month":"2025-09","story":"Kernel: Introduce Multikernel Architecture Support","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Tim Roscoe gave an interesting Keynote at OSDI '21 titled","text":"Tim Roscoe gave an interesting Keynote at OSDI '21 titled \"It's Time for Operating Systems to Rediscover Hardware\" - [VIDEO] . He was involved with the Barrelfish project."},{"id":39602216,"author":"Ygg2","month":"2024-03","story":"Linux Crosses 4% Market Share Worldwide","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"For more","text":"Linux on Android doesn't multiplex or protect software principals from each other. It did for a brief time, but that led to massive vulnerablilities. Because Linux view of hardware (nice symmetric system with homogeneous cores running single OS with shared memory address space) was different from what was the actual state. For that reason hardware was abstracted away from Linux and it's only used to run Linux programs. It's not containerized. It's isolated to an enclave where damage it can do is minimal. Android OS is essentially a ball of propietary firmware mud. With Linux programs and Dalvik JVM. For more: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35652208,"author":"pietrushnic","month":"2023-04","story":"PC Engines EOL","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm not a RISC-V expert, primarily relying on open-source firmware community knowledge and the opinion of such figures as Ron Minnich, but AFAIK, most firmware for production RISC-V deployments is closed-source, so we can't say if vendors use OpenSBI implementation or some modified version for their malicious purposes. If I need to be corrected, please point me to products that state how they transparently use SBI. There is evidence of transparent use of SMM in coreboot. SBI is not the only RISC-V TEE. There are also other concepts, like MultiZone [1] and PMP-based Keystone [2], advertised as trusted execution environments The openness of specification only matters a little here - UEFI specification about management mode is also open. But IBVs screwed us many times [3] by implementing low-quality SMIs that could elevate privileges, and the spec does not guarantee that implementers follow it. Lack of tooling makes compliance difficult - it slowly changes with various startups seeing that as an opportunity (e.g., Binarly, Eclypsium?). My point is that SMM as TEE is not a unique x86 feature, and other significant architectures have similar mechanisms. OS has a means of figuring out that it was in SMI, so it is not entirely hidden but still has superpowers. In the CNC world SMI latency is measured from OS [4]. Trusted execution environments are hammers and can be used for a good purpose in a transparent way and for malicious purposes. Further typically depends on the trustworthin"},{"id":30422193,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2022-02","story":"I tested four NVMe SSDs from four vendors – half lose FLUSH’d data on power loss","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The operating system is not really in charge of anything","text":"> Maybe we could do all the \"smart\" stuff in the OS and application code, and just attach commodity dumb flash chips to our computer. Yeah, this is how things are supposed to be done and the fact it's not happening is a huge problem. Hardware makers isolate our operating systems in the exact same way operating systems isolate our processes. The operating system is not really in charge of anything, the hardware just gives it an illusory sanboxed machine to play around in, a machine that doesn't even reflect what hardware truly looks like. The real computers are all hidden and programmed by proprietery firmware. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30076608,"author":"andrewf","month":"2022-01","story":"The Embedded Controller and Its Legacy (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"At 9:00 the speaker lays out some pretty conventional criteria for an operating system","text":"This reminded me of a recent talk: USENIX ATC '21/OSDI '21 Joint Keynote Address-It's Time for Operating Systems to Rediscover Hardware: [VIDEO] At 9:00 the speaker lays out some pretty conventional criteria for an operating system (multiplex hardware resources, abstract the hardware platform , protect software principals from each other), and points out that these things are increasingly outside the scope of what people call \"the OS\". [LINK]"},{"id":29345364,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2021-11","story":"Bringing OS Architecture to XXI Century – Part I. Changes over Last 50 Years","stance":"reference","why":"OSDI keynote noting only three of 103 OS papers were not about Linux.","quote":"only three were not about Linux!","text":"Related: The keynote presentation from OSDI (Operating Systems Design and and Implementation) online conference: “It’s Time for Operations Systems to Rediscover Hardware”: [VIDEO] The presenter says that, of the 103 papers about operating systems published by OSDI in 2020 and 2021, only three were not about Linux!"},{"id":28804755,"author":"chubot","month":"2021-10","story":"Rust, Wright's Law, and the Future of Low-Latency Systems","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Roscoe, who gives the 2021 keynote that Cantrill recommends","text":"Reminds me of Your computer is already a distributed system. Why isn’t your OS? from HotOS 2009: [LINK] We argue that a new OS for a multicore machine should be designed ground-up as a distributed system, using concepts from that field. Modern hardware resembles a networked system even more than past large multiprocessors: in addition to familiar latency effects, it exhibits node heterogeneity and dynamic membership changes And now that I look at the author list, I see Roscoe, who gives the 2021 keynote that Cantrill recommends: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28476873,"author":"hresvelgr","month":"2021-09","story":"Dear Open Source Developers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I recently came across Timothy Roscoe's Usenix talk[1] on OS development on HN","text":"I feel like this generally applies to all software developers. Software and its systems have largely evolved through accretion and that creates a strong network effect and eventually, a taller application stack. We're continually making everything more complicated because it's just easier to use other people work, especially when the barrier to entry for that work is low, i.e: Web/JS. As a slight segue, I recently came across Timothy Roscoe's Usenix talk[1] on OS development on HN and I'm starting to think that a complete rebuild of modern computing might occur when the limits of the old OS architectures become too burdensome, at which point there could be an opportunity to \"fix\" the application stack and try something simpler. I sincerely hope the OS's of the future do not look like Linux or even attempt POSIX compliance. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Vy5Ky50eGJs",1],["BnSUk0je6oo",1],["vvZA9n3e5pc",1],["79fvDDPaIoY",1],["3LopI4YeC4I",1],["PaypcVFPs48",1],["viJt_DXTfwA",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["ajGX7odA87k",1],["xMP_AfiNlX4",1],["8QiPFmIMxFc",1],["Th4VlqQyVr4",1],["Ggz_gd--UO0",1],["MtzCLd93SyU",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1],["qCGofLIzX6g",1]]},"WUvTyaaNkzM":{"wy":"A staple recommendation for anyone learning math, praised for visual intuition that makes memorized formulas make sense.","q":{"x":"When I discovered that video, all the formulas they tried to make me memorize started making a LOT more sense.","i":36358316,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36358316,"author":"tstrimple","month":"2023-06","story":"What I Learned from Two Years of Teaching High School CS","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made memorized calculus formulas finally make sense.","quote":"When I discovered that video, all the formulas they tried to make me memorize started making a LOT more sense.","text":"I took all the advanced math classes available to me in high school. This was despite me hating school and being a terrible student. But I enjoyed math as puzzles to solve. This was until I got into calculus. The teacher didn't have a strong grasp of the subject and more importantly it was just memorizing calculus formulas instead of understanding any sort of real-world application. In contrast, my AdMatics (advanced math and physics) class had us doing real-world physics based experiments. The application of the tools was immediately obvious. If I had a calculus lesson that broke down the problem space like this ( [VIDEO] ) I probably wouldn't have stopped my formal education in math at that point. When I discovered that video, all the formulas they tried to make me memorize started making a LOT more sense."},{"id":20434472,"author":"gibsonf1","month":"2019-07","story":"Calculus for Beginners and Artists (2003)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visual approach to understanding and enjoying calculus.","quote":"I know of no better way to both learn, truly understand, and enjoy calculus than the visual approach of 3Blue1Brown","text":"I know of no better way to both learn, truly understand, and enjoy calculus than the visual approach of 3Blue1Brown in the Calculus series of videos: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17104895,"author":"willurd","month":"2018-05","story":"Ask HN: How do I learn math/physics in my thirties?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains calculus intuition as if inventing the topic.","quote":"uncanny ability to explain difficult concepts and the fundamental intuition behind them","text":"Grant (the 3Blue1Brown guy) has an uncanny ability to explain difficult concepts and the fundamental intuition behind them. In many of his videos, he explains topics from the perspective of a person inventing that topic (such as in his first Calculus video [1]). I can't recommend his videos enough. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44975521,"author":"inetknght","month":"2025-08","story":"AWS CEO says using AI to replace junior staff is 'Dumbest thing I've ever heard'","stance":"rec","why":"Teaches calculus by showing where ideas came from.","quote":"My goal is for you to come away feeling like you could have invented calculus yourself.","text":"> This was boring to most students as they had no clue why the formalism was invented in the first place. > The history of how concepts were invented for the problems faced is far more motivating to students to build a mental model than canned capsules of knowledge. That's something I really like about 3blue1brown, and he says it straight up [0]: > My goal is for you to come away feeling like you could have invented calculus yourself. That is, cover all those core ideas, but in a way that makes clear where they actually come from, and what they really mean, using an all-around visual approach. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36690129,"author":"yCombLinks","month":"2023-07","story":"Proof You Can Do Hard Things","stance":"rec","why":"A sample of 3Blue1Brown's explanatory style.","quote":"to see if you like his style","text":"Khan Academy is great, but there are also an incredible number of great youtube videos. I would watch this 3blue1brown video to see if you like his style : [VIDEO]"},{"id":27995024,"author":"loftyal","month":"2021-07","story":"Calculus Made Easy (1910)","stance":"rec","why":"Easy-to-understand calculus series with great visuals.","quote":"Its very easy to understand and somes with some great visuals","text":"I would recommend watching 3Blue1Bown series on calculus. Its very easy to understand and somes with some great visuals. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24828594,"author":"jlos","month":"2020-10","story":"Euler's Formula: A Complete Guide","stance":"rec","why":"Builds intuition behind calculus concepts.","quote":"","text":"To answer your main question - \"what level of maths\" do you need is more about what topics: a couple introductory classes in Calculus, Linear Algebra, and Discrete Mathematics will make that sentence understandable. I'll give some definitions and resources, but think about how you would explain multiplication/division/algebra to a kid. At some point, you just have to work through some problems and the math just makes sense. So I'll explain the terms above, but best bet is to just take a few courses on the topics I mentioned above. Quick definitions: - Exponential: is the constant e to the power of some number. e, like pi, is a ubitiqous constant in mathematics and nature. You run into pi when doing geometry, and e when doing calculus. - A function in mathematics is similar to a function in programming, but not exactly. A course in Discrete Mathematics helps here. - F-apostrophe is notation used in calculus to show the relation of one type of function, called a derivative to another. f and f' are related. How, though, is better explained by taking calculus. - An eigenfunction exists in a system of equations. Think of it like a 'balance' point. Linear Algebra will help make sense of this term. Resources - Essence of Calculus: [VIDEO] - Essence of Linear Algebra: [LINK] Those 2 video's will do a good job of giving you the 'intuition' behind Calculus and Linear Algebra. Like programming, though, you just have to actually work some problems out by hand for the stuff to sink in. Fo"},{"id":20572616,"author":"kebman","month":"2019-07","story":"Math Basics for Computer Science and Machine Learning [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating calculus course recommended among math channels.","quote":"Take a look at his course, Essence of Calculus","text":"Well, there's always 3Blue1Brown: [LINK] Take a look at his course, Essence of Calculus: [VIDEO] I'm not fluent in math, but I find it fascinating to watch, for some reason. NancyPi is also great: [LINK] Oh, and don't forget the Mathologer: [LINK] What a great guy! While I'm at it, go watch Numberphile too: [LINK] Is there anyone I forgot? :D"},{"id":16562324,"author":"hiaux0","month":"2018-03","story":"Ask HN: How to self-learn math?","stance":"rec","why":"Math fundamentals series for building abstract thinking.","quote":"And definitely follow through his","text":"+1 And definitely follow through his \"pause and ponder\" sections. If you want to build up your maths skills, it is crucial to learn how to think in the maths way. Like becoming a good programmer involves writing lots of code yourself, or to become a good dancer you need to practice your steps. For maths it's abstract thinking. Appreciation of maths is one thing, having the discipline to self-study a whole other. Edit. Regarding your 2nd Edit: His videos are made for the broadest audience possible. I'd recommend picking any video whose topic interests you the most at the moment. You will see what knowledge you lack (take notes of these!) and can expand from there. Be it to watch his maths fundamental ((1)) series [0],[1] or just rewatch. ((1)): As in any other things, knowing your fundamentals is significant to the understanding of a topic. It won't help you at all if you can apply (copy paste) some machine learning techniques if you don't know about linear algebra at all. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14589154,"author":"hatsunearu","month":"2017-06","story":"Georgia Tech's free math textbook collective","stance":"rec","why":"Video series for learning calculus.","quote":"check out this series","text":"[VIDEO] check out this series (he also has a really good one on linear algebra) After that, I'd check out Khan academy."},{"id":40083893,"author":"mettamage","month":"2024-04","story":"Calculus Made Easy","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as a study resource.","quote":"","text":"Mostly [VIDEO] and Khan Academy I just use YouTube a lot and ChatGPT a lot too. ChatGPT is a pretty okay tutor at high school / early college level math"}],"r":[["xbBdIG--b58",1],["ARYP83yNAWk",1],["ZQFzMfHIxng",1],["2EWejmkKlxs",1],["B90_SNNbcoU",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["bY2FlayomlE",1],["x7cQ3mrcKaY",1],["hXgqik6HXc0",1],["IsE_8j5RL3k",1],["H1KP4ztKK0A",1],["ZMQbHMgK2rw",1],["kjBOesZCoqc",1],["ObhK8lUfIlc",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1]]},"np_ylvc8Zj8":{"wy":"Commenters repeatedly recommend it in tax haven and offshore finance threads as an excellent, low-budget overview of British offshore finance.","q":{"x":"The guy who behind it not only did an excellent job","i":21570114,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":40850197,"author":"arminiusreturns","month":"2024-07","story":"Pharma firms stash profits in Europe's tax havens","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary on Britain's transition to soft-power financial empire.","quote":"really needs to go see this awesome documentary","text":"Anybody who is interested in this topic really needs to go see this awesome documentary: The Spider's Web: Britain's Second Empire | The Secret World of Finance [VIDEO] It was even done on a 4klb budget!!! The reality is that the brits realized they could no longer maintain hard power and Lionel Curtis was the primary pusher of the psyop transition to \"commonwealth\" soft power while maintaining all the real vestiges of empire under a false name."},{"id":24395409,"author":"arminiusreturns","month":"2020-09","story":"How to Hide a Billion Dollars: Three Techniques the Ultrarich Use to Dodge Ex-Sp","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Superbly done documentary made on a tiny budget.","quote":"It's a superbly done documentary","text":"If are interested in this topic and you haven't seen the independent documentary \"The Spider's Web: Britain's Second Empire\", you owe it to yourself. It's a superbly done documentary on a budget of ~4k£. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21570114,"author":"arminiusreturns","month":"2019-11","story":"Hacker Publishes 2TB of Data from Cayman National Bank","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent low-budget documentary on British offshore finance.","quote":"The guy who behind it not only did an excellent job","text":"For those interested in this subject, I can't recommend the documentary \"The Spider's Web: Britain's Second Empire\" [1] highly enough. The guy who behind it not only did an excellent job, but did it for a tiny fraction of what a bigger studio would have spent for a much more inferior end result. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21212865,"author":"marcusjt","month":"2019-10","story":"The Isle of Man is not in the UK","stance":"strong_rec","why":"British tax havens and City of London influence on policy.","quote":"This is an incredible documentary about British tax havens","text":"This is an incredible documentary about British tax havens and the influence of the City of London in the running of UK government & foreign policy [VIDEO]"},{"id":34704354,"author":"arminiusreturns","month":"2023-02","story":"Reality Check","stance":"rec","why":"Informative documentary made on a shoestring budget.","quote":"check out this excellent documentary made on a budget of","text":"This was a very wordy article that really ignores the true documentary makers that make informative documentaries on a shoestring budget because it's an important topic to them. The corporatization of everything is such a long-tail evil... If you want just one example, check out this excellent documentary made on a budget of £4k: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31710337,"author":"olivermarks","month":"2022-06","story":"Low economic growth is a slow-burning crisis for Britain","stance":"rec","why":"80-minute film on secrecy jurisdictions and offshore wealth.","quote":"80 minutes, well worth watching imo","text":"The challenge for the UK is that the flywheel of the City of London corporation and its offshore wormholes were at the core of globalization. Ironically the nation state the CoL coexists with (the UK) is as large a casualty of this as the many colonial nation states ravaged by globalization. This was compounded by the globalists - which the Economist exists to promote - forcing the merger of the UK into the european union. The vote to leave by the proletariat demonstrated massive dissatisfaction, but sadly there are subsequently no credible political parties or leadership that are not closely aligned with the CoL and/or the EU, resulting in a becalming. The Spider's Web: Britain's Second Empire | Documentary Film [VIDEO] 'Michael Oswald's film The Spider's Web reveals how at the demise of empire, City of London financial interests created a web of secrecy jurisdictions that captured wealth from across the globe and hid it in a web of offshore islands. Today, up to half of global offshore wealth is hidden in British jurisdictions and Britain and its dependencies are the largest global players in the world of international finance'. 80 minutes, well worth watching imo"},{"id":26371609,"author":"atweiden","month":"2021-03","story":"Ask HN: NFTs legit or mania?","stance":"rec","why":"Explores financialization through British offshore wealth secrecy.","quote":"an excellent documentary","text":"Kind of off-topic, but there's an excellent documentary called The Spider’s Web [1] that explores how society reached this point of valuing increasingly ridiculous displays of paper wealth and the financialization of everything through the lens of British offshore wealth secrecy schemes. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21571407,"author":"zaro","month":"2019-11","story":"Hacker Publishes 2TB of Data from Cayman National Bank","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of how offshore financial havens began.","quote":"gives a good overview of how this started.","text":"> Although I will always be curious why countries are letting those small, insignificant islands and countries manage all this wealth This is easy, after all hiding cash is needed by everybody. And when it comes to finance , there are no countries only financial interest. Of course nothing bad will happen to these financial heavens, because they are protected by the richest and most powerful people and organisations. \"The Spider's Web: Britain's Second Empire\"[1] gives a good overview of how this started. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20886171,"author":"patrickk","month":"2019-09","story":"Review of Moneyland by Oliver Bullough (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"How Britain's elite built a global offshore financial web.","quote":"I can recommend this independently created documentary","text":"If this is a topic of interest, I can recommend this independently created documentary 'The Spider's Web: Britain's Second Empire'. It's the story of how Britain's elite created a financial web spanning the globe, replacing the old empire with a new financial one, running from the Commonwealth countries back to London. That's why so many overseas British territories are associated with money laundering and tax evasion. Free to watch on YouTube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39654664,"author":"veekayji","month":"2024-03","story":"India's unrealistic demands sank WTO agri talks, claims commissioner","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Bengali cuisine today is a living testament to these times. We have dishes that use jute leaves (a waste product from the jute being produced), tree barks, mango/jackfruit seeds, even just water leftover from cooking rice (people would beg at the doors of richer houses for the rice water). We have separate dishes for ripe/unripe/overripe fruits and vegetables. Nothing could be wasted. My friends would joke in school that “God you bengalis eat everything,” but there is a grim history behind it. — source, am bengali, grew up listening to grandparents’ stories. A number of reasons but primarily British Colonia policy. When the Japanese invaded Burma in 1942 there was a huge influx of refugees into Bengal, approximately 500,000 people which strained the local economy. Additionally the British Raj government had laws which forced local suppliers of goods such as food and textiles to sell to the military at a fixed low price but no price controls for the civilian market so any goods making it to the civilian market were already hugely inflated in price. What really kicked off the famine that killed over 3 million people though was the British decision to enact a scorched earth policy in bengal in anticipation of a Japanese invasion, burning crops in the fields and destroying stockpiled food supplies. Several members of the British government in India did in fact plead to Churchills cabinet during this period for relief shipments of food to stave off the growing famine from 1942-194"},{"id":30737057,"author":"ayngg","month":"2022-03","story":"How Putin’s Oligarchs Bought London","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary source for London's offshore financial system history.","quote":"","text":"It is a little bit of both influencing each other, but the system in London is definitely the killer app. My understanding of the history is that there are systems that have been entrenched in the London financial industry going back centuries due to the unique history of the City of London and how it administrates itself separately from the rest of England/ GB. It was the global hub during pax brittania, and remained the financial hub of the world after the war thanks to the Eurodollar. It was at this point that tax systems were devised involving their crown dependencies to obfuscate everything which made it a popular place to launder money. A lot of the opulence has developed along side over all these years. So it was the case that London was the city of the world during the empire which made it the candidate for running the Eurodollar system to rebuild Europe which cemented its place as the financial capital of the world up until America financialized their economy in the late 70s, its colonies allowed for unique tax systems which made it even more popular. I got most of this from the book entitled Treasure Islands: Tax Havens and the Men Who Stole the World by Nicholas Shaxson, and the documentary [VIDEO] that was inspired by it."},{"id":29573093,"author":"ghostwriter","month":"2021-12","story":"James Dyson answers design questions from Twitter [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Points to institutions like the City of London Corporation.","quote":"","text":"Before going after individuals like Dyson in that regard, deal with institutions like the City of London Corporation and BoE first [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23306129,"author":"arminiusreturns","month":"2020-05","story":"Bahamas Company Registry Leaked","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">It seems strange to me that so many people are onboard with privacy most of the time, but when it comes to financial privacy anybody who doesn't want the government to know which books they buy is somehow a villain. This is a failure of government to protect it's peoples from supra-nationalist oligarchs who abuse various economic shell and tax avoidance friendly nations systems in order to abuse their country of origin (or country of profit origin). This is not at all about \"what books they buy\" and to say so is poor form and intellectually disingenuous. That said, you may be right that some innocents may get wrapped up in this, but I would venture this example you've pulled out of your hat is an extremely small percentage of who would be revealed. To be honest the biggest problem I have is that we've become so normalized to this kind of shit that hardly anything happens from the revelations. Just look at the Panama papers for example, besides a journalist or two getting assassinated because of it, nobody was held to account. Combine that with things like Epstein's lack of prosecution and later assassination and what I see is the overton window moving to normalize corruption as a required pragmatic approach to power such that most people will openly admit the rule of law simply doesn't apply to the elite. This, therefor, is hacktivism of the finest kind imho. The people standing up for themselves when even their own governments won't. Any potential fallout relating to innoce"},{"id":18847464,"author":"olivermarks","month":"2019-01","story":"$1T is leaving Britain because of Brexit","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on City of London and offshore secrecy jurisdictions.","quote":"","text":"There is a massive 'fear campaign' drip dripping dire news in the UK every day if the people don't reconsider leaving a federated europe. The EU is well past its sell-buy date, won't reform and globalization has taken full advantage of its undemocratic structure for offshore commercial gain. The divide and rule aspects of the UK EU referendum results are being exploited to the full. Like the last US election there is no good result for the English people - remaining or leaving are both going to have an ugly side. The City of London rules the financial world and enables incredible sums to float offshore via trusts and opaque corporate setups. What we are seeing is a further divide between the haves and have nots. [VIDEO] < spiders web documentary 'At the demise of empire, City of London financial interests created a web of secrecy jurisdictions that captured wealth from across the globe and hid it in a web of offshore islands. Today, up to half of global offshore wealth is hidden in British jurisdictions and Britain and its dependencies are the largest global players in the world of international finance.' This makes the consumer banks and FS firms shuffling fiat currencies around europe a tiny drop in the ocean"}],"r":[["Zk11vI-7czE",1],["CpCrkO1x-Qo",1],["8455KEDitpU",1],["PTUY16CkS-k",1],["gmn7tjSNyAA",1],["T71ibcZAX3I",1],["RxabLA7UQ9k",1],["Kxryv2XrnqM",1],["LQRAfJyEsko",1],["4Z2uzEM0ugY",1]]},"XheAMrS8Q1c":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as the accessible 20-minute entry point to Levin's bioelectricity research whenever regeneration or cell signaling comes up.","q":{"x":"If you have 20 minutes to watch this ted talk interview I highly recommend it.","i":35075557,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35075557,"author":"LegitShady","month":"2023-03","story":"E-Bandages lightly zap and heal wounds","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Cells communicate via voltage, shaping behavior beyond DNA","quote":"If you have 20 minutes to watch this ted talk interview I highly recommend it.","text":"your cells are sensitive to charge in general - cells attempt to maintain certain voltage differentials across their membranes via ion channels in the cell membrane, can communicate voltage information with each other via gap junctions, etc. That electrical communication does control how cells behave. If you have 20 minutes to watch this ted talk interview I highly recommend it. My mind was blown. Your DNA determines the hardware, but there's a lot of non chemical communication that goes on between cells that determine how they behave. [VIDEO] Once you realize that cells can effectively communicate with each other via voltage, things like whats described about electrical bandages become less surprising."},{"id":30668007,"author":"comboy","month":"2022-03","story":"Electric fields, not individual neurons, may hold information in memory: study","stance":"strong_rec","why":"New and revolutionary work on cellular electrical communication","quote":"This is new though (and revolutionary)","text":"Electric potential across other cells and also single-cell organisms is well known and not new. This is new though (and revolutionary): [VIDEO] Regarding consciousness, I don't get why people who submit to consciousness being everywhere in the universe have problem with simply calling it the universe. It seems to make discussions much more productive."},{"id":45611430,"author":"simmanian","month":"2025-10","story":"Electricity can heal wounds three times as fast (2023)","stance":"rec","why":"20 minute TED talk on Levin's bioelectricity work","quote":"20 min ted talk","text":"The article and the comments remind me of Michael Levin's work on bioelectricity. 20 min ted talk - [VIDEO] 3 hr lex fridman episode - [LINK]"},{"id":38032603,"author":"farkanoid","month":"2023-10","story":"Bioelectricity could regrow limbs and organs","stance":"rec","why":"Regenerative experiments with flatworms by the same biologist","quote":"There's a fascinating (in my opinion) TED talk about this","text":"There's a fascinating (in my opinion) TED talk about this featuring the same Biologist, with examples of regenerative experiments performed using Flatworms: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35880886,"author":"holoduke","month":"2023-05","story":"Language models can explain neurons in language models","stance":"rec","why":"Neurons as hardware, electrical activity as software","quote":"There is a good video","text":"Maybe the neurons are the hardware layer. The software is represented by the electronic activity. There is a good video [VIDEO] about this topic."},{"id":34296542,"author":"laughingman2","month":"2023-01","story":"Will We Know Alien Life When We See It?","stance":"rec","why":"Levin group research on emergence of multicellular agency","quote":"One interesting research group tackling this question","text":"One interesting research group tackling this question is ex-computer engineer, currently biologist Prof. Micahel Levine's group. They built xenobots (biological robots) from frog cells which are not evolutionarily programmed to do tasks which they accomplished. Their research in part deals with emergence of agency at multi-cellular level. And they want to get at the fundamental signatures of what makes some collection of agents into a new single agent at an higher level. Checkout, [VIDEO] And a new research paper (inhavent had the time to read yet) on this question of how coherent higher order agents emerge from underlying sub-agents. [LINK]"},{"id":29622118,"author":"l2p","month":"2021-12","story":"Amputated fingertips sometimes grow back (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of Levin's bioelectric research on how bodies form missing parts","quote":"Here is an overview video","text":"Regarding how the body \"knows\" how to form missing pieces, might be worth checking out Michael Levin's research regarding bio-electricity. Most interesting finding to me is that once modified, the animal bodies continue to produce different parts as if there is some electrical \"memory\" in the cells. Here is an overview video: [VIDEO] , though there are much longer lectures and papers which I would recommend if you are interested."},{"id":48644854,"author":"waerhert","month":"2026-06","story":"Show HN: Neural Particle Automata","stance":"reference","why":"Levin's cells structuring and repairing morphology via electrical communication","quote":"","text":"On the outside it looks very similar to what Michael Levin found on electrical communication between living cells. There too, the organism's cells were able to structure and repair their larger-scale morphology: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42596724,"author":"pdl41064","month":"2025-01","story":"Zebrafish protein unlocks dormant genes for heart repair","stance":"reference","why":"Levin lab's electrical blueprints tie to regeneration findings","quote":"","text":"Hmga1 reduces repressive histone marks (H3K27me3), effectively unlocking genes typically silenced after embryonic development. This process seems to tap into an evolutionarily preserved \"blueprint\" encoded in the DNA. By reactivating these dormant pathways, differentiated cells like cardiomyocytes regain some of the plasticity and regenerative capacity characteristic of embryonic cells. If we combine this with the work coming out of Dr. Michael Levin's Lab at Tufts ( [VIDEO] the existence of electrical blueprints that orchestrate life, we should see, as we do in this study that the regeneration observed was precisely tailored to the injury, avoiding pathological outcomes like cardiomegaly or uncoordinated growth. This preserved blueprint exists in all differentiated cells."},{"id":33578493,"author":"ahMath8","month":"2022-11","story":"The world has reached 8B people but soon we'll hit a decline we'll never reverse","stance":"reference","why":"Levin's regenerative biology work at Tufts","quote":"","text":"We’d be better utilized as CPU and memory storage. Maybe generate brain structure, biology state with (re)generative biology like Michael Levin is up to at Tufts[0]. There’s tons of alternative research paths going untouched that leverage information theory to hack “reality” directly rather than hack on synthetic computers and software within an enclosed time-space vacuum constrained by known hardware limitations. Metaverses running on synthetic machines seems way more prosaic than a metaverse in my own head I might be able to switch on off with a pill. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32360914,"author":"ArtofIndirect","month":"2022-08","story":"What ever happened to the transhumanists?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Extropians hack reality versus a purpose built man made machine: [VIDEO] Ideas like designer drugs and therapy to embed experience and knowledge is way more interesting than VR apps"},{"id":30666992,"author":"grishka","month":"2022-03","story":"Electric fields, not individual neurons, may hold information in memory: study","stance":"reference","why":"Electrical signaling coordinates organism development","quote":"","text":"\"Something electrical floating around your cells\" isn't just the mind either, it's also how the organism coordinates its development: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30124731,"author":"flobosg","month":"2022-01","story":"Frogs Regrow Missing Limbs in Lab Study","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among further Michael Levin material","quote":"","text":"More about Michael Levin: What Bodies Think About: Bioelectric Computation Outside the Nervous System – [LINK] Why don’t (today’s) Robots get Cancer? – [LINK] The electrical blueprints that orchestrate life – [VIDEO] And a tweet about the article – [LINK]"}],"r":[["YJrOuBkYCMQ",1],["obS6TUVSZds",1],["AFVDZeg4RVY",1],["G4hL5Om4IJ4",1],["8ORLN_KwAgs",1],["31D94QOo2gY",1],["94ed4v_T6YM",1],["p83QUZ1-P10",1]]},"j3mhkYbznBk":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as a masterclass in explaining physics clearly without condescension, accessible without formal training.","q":{"x":"Feynman is so good at explaining these concepts that you don't even need any formal training to understand them.","i":6074246,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":18220081,"author":"jacobedawson","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman explains complex things like talking to a friend, never patronizing.","quote":"Never patronizing, always amusing & informative","text":"The Feynman Lectures (on Physics) are incredible - Feynman had such an amazing mind, one of the greatest the world has known, yet could explain complex things like he was talking to a friend in the living room over drinks. Never patronizing, always amusing & informative: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14564632,"author":"mabbo","month":"2017-06","story":"If you can’t explain something in simple terms, you don’t understand it","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman lectures worth rewatching with full attention; masterpieces of education.","quote":"I highly recommend that if you've never seen them, you take some time and watch them","text":"Edit: apparently, I have been misinformed for a very long time. These are still excellent lectures to watch though! The Feynman Lectures are now on Youtube[0], and I like to watch them (all of them) every few years. I highly recommend that if you've never seen them, you take some time and watch them- really watch them. Close the other windows, turn your phone to do not disturb, and really watch these masterpieces of education. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6074246,"author":"sillysaurus","month":"2013-07","story":"Neutrino shape-shift points to new physics","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman explains physics so clearly no formal training is needed.","quote":"Feynman is so good at explaining these concepts that you don't even need any formal training to understand them.","text":"Physics is just mindblowing. When I think deeply about the fact that things that are moving will keep on moving forever unless a force acts on them, and the fact that no one knows why it's true (and that the question may not even have any meaning), and that my body is nothing more than a very advanced method of propagating genetics, then... well, it's just a lot of fun to think about! The fact that there are mysteries that no life will ever have answers to is quite humbling. If you find physics interesting, then you'll love these Feynman lectures. Feynman is so good at explaining these concepts that you don't even need any formal training to understand them. Lecture 1: The Law of Gravitation [VIDEO] Lecture 2: The Relation of Mathematics to Physics [LINK] Lecture 3: The Great Conservation Principles [LINK] Lecture 4: Symmetry in Physical Law [LINK] Lecture 5: The Distinction of Past and Future [LINK] Lecture 6: Probability and Uncertainty [LINK]"},{"id":5512601,"author":"alxbrun","month":"2013-04","story":"Ask HN: What's the best technical talk you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Incredible talk in both content and form.","quote":"Incredible talk for both the content and the form !","text":"Richard Feynman - The Character of Physical Laws [VIDEO] Incredible talk for both the content and the form ! There is so much we could learn from him."},{"id":17927359,"author":"shawn","month":"2018-09","story":"Feynman's Lost Lecture (ft. 3Blue1Brown) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman's Character of Physical Law series is quite wonderful.","quote":"Feynman's podcast series The Character of Physical Law is quite wonderful","text":"If you haven't seen it yet, Feynman's podcast series The Character of Physical Law is quite wonderful: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17472595,"author":"TripleH","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: What talks or videos do you suggest to watch on a long flight?","stance":"rec","why":"Classic Feynman lectures, enlightening even on subjects you think you know.","quote":"I really enjoyed Richard Feynman lectures[0], especially on subjects I thought I knew well.","text":"It's kind of a classic, but I really enjoyed Richard Feynman lectures[0], especially on subjects I thought I knew well. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":31325122,"author":"throwaway2214","month":"2022-05","story":"No Dislikes has officially ruined YouTube for me","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It saddens me what will be lost if youtube continues on this route, 99% of the content is garbage, and the 1% good content is hardly monetizable, and there is 0.001% that content creators monetize with some form of sponsorship, but not so much from google's pov You can see Dirac himself speak ( [LINK] ), Feynman ( [VIDEO] ), Jung( [LINK] ), Minsky, McCarthy, Ellul, Ram Dass, Jiddu Krishnamurti, Terence McKenna and Alan Watts and many many more people who greatly influenced our understanding and way of life Youtube is quickly turning worse, just as google, with the flood of click baity content, and not only them, look at cnn.com or foxnews.com you will see every single article is with click bait title. I dont think the issue is in the dislike button, I think they have to up their game to help you navigate the sea of garbage to find some islands of good content. The reddit rule of 90/9/1% is no longer true (90% of community members are lurkers who read or observe, but don't contribute. 9% of community members edit or respond to content but don't create content of their own) now it seems we are more to 20/30/50% (my intuition), and the tooling and understanding used to decide what content to create and game the system has improved a lot. Like in video games, the way people play videogames now is fundamentally different than 5 years ago, now you are expected to minmax your character, items, gameplay etc because there are so many tools and guides out there to help you, but the sam"},{"id":5772647,"author":"Arjuna","month":"2013-05","story":"42","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman notes 42-digit ratio of gravitational to electrical force.","quote":"The ratio of the gravitational attraction to the electrical repulsion is given by a number with 42 digits","text":"I always thought that this was interesting... I am sure that many of you are familiar with Dr. Feynman's 7-part lecture series entitled The Character of Physical Law , which were part of the \"Messenger Lectures\" [1], given at Cornell University in 1964. In the first lecture, Law of Gravitation - An Example of Physical Law , Feynman states the following: \"Question: what is the ratio of the gravitational force to the electrical force? That is illustrated on the next slide. The ratio of the gravitational attraction to the electrical repulsion is given by a number with 42 digits, and goes off here: all this is written very carefully out, so that's 42 digits. Now, therein lies a very deep mystery: where could such a tremendous number come from? That means if you ever had a theory from which both of these things are to come, how could they come in such disproportion? From what equation has a solution which has for one, two kinds of forces, an attraction and a repulsion with that fantastic ratio? People have looked for such a large ratio in other places. They're looking for a large number. They hope, for example, that there's another large number. And if you want a large number why not take the diameter of the universe to the diameter of a proton. Amazingly enough, it also is a number with 42 digits.\" [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5158641,"author":"charonn0","month":"2013-02","story":"Is Scientific Truth Always Beautiful?","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Feynman quote about nature's patterns.","quote":"Nature uses only the longest threads to weave her patterns","text":"\"Beauty\", in science and mathematics, is not a quality of the object regarded so much as it is a pattern-recognition response in the one regarding it. Patterns which are predictable are naturally pleasing to us because our pattern-recognition abilities evolved to harness the patterns of nature for prediction of outcomes. Predicting and harnessing outcomes based on patterns may be the power that humans posses in strength over the other animals. It should not be the case that exploration of an outcome is excluded on the basis that it is not perceived as beautiful. However, that there are patterns among the laws of nature was one of the fundamental discoveries of our species and a guiding principle of modern science. Richard Feynman said[1]: \"Nature uses only the longest threads to weave her patterns, so that each small piece of her fabric reveals the organization of the entire tapestry.\" [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4QXZ2RzN_Oo",1],["4jEz03Z8azc",1],["zFhYJRqz_xk",1],["2tdiKTSdE9Y",1],["HaEPXoXVf2k",1],["eg3_kUaYFJA",1],["kd0xTfdt6qw",1]]},"G_eYTctGZw8":{"wy":"It is the go-to answer to whether Prolog is used for real-world work, praised as a survey of its uses, advantages and drawbacks.","q":{"x":"gives an excellent high-level survey of the uses, advantages, and drawbacks of Prolog","i":9248500,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":21389618,"author":"samuell","month":"2019-10","story":"Swish: SWI Prolog Notebook","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Showcases the real power of Prolog.","quote":"For an awesome video showcasing a bit of the real power of Prolog, you need to check","text":"For an awesome video showcasing a bit of the real power of Prolog, you need to check (if you haven't already) this Strangeloop talk: \"Production Prolog\" by Michael Hendricks [VIDEO]"},{"id":9248500,"author":"abathologist","month":"2015-03","story":"Learn Prolog Now","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent high-level survey of Prolog uses, advantages, and drawbacks.","quote":"gives an excellent high-level survey of the uses, advantages, and drawbacks of Prolog","text":"You might be interested in these SO answers regarding \"Real world Prolog Usage\": [LINK] The answers are replete with claims about good use-cases for Prolog. Michael Hendricks' talk at last year's Strange Loop on \"Production Prolog\" gives an excellent high-level survey of the uses, advantages, and drawbacks of Prolog: [VIDEO] BTW, I have played a tiny bit with Flora2 myself. It seemed really neat. If you're interested in the intersection of OO and Prolog, you may also want to look at Logtalk[0]. FWIW, if I understand your meaning correctly, it is very easy to \"pass rules as parameters to other rules\" in vanilla Prolog. It facilitates all sorts of higher-order approaches and is very good for meta-programming. [0]: [LINK]"},{"id":34436644,"author":"samuell","month":"2023-01","story":"Tar.pl – A tar creator and extractor in approx. 100 lines of Prolog","stance":"rec","why":"Talk demonstrates similar practical Prolog use.","quote":"Check out \"Production Prolog\" by Michael Hendricks on YouTube","text":"Check out \"Production Prolog\" by Michael Hendricks on YouTube, which demonstrates the same thing, at a talk at StrangeLoop: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22863796,"author":"simplify","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: How to rediscover the joy of programming?","stance":"rec","why":"Motivation for using Prolog, in a list of resources.","quote":"[2] is good motivation","text":"I recommend starting with [0] to get a feel for what's possible. [1] is great when you have time to dive deeper. [2] is good motivation, and [3] will show you how to build a practical roles & permissions system. Keep in mind that you don't have to build your app in 100% prolog to use prolog. There are likely implementations in your favorite language. For example, Rust[a] JavaScript[b], and Ruby[c]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [a] [LINK] [b] [LINK] [c] [LINK]"},{"id":14051446,"author":"StreamBright","month":"2017-04","story":"The Power of Prolog","stance":"rec","why":"Helpful video for understanding Prolog.","quote":"I have found these videos helpful for understanding Prolog","text":"Same here, I don't think this is a particularly great way of starting with Prolog. I have found these videos helpful for understanding Prolog: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9247757,"author":"profquail","month":"2015-03","story":"Learn Prolog Now","stance":"rec","why":"Talk gives ideas about jobs that use or could use Prolog.","quote":"that might give ideas about jobs that use (or could use) Prolog","text":"I found a page from 1994(!) asking the same question: [LINK] Given that Prolog isn't super-widely used (compared to say, C++), there are going to be a limited number of developers with Prolog expertise. That means companies who use it in their tech stack are generally going to have to hire developers who fit the other qualifications for the role then put them through some internal Prolog training. The listings for such positions probably won't have Prolog as a \"required\" skill but a \"desired\" skill. One other point -- just because there aren't any/many jobs where Prolog is required doesn't mean there aren't many cases where Prolog could be useful. Prolog hasn't been widely taught for a couple of decades, so a significant proportion of developers today won't recognize a problem that Prolog would be a good solution for as a problem that Prolog would be a good solution for. There's a Strange Loop 2014 talk on YouTube, \"Production Prolog\", that might give ideas about jobs that use (or could use) Prolog: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8869045,"author":"MonkeyIsNull","month":"2015-01","story":"Prolog Programming Language (1997)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Prolog being used for real-world problems.","quote":"it's worthwhile to use for real world problems","text":"Not only is it worthwhile to learn, it's worthwhile to use for real world problems: [VIDEO] From his blog: [LINK]"},{"id":22548552,"author":"simplify","month":"2020-03","story":"The FAQ of comp.lang.prolog is maintained as a Prolog source file","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of pausing, changing code, and resuming execution at 12:17.","quote":"Michael Hendrick's \"Production Prolog\" talk gives a demo of this (at 12:17)","text":"Prolog also has some really impressive debugging tools. It allows you to pause execution, change the code, and then resume execution from an earlier point in time . Michael Hendrick's \"Production Prolog\" talk gives a demo of this (at 12:17) [VIDEO]"},{"id":18754905,"author":"worldsayshi","month":"2018-12","story":"Learn Prolog Now","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among relevant resources on real-world Prolog use.","quote":"Found some relevant threads and links","text":"Yes. I'd love to know more about real world use cases. Edit: Found some relevant threads and links: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18769478,"author":"hjek","month":"2018-12","story":"Racklog: Prolog Style Logic Programming [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence Prolog is used in practice.","quote":"pointing to instances where it is being used in practice[0]","text":"> Hmm I'm fine with prolog, I love it, but I'm not sure how it can be used in practice. Well, that rings similar to what is said about Lisp sometimes, and I think it can easily be refuted by pointing to instances where it is being used in practice[0]. There are some problems that are fairly easy to solve in Prolog, but would be very difficult to express in SQL[1]. And recursive relations, which are at the core of Prolog, only made their way into SQLite 4 years ago[2]. My comment was specifically about those Prolog/Datalog implementations in Racket . Other Prolog implementations might be dealing sensibly with incremental updates (but I don't know). > Having seen companies where SQL was used very extensively for analytics, data storage etc I can't see how prolog wouldn't help. You can't see how Prolog wouldn't help, as in Prolog can only be an improvement over SQL? [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK]"}],"r":[["4fFDFbi3toc",1]]},"sG9UMMq2dz4":{"wy":"HN commenters pass it around as an eye-opening first-hand account of patent trolling and the Texas courts that favor it.","q":{"x":"This video from Austin Meyer (creator of X-Plane) is pretty eye-opening","i":13973653,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13973653,"author":"clamprecht","month":"2017-03","story":"Supreme Court Considers Why Patent Trolls Favor Texas Courts","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Two Texas judges whose lawyer sons handle patent cases before them","quote":"This video from Austin Meyer (creator of X-Plane) is pretty eye-opening","text":"This video from Austin Meyer (creator of X-Plane) is pretty eye-opening: [VIDEO] Apparently there are TWO judges in the district, BOTH of whose own sons are lawyers in the same district. And BOTH sons handle a lot of the patent litigation, in front of their own fathers, the judges. Pretty incredible. Watch the video starting at about 5 minutes to see it."},{"id":12349719,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2016-08","story":"Trollala – Fight patent trolls by banding together confidentially","stance":"strong_rec","why":"X-Plane developer recounts being sued by patent trolls","quote":"Just watched The Patent Scam video by Austin Meyer (developer of X-Plane flight sim who was sued by patent trolls).","text":"This is great. Would like to see more of these kinds of efforts. Just watched The Patent Scam video by Austin Meyer (developer of X-Plane flight sim who was sued by patent trolls). [VIDEO] That kind of stuff makes my blood boil."},{"id":27785581,"author":"zubspace","month":"2021-07","story":"Do patents kill innovation? The US patent office is asking","stance":"rec","why":"Developer sued over license server explains troll shell-company tactics","quote":"Just watched this video [1] by someone who got sued because his \"android app uses a license server\".","text":"Just watched this video [1] by someone who got sued because his \"android app uses a license server\". Those patent troll shell companies sue one victim after another and forward the incoming money to other companies. So there's no use in going after them directly. The victim can negotiate better terms if he signs a non-disclosure agreement. The patent trolls are protected by politicians, judges and lawyers. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19239523,"author":"rixrax","month":"2019-02","story":"Apple to Close Stores in Eastern District of Texas to Fight Patent Trolls","stance":"rec","why":"Explains peculiar US patent law and the eastern Texas district","quote":"Above two YouTube videos I’ve been passing around","text":"URL: [VIDEO] (Austin Myers) URL: [LINK] (John Olivier) Above two YouTube videos I’ve been passing around in regard to sometimes peculiar nature of patents law in US and in particular that of eastern district of TX."},{"id":14394322,"author":"dangerlibrary","month":"2017-05","story":"U.S. top court tightens patent suit rules in blow to ‘patent trolls’","stance":"rec","why":"Short intro to how overbroad patents hurt small tech companies","quote":"","text":"For anyone looking for an ~20 minute intro to why this is a big deal and the impact it has on small tech companies: [VIDEO] tl;dw: There are some over-broad patents on usage of very common technologies ( using wifi or selling on the google play store are two egregious examples) that are being used as a bludgeon against small and medium sized firms that can't afford to defend themselves. It costs about $3 million to defend against such a suit, and the Eastern district is known for refusing to force plaintiffs to pay defendant's attorney's fees even if the suit is ultimately dropped."},{"id":12620362,"author":"roflchoppa","month":"2016-10","story":"Apple loses patent retrial to the litigious VirnetX, ordered to pay $300M","stance":"rec","why":"Judge and son lawyer enabling patent troll litigation in Texas","quote":"It would be interesting to see emails dumped from these guys","text":"So there is a judge out there, and his son is a practicing lawyer who reps. PT and PT victims. Basically legal racketeering. It would be interesting to see emails dumped from these guys, and how much corruption is taking place. [VIDEO] its like 5 min in."},{"id":32826307,"author":"deepsun","month":"2022-09","story":"X-Plane 12 Early Access","stance":"reference","why":"X-Plane's developer describes fighting patent trolls","quote":"he even made video how he fought against patent trolls","text":"I don't know much about both, but xplane is developed by private developer, he even made video how he fought against patent trolls: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18103570,"author":"Cypher","month":"2018-09","story":"China Claims More Patents Than Any Country. Most Are Worthless","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"All patents should be worthless, the fact that trolls can come after anyone for uploading an app to google play store for fees and win because of some obscure patent is ridiculous. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Uo08uS904Rg",1],["k1BneeJTDcU",1],["prXuyMCgbTc",1],["rFejpH_tAHM",1],["TPpoJGYlW54",1],["h3gwyHNo7MI",1],["TVwKjGbz60k",1],["BpsMkLaEiOY",1],["PIPjmmmh_os",1],["XJ9ckqCMiKk",1],["c12cYAUTXXs",1],["3bxcc3SM_KA",1],["RfdZTZQvuCo",1],["3IryIOyPfTE",1]]},"U4FNBMZsqrY":{"wy":"HN raves about it as one of the best conference talks, showing a master's creative process and sound design.","q":{"x":"One of my favorite conference talks ever","i":35786739,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35786739,"author":"epiccoleman","month":"2023-05","story":"Show HN: I've built a spectrogram analyzer web app","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Master of his craft explains experimental sound creation for Doom.","quote":"One of my favorite conference talks ever","text":"Mick Gordon did some fun hidden spectrogram imagery in the Doom 2016 soundtrack. He talks about that and plenty of other cool stuff in his talk at the 2017 GDC conference. One of my favorite conference talks ever, he did so much cool experimentation to get the sounds he used on the soundtrack, and watching his talk is one of those moments where you really get to see a master of his craft let loose and explain his process. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33533124,"author":"agentwiggles","month":"2022-11","story":"My full statement regarding DOOM Eternal","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mick Gordon explains his soundtrack creation process creatively.","quote":"His talk at GDC where he goes into some of the process of creating the soundtrack is fantastic","text":"This is really disappointing to read. Mick's soundtrack was a huge part of making DOOM 2016 and Eternal such great experiences. His talk at GDC where he goes into some of the process of creating the soundtrack is fantastic, so much fun to see the creativity he brought to the game's music. [VIDEO] The next DOOM will be a little less DOOMy without him."},{"id":15640039,"author":"pault","month":"2017-11","story":"Audio Design Deep Dive: Scoring Wolfenstein II with the Baschet sonic sculptures","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mick Gordon on instrument design for the Doom 2016 soundtrack.","quote":"did a great presentation on the instrument design for the Doom 2016 soundtrack","text":"Here's one of the instruments from the Baschet workshop: [LINK] Mick Gordon–who scored the previous Wolfenstein game–did a great presentation on the instrument design for the Doom 2016 soundtrack, which is in my opinion one of the best (the best?) game soundtracks of all time: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25152199,"author":"acomjean","month":"2020-11","story":"Dusk is a 90s style shooter from the mind of a teenager (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long talk on how Doom's music came about, with fun stories.","quote":"Its more than about just the sound, its about process and change.","text":"Doom came out right when I left univeristy. Loved that game. I like the new Dooms too. I think they're fun. Way more \"doom like\" than anything else I've played since. The \"New Doom\" music/sound producer gave a pretty good GDC one hour talk about the music came about. Some fun stories there ( the sound easter egg story was fun). Its more than about just the sound, its about process and change. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23080806,"author":"acomjean","month":"2020-05","story":"Learn sound theory with an acclaimed synth company","stance":"rec","why":"Doom sound designer shows how synths and effects built the sounds.","quote":"the GDC talk by the sound designer of doom","text":"I had a dx7 in college. Its an FM synth, an from the little diagrams on the top, it was fascinating. I could make my own sounds, but I never could get the time to get programming (beyond detuning the sound and doubling it to make a \"fatter\" sound, the dx7 had a gray matter mod chip). Lamentably in the 90s information about this stuff was harder to come by, plus the 2 line led screen to input things wasn't ideal. You can see the top of the keyboard images here: [LINK] But the internet abounds with sound making tools! ableton has a synth basics online class/demo kind of thing: [LINK] visual programing sound machines in pd [LINK] Also the GDC talk by the sound designer of doom (the new one). He's using sine wave, and a bunch of effects boxes (some in parallel) to generate all those sounds. There is also a sound morph tool they used to combine sounds with a chainsaw sound... The talk starts a couple minutes in after a doom sound trailer thing: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["yY1FSsUV-8c",2],["WU0gvPcc3jQ",1],["KFNxJVTJleE",1],["2KSahAoOLdU",1],["rDPj5zI66LA",1]]},"S89BHnaxULo":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a good, condensed way to experience Neuromancer when short on time.","q":{"x":"The BBC did a great adaptation of the book to a radio play, complete with professional voice acting, sound effects, even music","i":17376627,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":45256266,"author":"sien","month":"2025-09","story":"William Gibson Reads Neuromancer (2004)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent BBC audio drama of Neuromancer","quote":"There is a BBC Audio drama of Neuromancer that is 1:56 long. It is excellent.","text":"There is a BBC Audio drama of Neuromancer that is 1:56 long. It is excellent. [VIDEO] I haven't listened to the audio book of Neuromancer but I re-read it a few weeks back. The audio play I still go back to once in a while as well."},{"id":40845433,"author":"keiferski","month":"2024-07","story":"Neuromancer was released 40 years ago today","stance":"rec","why":"Two-hour condensed BBC rendition for those short on time","quote":"this BBC audio rendition is only about 2 hours long and is pretty good.","text":"Although I highly recommend reading the full novel, if you don't have the time, this BBC audio rendition is only about 2 hours long and is pretty good. Note that it's a highly condensed version of the story and skips many of the best parts. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34023244,"author":"sien","month":"2022-12","story":"Apple TV+ to adapt William Gibson’s cyberpunk novel Neuromancer","stance":"rec","why":"Pretty good BBC audio adaptation of Neuromancer","quote":"The BBC did a pretty good audio adaptation of Neuromancer.","text":"The BBC did a pretty good audio adaptation of Neuromancer. You can listen to it here: [VIDEO] Neuromancer would be hard to do well as a film. But as fairly short TV series it would be excellent. There is huge potential as well because you could do, say Neuromancer in 4 hours and then do the rest of the trilogy. The best screen adaptation of a book I know of, Brideshead Revisited from 1981, took the book almost word for word and made a fairly long TV series. Neuromancer and the rest of the trilogy could be done the same way."},{"id":28387144,"author":"sien","month":"2021-09","story":"William Gibson is a literary genius","stance":"rec","why":"Very good BBC radio play adaptation of Neuromancer","quote":"There is a very good radio play adaption of Neuromancer from the BBC","text":"There is a very good radio play adaption of Neuromancer from the BBC : [VIDEO] The Neuromancer film was last meant to be directed by Tim Miller who directed Deadpool and was to be released in 2021. So somehow the mess with the film rights may be resolved somehow. The Neuromancer trilogy would make a great TV series. A few episodes for each book."},{"id":17376627,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2018-06","story":"Revisting Neuromancer After Three Decades","stance":"rec","why":"Abridged BBC radio play with voice acting, sound effects, music","quote":"The BBC did a great adaptation of the book to a radio play, complete with professional voice acting, sound effects, even music","text":"The BBC did a great adaptation of the book to a radio play, complete with professional voice acting, sound effects, even music: [1] Unfortunately, it's only an abridged version. On the positive side, it's a quick listen, and well worth it if you're a fan (or just want to get a taste). [1] - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hSrGfElyfVE",1],["GoW8Tf7hTGA",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["Q3uvzcY2Xug",1],["3wfNl2L0Gf8",1],["uRGljemfwUE",1],["qbIk7-JPB2c",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["doaQC-S8de8",1],["kYfNvmF0Bqw",1],["UWEjxkkB8Xs",1]]},"klaJqofCsu4":{"wy":"HN points to it as proof that efficient, long-lived LED bulbs can be built and as an example of well-designed lamps.","q":{"x":"Check out the dubai lamp to see a lamp designed to be efficient and last","i":29300395,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":36230389,"author":"shagie","month":"2023-06","story":"Amount of work that once bought an hour of light now buys 51 years of it (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Different Dubai lamp design with a look at the electronics","quote":"They have a different design and it gets into the electronics.","text":"There are a lot of cheap, poorly designed LED bulbs that put heat stress on critical components. Technology Connections : A troubling trend in lighting? - [LINK] (It goes into how a number of LED bulb failures are because the lamp wasn't able to dissipate heat from the electronic components. It also shows how some higher quality LED bulbs are made so that they dissipate heat better - they don't make as much heat as an incandescent bulb, but there is still some and that is the cause of a lot of failures.) The lamps you're not allowed to have. Exploring the Dubai lamps - [VIDEO] is also interesting. They have a different design and it gets into the electronics."},{"id":35013048,"author":"adolph","month":"2023-03","story":"LEDs from Dubai: The Royal Lights You Can’t Buy","stance":"rec","why":"Source material for the article, from a favorite channel","quote":"cited source material comes from the always awesome Big Clive YouTube Channel.","text":"2021, cited source material comes from the always awesome Big Clive YouTube Channel. bigclivedotcom: The lamps you're not allowed to have. Exploring the Dubai lamps: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29300395,"author":"asddubs","month":"2021-11","story":"Upgrading the soldered-on RAM of a Dell XPS13 7390 laptop","stance":"rec","why":"A lamp designed to be efficient and long-lived","quote":"Check out the dubai lamp to see a lamp designed to be efficient and last","text":"although it certainly is true these days, that the average LED based bulb is pushing those LEDs hard, which incidentally also makes them less efficient, rather than just using more of them at the same wattage. Check out the dubai lamp to see a lamp designed to be efficient and last: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26142468,"author":"foxhop","month":"2021-02","story":"Ercot nearly at capacity for Texas power grid","stance":"rec","why":"Example of well-designed long-lasting LED lamps","quote":"For actual good LED lamps (not the crap they push in USA) check out","text":"I love my incandescent bulbs. It is a threat to remove them. The heat they produce is amazing in the winter. I use a 100 watt bulb to keep me warm at my desk. Heat bodies not buildings. I've been practicing this since a child and I'm 36 now. To heat yourself, check out: [LINK] For actual good LED lamps (not the crap they push in USA) check out: [VIDEO] TLDR most LED lamp are running over spec which cause them to fail and become a commodity rather than infra. I had a rear projection DLP LED TV which ran its red LED over spec and it burned out at least 3 times as fast as the green and blue. [LINK] The heat is not always just a byproduct, sometimes you need or want the heat. Compare this 100 watt bulb to a tiny space heater and you will see the difference in your electricity bill. Buy a \"kill a watt\" meter to start testing you appliances rather assuming incandescent is evil."},{"id":25822217,"author":"MrGilbert","month":"2021-01","story":"The Dubai Lamp: The “World’s Most Efficient” LED Light Bulb","stance":"rec","why":"Teardown of Dubai lamps with more filament strips running cooler","quote":"I can really recommend the channel","text":"I think this one here appeared because it's been featured on hackaday recently[1], which got featured there because Youtube channel \"bigclivedotcom\" made a video about them[2], which blew through the roof because of some Youtube-algorithm-doodads? Compared to his recent other videos, this really stands out in terms of views. (Although I can really recommend the channel). Anyways, the main thing is that these lamps use more filament-strips than usual led bulb, thus allowing them to run cooler and last longer. Unfortunately, they are only available in Dubai (afaik). [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47942270,"author":"bb88","month":"2026-04","story":"United Wizards of the Coast","stance":"reference","why":"Why the Dubai lamp is not sold elsewhere","quote":"Big Clive did a good video on the Dubai Lamp a few years back","text":"No argument from me, other than: All those things can be also be said about corporations. You brought up lamplighters and the electric lightbulb. It's worth noting that corporations had a cartel that prevented lightbulbs from running longer than a few years. And to some extent that still happens. Big Clive did a good video on the Dubai Lamp a few years back, and why you can't buy them anywhere but in Dubai. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40943717,"author":"chongli","month":"2024-07","story":"The Great Lightbulb Conspiracy (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Efficient long-lasting LEDs exist in Dubai under strict regulation","quote":"In Dubai they actually have efficient and long-lasting LED lights","text":"Yes, it’s because they drive the heck out of the LEDs to get their maximum rated light output. This produces excessive heat, shortening the lifespan of everything inside the case, including the very cheap electronics they use to rectify mains AC. In Dubai they actually have efficient and long-lasting LED lights [1]. These exist in order to satisfy strict government regulation there. They’re not sold anywhere else because they’re more expensive to make (more LEDs per bulb running at lower output). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40706250,"author":"fkyoureadthedoc","month":"2024-06","story":"How to get stuff repaired when the manufacturer don't wanna: take 'em to court","stance":"reference","why":"Philips and Dubai's ruler collaboration on a long-life LED bulb","quote":"I remember seeing a relevant video about Dubai and some bulb company working together to create a longer life led bulb.","text":"I remember seeing a relevant video about Dubai and some bulb company working together to create a longer life led bulb. Ok here it is -- The lamps you're not allowed to have. Exploring the Dubai lamps [VIDEO] > These fascinating lamps are a result of a collaboration between Philips Lighting and Sheikh Mohammad Bin Rashid Al Maktoum - the ruler of Dubai. They are designed to be the most efficient available, matching high lumen output with very long life. Once you see the construction and circuitry you'll realise this isn't just marketing spin. Now I want to actually look into and see if they're available elsewhere, because my LED bulbs really do seem to fail pretty frequently..."},{"id":35375402,"author":"chongli","month":"2023-03","story":"There's something off about LED bulbs","stance":"reference","why":"Long-lived LED bulbs need more LEDs driven at lower current","quote":"It is possible (and not very difficult) to design LED bulbs that will practically outlive their owners","text":"The big problem for me is, as a consumer, how do I know that brand X is producing quality bulbs that'll last a long time? The issue is that LED bulbs aren’t simple devices like incandescent bulbs. LED bulbs have an electronic power supply inside which drives the LEDs at a constant current. Power supply design is a major subfield of electronics engineering and there are all kinds of tradeoffs you can make to optimize for different goals. Consumer electronics almost always optimizes for cost, to the detriment of all else. It is possible (and not very difficult) to design LED bulbs that will practically outlive their owners [1]. The problem is that it requires putting more LEDs in the bulb and driving them at lower current. This makes the bulb cost more and the only benefit is longer life. For a manufacturer, there are nothing but downsides to this approach. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30497221,"author":"adhesive_wombat","month":"2022-02","story":"Someone should probably start a bright home lighting company (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Non-overdriven LED bulbs sold only in Dubai","quote":"There are in fact LED bulbs that aren't overdriven to a deliberate and premature thermal death, but you can only buy them in Dubai","text":"There are in fact LED bulbs that aren't overdriven to a deliberate and premature thermal death, but you can only buy them in Dubai: [VIDEO] Lightbulbs have a long and very successful history of artificially driving lightbulb lifetimes down though a cartel system: [LINK] If you're building your own LED fixtures, you can probably underdrive the LEDs and run them at a radically cooler and slightly more efficient operating point, at a higher upfront cost: you need more LEDs for the same output."},{"id":30139089,"author":"stordoff","month":"2022-01","story":"Controlling RGB LEDs with only powerlines: Anatomy of a Christmas light string","stance":"reference","why":"Dubai lamps designed for higher efficiency and longer life","quote":"They're designed to have a higher energy efficiency and a longer operational life","text":"Big Clive does this sort of thing on his YouTube channel fairly often. A few examples: * Adjusting a current regulator to drop a 10W lamp to ~2W - [LINK] * Adjusting a capacitive dropper-based lamp from 5W to 0.5W - [LINK] * Making a \"Dooby\" (inspired by the Philips Dubai lamps[1]) lamp - using a series capacitor to make various lamps run at 0.1-2W - [LINK] [1] They're designed to have a higher energy efficiency and a longer operational life - [VIDEO]"},{"id":29070675,"author":"CelestialTeapot","month":"2021-11","story":"Livermore's centennial light, the world's longest-burning light bulb","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The same game is still played today with LED bulbs","text":"The same game is still played today with LED bulbs (whether there's a cartel behind it or not, I do not pretend to know). See: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28088625,"author":"JKCalhoun","month":"2021-08","story":"The smart home is flailing as a concept","stance":"reference","why":"Big Clive teardowns of LED bulb drivers","quote":"Big Clive loves to tear down LED bulbs to examine the driver boards.","text":"> the fault is not in the actual light emitting element but (of course) in the driver.... Being over-driven. Big Clive loves to tear down LED bulbs to examine the driver boards. Often shows you ways to drop the current to sustainable levels and get back your 20-year lifespan LED bulbs (caveat, at lower power levels). [LINK] More specifically, example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27779010,"author":"guitarbill","month":"2021-07","story":"What Happened to the 100k Hour LED Bulbs?","stance":"reference","why":"Big Clive videos on the underrun Dubai lamp","quote":"the LEDs are underrun, which increases the efficiency, and should increase the lifetime","text":"Interesting to see no mention of the \"Dubai lamp\". There was a previous discussion on HN [0], and of course the Big Clive videos about it [1]. In short, the LEDs are underrun, which increases the efficiency, and should increase the lifetime (which as the article mentions is mainly related to heat). Edit: They're called \"Dubai lamps\", because they're only supposed to be sold there (even though Philips makes them). [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26791810,"author":"kenmacd","month":"2021-04","story":"How Government Wrecked the Gas Can","stance":"reference","why":"How properly built bulbs are made for Dubai","quote":"To see how they should be built watch Clive on how bulbs are made for Dubai","text":"> Light bulbs don’t illuminate. I find this one especially funny as it's pretty much the lack of regulation that made the 'old' bulbs burn out much too quickly [1]. And before someone says \"but my LED bulbs keep burning out\", also the free-market/lack of regulation. To see how they should be built watch Clive on how bulbs are made for Dubai[2]. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4PaWFYm0kEw",1],["CbIZU8cQWXc",1],["yiw6_JakZFc",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["LxgMdjyw8uw",1],["rvskMHn0sqQ",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["XVmsfL5LG90",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["wO2lWmgEK1Y",1],["hyCIpKAIFyo",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["fD-R35DSSZY",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["bLk1cjZ4ll0",1]]},"MtzCLd93SyU":{"wy":"HN people cite it when discussing perceived randomness, luck and player experience in games, and say more designers should heed it.","q":{"x":"More people need to listen to what Sid Meier has said on this.","i":18696763,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18696763,"author":"mlindner","month":"2018-12","story":"Sandspiel – A falling sand game built in Rust and WebGL","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sid Meier on games and win states.","quote":"More people need to listen to what Sid Meier has said on this.","text":"Many games don't have win states or shouldn't have win states. More people need to listen to what Sid Meier has said on this. That HN article was written by someone who did not know what they were talking about. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20348900,"author":"ringshall","month":"2019-07","story":"Ask HN: Why are modern music players so bad at playing music in random order?","stance":"rec","why":"Good description of true randomness problem from a game perspective.","quote":"This talk by Sid Meier is a good description of the problem of true randomness, from a game perspective","text":"This talk by Sid Meier is a good description of the problem of true randomness, from a game perspective: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19408327,"author":"codetrotter","month":"2019-03","story":"How Designers Engineer Luck Into Video Games (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Sid Meier on engineering luck in games.","quote":"You might enjoy these videos as well","text":"> The anecdote about Sid Meier hard-coding a guaranteed \"win on the third attempt\" for a battle with a 1/3 victory probability was very interesting. You might enjoy these videos as well: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12813688,"author":"timo614","month":"2016-10","story":"What Sid Meier’s Video Game Empire Got Right and Wrong About ‘Civilization’","stance":"rec","why":"Sid Meier on negative random events and player experience.","quote":"Sid Meier gave a talk at the GDC","text":"Agreed; the point of the game is to make you feel like a God. Sid Meier gave a talk at the GDC: [VIDEO] In it he explained how Civilization tested random negative events that could shape your world. He mentioned how this had a negative effect on the player experience and many times people would just grab a past save to avoid them IIRC."}],"r":[["Ggz_gd--UO0",1],["36myc8wQhLo",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1],["AJ-auWfJTts",1],["UTqZNujQOlA",1],["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["lnnsDi7Sxq0",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1]]},"0g9BcZvQbXU":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a practical illustration of what Org mode and literate programming can do for documentation.","q":{"x":"I think does a pretty good job of illustrating the power of Org.","i":28363030,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":43767570,"author":"spudlyo","month":"2025-04","story":"Atuin Desktop: Runbooks That Run","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on using org-babel for documentation.","quote":"I think org-babel is really well suited for this task, and can make some really great documentation.","text":"Thanks for the shout-out! I think org-babel is really well suited for this task, and can make some really great documentation. You can check out the video[0] from the talk and a git repo[1] with a more advanced demonstration. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":28363030,"author":"spudlyo","month":"2021-08","story":"The open calendar, task and note space is a mess","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrates Org's literate programming for consistent technical docs.","quote":"I think does a pretty good job of illustrating the power of Org.","text":"Org mode does a lot of different things, but one use case I find interesting is to be able to mix prose and program output in a technical document in a way that keeps things consistent when things change. I made a video[0] a year or so ago that explores Org's literate programming features that I think does a pretty good job of illustrating the power of Org. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26431380,"author":"spudlyo","month":"2021-03","story":"From Vim to Emacs in fourteen days (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows org-babel keeping technical documents consistent via dynamic code blocks.","quote":"all about writing technical documents that are easy to keep consistent when things change","text":"I made a video[0] showing off the power of org-babel, which is the part of org that lets you embed dynamic code blocks in your document. In the video I write a little essay[1] on how git stores data that has a lot of dynamic content that is managed by org. It's a bit like reproducible research or literate programming, but for me it's all about writing technical documents that are easy to keep consistent when things change. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":23632470,"author":"d0mine","month":"2020-06","story":"Text-Only Websites","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of writing technical docs with Emacs and Org mode.","quote":"Here's a nice demo on how to write tech docs using Emacs and Org mode","text":"Org mode supports inline Latex that can be exported to html. Here's a nice demo on how to write tech docs using Emacs and Org mode [VIDEO]"},{"id":22883036,"author":"rwnspace","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: Resources to grok Emacs and use it well?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is what I've done the past couple weeks and it's going pretty well: Start with plain Emacs. Print/write out the popular cheatsheets. Just make sure you know how to exit, access the documentation, and move around the windows/frames/buffers. Get Melpa & install some packages. Play with the config, adding keybindings, figure out hooks and so on. Copy some elisp functions from dotfiles on Github. Spam the documentation command on everything. Get frustrated with how much tinkering you need to do to get the crazy setups you've seen on blogposts. Stay motivated by consuming more content about custom setups. Discover the magic of magit and parinfer and dired. Look up some of the org-mode/PIM heroes on github, usernames: novoid, alphapapa, karlicoss Install Doom. Tinker with it a bit. Take notes of your learning in an org file. Tinker some more. Go back to plain emacs for a bit. Then try spacemacs. Go straight back to Doom because it's faster and you now understand enough about emacs to be scared of spacemacs' layers. Below are some links to help you along the way. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["gd5uJ7Nlvvo",1],["JWD1Fpdd4Pc",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["rzQEIRRJ2T0",1],["PriwCi6SzLo",1]]},"HPqGaIMVuLs":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the original explainer for a clever dithering technique and the go-to video in dithering threads.","q":{"x":"a 4-days old video related to dithering which is really worth watching.","i":42845150,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":47444554,"author":"m12k","month":"2026-03","story":"Return of the Obra Dinn: spherical mapped dithering for a 1bpp first-person game","stance":"rec","why":"Goes deeper into making dither surface stable.","quote":"someone diving even deeper into how to make the dither surface stable","text":"If you find this interesting, you might also be interested in this video of someone diving even deeper into how to make the dither surface stable: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45752908,"author":"m12k","month":"2025-10","story":"Dithering – Part 1","stance":"rec","why":"Explains a surface-stable technique inspired by Obra Dinn.","quote":"a couple videos about a technique that was inspired by the Obra Dinn's dither, but making it surface stable","text":"And here's a couple videos about a technique that was inspired by the Obra Dinn's dither, but making it surface stable: [VIDEO] (explanation) [LINK] (demo)"},{"id":43675819,"author":"qwertox","month":"2025-04","story":"Writing my own dithering algorithm in Racket","stance":"rec","why":"Related explainer on surface-stable dithering, with a follow-up.","quote":"Somewhat related and worth watching: Surface-stable fractal dithering explained","text":"Somewhat related and worth watching: Surface-stable fractal dithering explained [VIDEO] There's a follow-up video to that one."},{"id":42810537,"author":"abetusk","month":"2025-01","story":"Surface-Stable Fractal Dithering","stance":"rec","why":"Covers experiments and shortcomings behind stable 3D dithering.","quote":"The video linked [0] to in the repo talks about experiments and shortcomings","text":"To create a dithering effect for 3D scenes. Doing a straight 2D dither on each frame will result in noisy artifacts and won't be \"stable\". Doing an \"ordered\" dithering produces a \"porch screen\" artifact effect, where there's a stable pattern in screen space that can be seen. Trying to naively map dithering patterns as textures on 3D objects again results in unstable patterns as the dithering is dependent on the distance of the screen to the object and produces noise. This is a proposed solution that maps a dithering pattern as a texture that doesn't have the \"porch screen\" effect and is stable. The video linked [0] to in the repo talks about experiments and shortcomings from the above I just listed. This work is in response to some challenges the developer of the \"Return of the Obra Dinn\" game experienced when trying to create a dithering effect in game [1] [2]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":42845150,"author":"qwertox","month":"2025-01","story":"Bilinear down/upsampling, aligning pixel grids, and that infamous GPU half pixel (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Worthwhile video on surface-stable dithering by an indie developer.","quote":"a 4-days old video related to dithering which is really worth watching.","text":"On a similar note, yesterday I stumbled across a 4-days old video related to dithering which is really worth watching. \" Surface-Stable Fractal Dithering \" [0] by \"Rune Skovbo Johansen, indie game developer and procedural generation enthusiast\" [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43675841,"author":"jansan","month":"2025-04","story":"Writing my own dithering algorithm in Racket","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an extremely ambitious dithering algorithm.","quote":"unless you are trying to implement something completely insane like Surface-Stable Fractal Dithering","text":"I think implementing a dithering algorithm is one of the most satisfying projects, because it is fun, small(ish) and you know when you are done. Of course, unless you are trying to implement something completely insane like Surface-Stable Fractal Dithering [VIDEO]"},{"id":42918291,"author":"biercarsten","month":"2025-02","story":"The Art of Dithering and Retro Shading for the Web (2024)","stance":"reference","why":"Creator's video on the technique inspired by Obra Dinn.","quote":"I recently whatched his youtube video on this","text":"Ah, thats from runevision. I recently whatched his youtube video on this ( [VIDEO] ) and \"Return of the Obra Dinn\" was also the first thing I thought of."},{"id":43002654,"author":"runevision","month":"2025-02","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Creator's own original explainer video of the technique.","quote":"The original explainer video is here","text":"I'd like to expand with some more details and developments since the post I saw here a few weeks ago about my Surface-Stable Fractal Dithering technique. (That post (not by me) was here: [LINK] ) I've since made it work also in color (RGB dithering and CMYK halftone) and demonstrated what it looks like in true 1-bit (or 3-bit for color) at low resolutions as well. See the linked demonstration video for a variety of styles/aesthetics it can be used for: [LINK] The original explainer video is here: [VIDEO] And the repository is here: [LINK] Lots of people both here and elsewhere has speculated about various alterations or different use cases, etc. I'd like to point to this FAQ that have a lot of common topics covered. If you have questions, suggestions or similar, it would be a good idea to check that out first (after the explainer video): [LINK] Furthermore, lots of people have been inspired to make their own implementations, or related effects inspired by Surface Stable Fractal Dithering. I keeping a list of the ones I know about here: [LINK] For example, Aras Pranckevičius got a simplified version of the effect to work on Playdate. Since the Playdate does not have a GPU, the effect (along with 3D rasterization in general) is implemented fully CPU-based here. [LINK] Ryan / InfernalWAVE is working on an implementation for Godot: [LINK] arColm made shaders for MineCraft: [LINK] I've been surprised and happy that the effect has gotten as much interest as it has, and I'm hoping w"}],"r":[["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["AJdEqssNZ-U",1],["7J7X7aZvMXQ",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["HVv_IQKlafQ",1],["hB6eY73sLV0",1],["ZYqG31V4qtA",1]]},"JtKAScORevQ":{"wy":"The standard link in ranked-choice voting threads to show IRV does not eliminate the spoiler effect.","q":{"x":"here's a video explaining the strategic voting issue","i":27600045,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27598286,"author":"godelski","month":"2021-06","story":"What is ranked-choice voting and why is New York using it?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the strategic voting issue in ranked-choice voting.","quote":"","text":"RCV doesn't solve what is claimed in the article. It is interesting since countries that they give as examples are clear evidence of this. (Yes, I'm going to advocate cardinal systems like Approval, Range/Score, and STAR) > More moderate candidates. RCV doesn't encourage this at all. Australia is a clear example of this. In the house Liberal and Labor (the two major parties) control 85% of seats. The next major party (National) is part of a coalition with the Liberals and this sums to 95%. Things are only slightly better in the senate. Look at any country that uses IRC/RCV and you'll see a similar pattern. The system didn't encourage more parties and more moderates, Australia still has divided politics like America. As to the more parties, I have an alternative explanation. They use a parliament, which uses proportionate representation. Don't buy it? Go look at countries with parliaments and don't have IRV and you'll find similar distributions. We also need to recognize that in America our parties are closer to coalitions in other countries out of the necessity. I'm not sure how anyone can look at someone like AOC or Sanders and think they are the same \"party\" (by other country norms) as Biden or Pelosi (similarly on the Republican side, though Trump caused a consolidation). > ranked-choice voting can drastically reduce the possibility of spoilers. Yes, but no. It reduces spoilers when a candidate is not going to win anyways (e.g. Jo Jorgensen spoiling Trump). But this does n"},{"id":27600045,"author":"elihu","month":"2021-06","story":"What is ranked-choice voting and why is New York using it?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the strategic voting issue in IRV","quote":"here's a video explaining the strategic voting issue","text":"I agree with what /u/zestyping says in the other comment, so I won't repeat it. But here's a video explaining the strategic voting issue: [VIDEO] Also, wikipedia has an example of a non-monotonic result here: [LINK]"},{"id":21327118,"author":"user234683","month":"2019-10","story":"Ranked-choice voting is on the ballot in New York City","stance":"rec","why":"Easy-to-follow explanation of spoiler effects in plurality voting and IRV.","quote":"I always recommend this pair of videos when discussing STAR voting vs other alternative systems.","text":"I always recommend this pair of videos when discussing STAR voting vs other alternative systems. The first video is an easy-to-follow explanation of how spoiler effects can happen in both plurality voting and IRV [1]. In the second [2], the guy who created Star voting applies Ka-Ping Yee's methods to Star voting and animates it side-by-side with alternative systems to show its performance. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":12920317,"author":"aninhumer","month":"2016-11","story":"Show HN: Mest – Place to talk with those you disagree with","stance":"rec","why":"Shorter video dealing specifically with IRV problems.","quote":"This is a shorter video from the same source as the other reply that deals specifically with IRV","text":"This is a shorter video from the same source as the other reply that deals specifically with IRV: [VIDEO] >Unless I misunderstand IRV, there is no \"vote stealing\" due to your vote automatically dropping off to your next-preferred candidate. The problem is that they drop to your next preferred candidate who has not been eliminated . If you're thinking about the problems that occur with plurality voting, it's easy to imagine your second choice will always be a popular \"safe\" choice, but if third parties get stronger (which is surely the point?) those safe choices can end up being eliminated before your second choice can help them."},{"id":8588145,"author":"ClayShentrup","month":"2014-11","story":"Police Use Department Wish List When Deciding Which Assets to Seize","stance":"rec","why":"Explains favorite betrayal in instant-runoff voting.","quote":"Regarding favorite betrayal, you might enjoy this video.","text":"This issue has been solved by math PhD's and political scientists who've studied the matter for decades. www.electology.org/approval-voting-vs-irv IRV can elect X even if Y was preferred to X by an arbitrarily huge majority and got more first-place votes than X. www.electology.org/core-support Regarding favorite betrayal, you might enjoy this video. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34283740,"author":"ClayShentrup","month":"2023-01","story":"Alameda County admits tallying error in ranked choice voting, flips one result","stance":"reference","why":"Example of optimal ranked ballot strategy being complex.","quote":"an optimal ranked ballot can be quite complex","text":"strategy affects all deterministic voting methods, and an optimal ranked ballot can be quite complex. see an example from andy jennings, who did his math phd thesis on voting methods and co-founded the center for election science (with myself and some others). [VIDEO] optimal approval voting strategy is simply to approve everyone you prefer to your expected satisfaction. to an approximation it's, \"vote for who you normally would, plus everyone you like better.\" so it's rarely any more taxing than now, and often easier. hundreds of thousands of voters have now used approval voting in fargo and st louis, and we've heard no reports of difficulty. approval voting is also simpler in essentially every way we can objectively measure. most of the points here apply to approval voting even more so. [LINK]"},{"id":33126298,"author":"ClayShentrup","month":"2022-10","story":"Show HN: RankedVote – SurveyMonkey but focused on ranked-choice voting","stance":"reference","why":"Supports argument that ranked methods push strategic first-place votes for major parties.","quote":"","text":"this is obviously false. simply consider that green party supporters usually vote democrat. with score or approval voting, they'd ALSO want to vote for the green. whereas with ranked voting methods, they'd still strategically rank the democrat in 1st place, crushing any hope for the greens. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33126314,"author":"ClayShentrup","month":"2022-10","story":"Show HN: RankedVote – SurveyMonkey but focused on ranked-choice voting","stance":"reference","why":"Supports argument that scoring resists strategy better than ranking.","quote":"","text":"no. scoring is more resistant to strategy. consider a green party supporter who normally votes democrat. with score voting or approval voting, he also votes green (of course). with ranked voting, he still ranks the democrat strategically in 1st place to avoid getting the republican, and that makes it impossible for third parties to grow. ranking is too vulnerable to strategy. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24563236,"author":"godelski","month":"2020-09","story":"Ranked-choice voting will be used in Maine’s presidential election, court rules","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that IRV does not prevent spoilers.","quote":"One of the claims is that IRV prevents spoilers, which is objectively false","text":"For a more interesting experiment Fargo had an election with Approval Voting[0]. Unfortunately RCV (IRV) doesn't solve many of the problems that it claims to do. One of the claims is that IRV prevents spoilers, which is objectively false[1][2]. We're pretty interested in the Favorite Betrayal Criterion to prevent this[3]. We also see that IRV has a fairly poor voter satisfaction efficiency (VSE)[4][5]. It really feels like people just watched the CGP Grey videos on election science and dug no further. Even Arrow himself said that cardinal voting (Approval, score, STAR) is probably the likely answer. They also tend to be much simpler as they only are single rounds and resistant to people accidentally \"ranking\" two candidates equally (which you might actually favor two candidates equally and these methods let you express that). [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":24563904,"author":"ruytlm","month":"2020-09","story":"Ranked-choice voting will be used in Maine’s presidential election, court rules","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how ranking someone higher can make them lose.","quote":"Found this[0] talking about it","text":"Perhaps I am suffering a failure of imagination, but in what circumstance does \"ranking someone too high .. cause them to lose\"? Edit: Found this[0] talking about it; what remains unclear to me is whether this should be seen as \"this hurts my candidate\" over \"this result better reflects the electorate\". [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23709710,"author":"ClayShentrup","month":"2020-07","story":"Oppose the Earn IT Act","stance":"reference","why":"Shows strategic burying of third parties under IRV.","quote":"","text":"> it exacerbates the issue of voting being highly tactical. No. Approval voting has been extensively studied by game theory experts like NYU political science professor Steven Brams, and found to be one of the most resistant to tactical voting. Computer simulations show it performing exceptionally well even with highly strategic voters. [LINK] [LINK] There's even a mathematical theorem (proof) that, given plausible strategic behavior by voters, approval voting always elects a beats-all \"Condorcet winner\" when one exists. This is mild (some would say GOOD) reaction to strategy. [LINK] > Checking approve on someone I believe to be more popular than my favorite candidate lowers the chance my favorite candidate wins. Yes, this is beneficial not harmful. The alternative is that the voting method must ignore all preference data about the relative support of X vs. Y as long as the voter prefers X to Y. That causes big problems. [LINK] It is precisely why ranked voting methods like Instant Runoff Voting are so vulnerable to tactics. If I prefer the Green, my general best strategy is to tactically rank the Democrat in 1st place, thus \"burying\" the Green and making the mere appearance of un-electability a self-fulfilling prophecy. [VIDEO] [LINK] The major moral of voting theory is to AVOID RANKED VOTING METHODS, favoring RATED voting methods like score voting, STAR voting, and approval voting. Here's a presentation I gave in 2015 to the Colorado League of Women Voters explaining more. "},{"id":21326848,"author":"ajennings","month":"2019-10","story":"Ranked-choice voting is on the ballot in New York City","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates a Nader-Gore-Bush scenario where IRV fails.","quote":"IRV doesn't handle this situation well.","text":"If Nader takes enough votes from Gore, then Gore will have the fewest first place votes and get eliminated first. Then Nader has to go up against Bush and it all comes down to who the Gore voters put as their second choice. It may only take a small percentage of them putting Bush second for Bush to win. [VIDEO] Was this election spoiled? By Nader or by Gore? You can argue the semantics of what \"spoiler\" means, but to me it's clear that IRV doesn't handle this situation well. It is safe to vote for a third-party only if they can't win. When three-way elections get close, you have to be more careful."},{"id":20399571,"author":"ClayShentrup","month":"2019-07","story":"Ross Perot Has Died","stance":"reference","why":"Math PhD explains IRV does not eliminate the lesser-evil problem.","quote":"See this explained by a math PhD.","text":"> with ranked choice voting the stigma of voting third party is removed That is a myth. Instant Runoff Voting does not eliminate the lesser evil (spoiler) problem. See this explained by a math PhD. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20442516,"author":"ClayShentrup","month":"2019-07","story":"Ross Perot Has Died","stance":"reference","why":"Game theory of IRV explained by a voting methods PhD.","quote":"See the game theory explained by Andy Jennings","text":"This is false. See the game theory explained by Andy Jennings, who did his math PhD thesis on voting methods and co-founded the Center for Election Science. [VIDEO] Here's a more technical explanation by Warren Smith, a Princeton math PhD whose work was the centerpiece of the book \"Gaming the Vote\". [LINK] Finally, see real world data from a century of IRV in Australia. [LINK]"},{"id":19574292,"author":"ClayShentrup","month":"2019-04","story":"Approval Voting versus Instant Runoff Voting","stance":"reference","why":"Visual explanation of the later-no-harm criterion by a voting theory PhD.","quote":"See this explained visually in this video by Andy Jennings","text":"The Later-no-harm criterion is basically a nonsensical idea invented to make IRV look better than it is. See a Princeton math PhD dissect it here. [LINK] tl;dr for non-experts (I co-founded the Center for Election Science and have studied voting methods since 2006). The idea behind LNH is that if I vote for the Green in 1st place, it's \"safe\" to then rank the Democrat 2nd, because that can't cause the Democrat to defeat the Green. See this explained visually in this video by Andy Jennings, another CES co-founder who did his math PhD thesis on voting methods. [VIDEO] The Later-no-harm criterion is highly misleading, because \"safe\" here means safe for the CANDIDATE. But tactical voting is about what's good for the VOTER, not the candidate. With IRV, the Green voter's best strategy is to actually rank the Democrat in 1st place. So the whole notion that he can safely rank the Democrat #2 after he's ranked the Green #1 starts with a false premise. If he's rational, he WILL NOT rank the Green #1. It's the same reasoning that Democratic primary voters have about far left candidates. Even if you win the nomination and advance to the general, you're so far left that you'll probably lose against the Republican, by not capturing enough of the center of the electorate. Approval Voting actually makes it safe to ALWAYS vote your favorite. And, in general, to vote for everyone you prefer to your favorite of the frontrunners."},{"id":12876421,"author":"anderskaseorg","month":"2016-11","story":"Voting Paradoxes [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Counter to the claim that elimination voting avoids the spoiler effect.","quote":"","text":"This is a great introduction to voting theory, but there are several notable omissions. The video doesn’t tell the whole story about what it presents as “ranked voting”, usually known as Condorcet voting. There are systems that always yield the Condorcet winner when one exists, and do a good job of resolving the unavoidable but rare preference cycles as consistently as possible. The most standard one is the Schulze method, which is used by the Debian, Ubuntu, and Gentoo projects, among many other organizations. [LINK] The video asserts that what it presents as “elimination voting”, usually known as instant-runoff voting, doesn’t suffer from the third-party spoiler effect. But that’s only true as long as the third party never gains enough support to have a real chance of winning. [VIDEO] (I wonder: can you think of a pair of powerful organizations who might want to ensure that third parties never gain enough support to have a real chance of winning? Hmmm.) Finally, the video doesn’t talk about approval voting or score voting, which use a different ballot type to which Arrow’s impossibility theorem does not apply. Some game theorists argue that these systems actually do a better job of finding the ideal Condorcet winner than Condorcet systems do, in the presence of strategic voting. [LINK]"},{"id":12887332,"author":"aninhumer","month":"2016-11","story":"How Does the Electoral College Work and Is It Fair","stance":"reference","why":"Explains favourite betrayal in instant runoff.","quote":"The problem with Instant Runoff is called \"Favourite Betrayal","text":"The problem with Instant Runoff is called \"Favourite Betrayal\", where placing a party second can lead to them being eliminated before your vote for them can be counted. [VIDEO] >a candidate can still be elected despite not being approved or preferred by a majority. Only if there are no candidates preferred by a majority, in which case no voting system can produce a result people are happy with. The only way a ranked system could avoid this is by forcing people to choose between options they hate, and calling that a majority, which doesn't seem much better than letting someone with 30% approval win because it was the most. In fact, there's no reason you couldn't add a majority victory criterion to approval voting."},{"id":12951809,"author":"ClayShentrup","month":"2016-11","story":"Maine Ranked Choice Voting Initiative Approved","stance":"reference","why":"Shows IRV does not eliminate the spoiler effect.","quote":"No, IRV does not eliminate the spoiler effect.","text":"No, IRV does not eliminate the spoiler effect. [VIDEO] Score Voting and Approval Voting are simpler and better. [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":11511802,"author":"aninhumer","month":"2016-04","story":"When Lobbying was Illegal","stance":"reference","why":"Explains favourite betrayal as a serious IRV problem.","quote":"This is called Favourite Betrayal, and it's a serious problem with IRV","text":"Yes, I understand how IRV works, and indeed I used to believe it was flawless. >There is no way that placing your preferred candidate first can ever result in your vote not being given in the order you choose. No, but it can end up harming your second choice, by eliminating them early, so your vote for them doesn't matter. This is called Favourite Betrayal, and it's a serious problem with IRV: [VIDEO] And as a quick worked example, if you have 4 people voting A>B>C, 4 people voting C>B>A and 3 people voting B>A>C, then A wins. If one person voting C>B>A changes their vote to B>C>A, then B wins. So by putting C first, they were harming their second choice B."}],"r":[["-4FXLQoLDBA",2],["S0dSm_ClcSw",1],["6OPsH8PK7xM",1],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["5uiroWBkdFY",1],["HZGCoVF3YvM",1],["EK32jo7i5LQ",1],["HLNhPMQnWu4",1],["qOTYgcdNrXE",1],["LS3GQk9ETRU",1],["jA1C9VFqJKo",1],["thOifuHs6eY",1],["ljOoGyCso8s",1]]},"KSWqx8goqSY":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a great, hilarious talk about legal threats and cease and desist letters.","q":{"x":"Watch his talk on the matter, it is really great","i":9570380,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9570380,"author":"__z","month":"2015-05","story":"I Want Your AOL CDs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on being sued and receiving cease and desist letters.","quote":"Watch his talk on the matter, it is really great","text":"This guy has gotten cease and desist letters from catheter companies as well as been sued for two billion dollars. Watch his talk on the matter, it is really great - [VIDEO]"},{"id":8838864,"author":"hobs","month":"2015-01","story":"The MS-DOS Showcase","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jason Scott's talk with details about textfiles.","quote":"He gave a great talk at DEFCON 17","text":"If you don't know him already, textfiles has one of my favorite people on the internet (Jason Scott) running a lot of things, and hoarding interesting material for our future. As far as I can tell the dude just loves MS-DOS games and hence we have this great project. (I imagine there are a lot of contributors, but he is clearly pushing it forward) He gave a great talk at DEFCON 17 called \"That one time I was sued for two billion dollars.\" in which he gives a few more details about textfiles if you are interested. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46815944,"author":"Rendello","month":"2026-01","story":"Anna's Archive is sued for $13T","stance":"rec","why":"Hilarious talk about being sued for a huge sum.","quote":"gave a hilarious DEFCON talk called","text":"For a less-serious but similar situation, Jason Scott (textfiles.com, Internet Archive) gave a hilarious DEFCON talk called \"That Awesome Time I Was Sued For Two Billion Dollars\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":22995348,"author":"reitzensteinm","month":"2020-04","story":"textfiles.com","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The owner of the site gave a talk at DEFCON 17, \"That Awesome Time I Was Sued For Two Billion Dollars\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":28386987,"author":"mhh__","month":"2021-09","story":"UK national extradited to US for film/TV piracy","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's no better than [VIDEO] (That Awesome Time I Was Sued For Two Billion Dollars) level argument, just with better lawyer-speak. The US is sheltering a diplomat(spy?) who negligently ran over and killed a British civilian as we speak, but these guys are the real danger."}],"r":[["UCgoxQCf5Jg",1],["IroPQ150F6c",1],["RSTNhvDGbYI",1],["wlvKAT7SZIQ",1],["vHWiDx_l4V0",1],["fhOHn9TClXY",1],["ARKbfWk4Xyw",1],["DpXy041BIlA",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["VVUeqxXwCA0",1],["rQhzEnWCgHA",1],["tt2AYafET68",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1],["3kEpZWGgJks",1],["mdKst8zeh-U",1]]},"WlT3UhC7NwQ":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an excellent speech from Watsi's founder, especially on keeping a nonprofit aligned with the people it serves.","q":{"x":"Startup School presentation (which is excellent, BTW)","i":12862249,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12862249,"author":"nostrademons","month":"2016-11","story":"Signs that a startup is focused on stuff that doesn’t matter","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Watsi founder on nonprofit alignment with the people served","quote":"Startup School presentation (which is excellent, BTW)","text":"Curious why you make the distinction between for-profit and non-profit organizations. All organizations face the challenge of their vision of \"making the world a better place\" not necessarily lining up with the vision of the folks for whom they want to make it better. For-profit companies at least have the signal of \"someone is willing to pay me for this\" as a concrete guide to whether they align with anyone. Watsi's founder talked a fair bit about this in his Startup School presentation (which is excellent, BTW): [VIDEO]"},{"id":7307199,"author":"akcreek","month":"2014-02","story":"An Early Retirement","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great Startup School speech by Watsi's founder","quote":"the great speech Chase gave at Startup School","text":"I appreciate what Watsi does, but I'm mostly buying a shirt because of the great speech Chase gave at Startup School [1]. It was of the most value to me at that event. Thanks. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8287316,"author":"djcary","month":"2014-09","story":"Stories from our first two years","stance":"rec","why":"Watsi founder's Startup School talk","quote":"Chase, Watsi's founder, spoke at start up school last year.","text":"Chase, Watsi's founder, spoke at start up school last year. Donate first, then watch his talk and then donate again. Make sure you look for the tip option on the checkout page if you want to help cover Watsi's operational costs. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["3t6L-FlfeaI",1]]},"YCjNT9qGjh4":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an excellent inside look at Valve's history, its technology and the games industry of the time.","q":{"x":"the excellent YouTube documentary Valve released for Half-Life 2's 20th Anniversary","i":42234950,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":45641513,"author":"sky2224","month":"2025-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of your favorite documentaries?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Perspective on the games industry and Valve's technology at the time.","quote":"I really enjoyed the Half Life 2: 20th Anniversary documentary.","text":"I really enjoyed the Half Life 2: 20th Anniversary documentary. It gave a pretty neat perspective of what the games' industry was like at the time, and also made me really appreciate the technology that Valve was building. A lot of really innovative stuff came out of the creation of that game. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42234950,"author":"bejd","month":"2024-11","story":"A summer intern once saved Valve from a near-fatal lawsuit","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Valve's anniversary documentary, source of the article's story.","quote":"the excellent YouTube documentary Valve released for Half-Life 2's 20th Anniversary","text":"Mentioned in the article, but the source is the excellent YouTube documentary Valve released for Half-Life 2's 20th Anniversary [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43071039,"author":"scrlk","month":"2025-02","story":"Half-Life 2 and Dishonored art lead Viktor Antonov has died","stance":"rec","why":"Includes interview with Viktor Antonov.","quote":"For those who haven't seen it yet, he was interviewed in the HL2 20th Anniversary Documentary","text":"RIP. For those who haven't seen it yet, he was interviewed in the HL2 20th Anniversary Documentary: [VIDEO] (intro and chapter 1 at 13:40)"},{"id":42583979,"author":"mrbn100ful","month":"2025-01","story":"Why Half-Life 3 speculation is reaching a fever pitch again","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary discusses dropping episodic content approach.","quote":"You can watch the 20th anniversary doc, they speak about it","text":"They drop the episodic content idea, it will be a full game You can watch the 20th anniversary doc, they speak about it : [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LtFyP0qy9XU",1],["5ESJH1NLMLs",1],["OyBNmecVtdU",1],["9syvZr-9xwk",1]]},"qhbuKbxJsk8":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the clearest explanation of the patterns behind modular multiplication diagrams and times table art.","q":{"x":"This YouTube video explains the algo behind what you're seeing.","i":23475691,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":35550605,"author":"photochemsyn","month":"2023-04","story":"Number System Invented by Inuit Schoolchildren Makes Its Silicon Valley Debut","stance":"rec","why":"Times tables in any number base.","quote":"see Mathologer on times tables in any number base","text":"Grasping the concept of number regardless of base system is an interesting exercise (linked to modular arithmetic), see Mathologer on times tables in any number base: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23475691,"author":"csbuja","month":"2020-06","story":"Times Tables Can Make Beautiful Art","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the algorithm behind the art.","quote":"This YouTube video explains the algo behind what you're seeing.","text":"Check out some of the png files to view the art! This YouTube video explains the algo behind what you're seeing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20560323,"author":"espeed","month":"2019-07","story":"Powers of Two in Lexicographic Order","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the patterns behind modular multiplication sequences.","quote":"Then watch this","text":"How does that work? ... Explanations invited. Hold the 1 fixed. Notice in both sequences the remaining numbers are 3 interleaved ordered sets, in multiples of 2. Then watch this (just happened to post it two hours ago too)... \"Times Tables, Mandelbrot and the Heart of Mathematics\" [video] [VIDEO] [LINK] NB: And once again we find cycloids hiding underneath it all."},{"id":15397383,"author":"jacobolus","month":"2017-10","story":"Beauty in Mathematics: Modular Multiplication Tables","stance":"rec","why":"Gives insight into moire-like patterns in related diagrams.","quote":"Check out for some insight based on a related type of diagram","text":"There’s not really a reasonable continuous analog, IMO (unless you just want to see a single solid blob, like in your picture, but that hides all of the interesting part of the structure). But there are moiré-like patterns. Check out [VIDEO] for some insight based on a related type of diagram (related to a single row at a time from the OP’s diagrams)."}],"r":[["cidchWg74Y4",1],["KTzGBJPuJwM",1],["iph500cPK28",1],["Og6cTlwBTrk",1],["86gyW0vUmVs",1],["n8m7lFQ3njk",1],["Ygty9HxhFK4",1],["-k3mVnRlQLU",1],["FJJTYQYB1JQ",1],["s86-Z-CbaHA",1],["gM95HHI4gLk",1],["L3LMbpZIKhQ",1],["aHx-XUA6f9g",1],["3Y2weLDiUWw",1],["7ZGCPap7GkY",1],["YyIQKBzIuBY",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["XeJSXfXep4M",1],["kjBOesZCoqc",1],["xnez6tloNSQ",1]]},"rDPj5zI66LA":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend Arthur's Fermi Paradox videos as a thorough, clear treatment of the subject.","q":{"x":"He describes the Fermi Paradox and related concepts 100x better than anyone else.","i":26505637,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26505637,"author":"mchusma","month":"2021-03","story":"Grabby Aliens","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains Fermi paradox better than anyone else.","quote":"He describes the Fermi Paradox and related concepts 100x better than anyone else.","text":"If interested in this kind of thing, I always recommend Issac Arthur. He describes the Fermi Paradox and related concepts 100x better than anyone else. Some relevant ones: The Fermi Paradox Compendium [VIDEO] Fermi Paradox: The Dyson Dilemma v2.0 [LINK] I believe he would say that there is no really good explanation to why we don't see other intelligent civilizations except that we are effectively the first (at least in our galaxy), and intelligent space-faring life is exceedingly rare. This is a similar conclusion to Robin Hanson here, but I think I think Issac Arthur does a better job of deeply explaining it. I think both would agree though."},{"id":14396947,"author":"pault","month":"2017-05","story":"Fermi's Paradox [audio]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hours of content exploring Fermi paradox solutions.","quote":"I really enjoy Isaac Arthur's videos on the subject","text":"I really enjoy Isaac Arthur's videos on the subject (he has a playlist with several hours of content exploring different possible solutions). The rest of his channel is great too; he posts a new 30-60 minute video every Thursday. [VIDEO] Edit: The entire playlist: [LINK]"},{"id":19339255,"author":"cletus","month":"2019-03","story":"Galaxy Simulations Offer a New Solution to the Fermi Paradox","stance":"rec","why":"Early video in a series on the Fermi Paradox.","quote":"Obligatory plug for Isaac Arthur who has a series of videos on the Fermi Paradox.","text":"tl;dr it shouldn't take as long as previously thought to colonize the galaxy based on a simulation. That's largely an irrelevant finding that adds nothing to the Fermi Paradox because the time it would take to colonize the galaxy is so small in cosmological terms that shortening it is irrelevant. What's more the authors decided to modeled the suitability of star systems impeding colonization when this seems like it would be largely irrelevant because starfaring civilizations seem highly unlikely to be planet-bound as this is a pretty inefficient use of mass. The Dyson Sphere (Swarm) seems much more likely at which point the only thing of value is the star. The planets are just piles of raw materials ultimately. Obligatory plug for Isaac Arthur who has a series of videos on the Fermi Paradox. Here's an early one [1]. There are 10-20 others that go into particular aspects. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["zmCTmgavkrQ",1],["Pld8wTa16Jk",1],["Qam5BkXIEhQ",1],["O5BJVO3PDeQ",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["E3URhJx0NSw",1],["U4FNBMZsqrY",1],["QfuK8la0y6s",1],["eBCbdThIJNE",1]]},"ysa5OBhXz-Q":{"wy":"HN cites it as the go-to example of how one keystone species can reshape an entire ecosystem.","q":{"x":"You need to learn about trophic cascades. Here's a quick intro","i":13052443,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":22311731,"author":"heimatau","month":"2020-02","story":"Colorado voters are set to decide if wolves should be reintroduced to the state","stance":"rec","why":"Wolves changed deer behavior, producing ripple effects across the ecosystem.","quote":"It's interesting how the wolves changed the entire ecosystem by just changing the behavior of deer","text":"This could be good for Colorado. I had to watch this video [1] for my biology class. It's interesting how the wolves changed the entire ecosystem by just changing the behavior of deer (they avoided grazing near the river because that's where the wolves wanted to be). The ripple effects are fairly significant. 1 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":14402727,"author":"GFischer","month":"2017-05","story":"Last major primeval forest in Europe on 'brink of collapse'","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on wolves improving the Yellowstone ecosystem.","quote":"I saw a pretty cool documentary on how reintroducing wolves to Yellowstone changed the ecosystem for the better.","text":"I saw a pretty cool documentary on how reintroducing wolves to Yellowstone changed the ecosystem for the better. Not the one I saw but very similar: [VIDEO] More serious article: [LINK]"},{"id":13052443,"author":"nkoren","month":"2016-11","story":"Let's Colonize Titan","stance":"rec","why":"Quick intro to trophic cascades.","quote":"You need to learn about trophic cascades. Here's a quick intro","text":"You need to learn about trophic cascades. Here's a quick intro: [VIDEO] This played out in the Pleistocene extinctions to a very significant degree, having tremendous effect on ecosystems everywhere. Here's an entire conference worth of the subject: [LINK]"},{"id":9864491,"author":"zackmorris","month":"2015-07","story":"Why is the Northwest so warm?","stance":"rec","why":"Sums up how ecosystem tampering has wide-ranging effects.","quote":"this video sums up a lot of what I was trying to say","text":"In hindsight this looks like the idle ramblings of someone out of touch with reality. But in truth it's closer to the opposite - I've spent too much time analyzing the repercussions of everything because that's what programmers do. If someone ever finds this, this video sums up a lot of what I was trying to say: [VIDEO] Now multiply that by every ecosystem that has been tampered with by humans across the face of the planet and decide for yourself if our climate has been affected."},{"id":29841086,"author":"hsn915","month":"2022-01","story":"Quit Your Job","stance":"reference","why":"Explains wolves' reintroduction changing prey behavior and regenerating vegetation.","quote":"unchecked herboviore species has caused a lot of damage to the environment, grazing much of the vegetation.","text":"> justification to hurt others for your own gain You're jumping into conclusions. His sentiment is better understood in terms of the balance of nature. He posted this on his Twitter a while back: [VIDEO] The video is titled \"How Wolves Change Rivers\". \"When wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone National Park in the United States after being absent nearly 70 years, the most remarkable \"trophic cascade\" occurred.\" As the video explains, unchecked herboviore species has caused a lot of damage to the environment, grazing much of the vegetation. The introduction of wolves radically changed the behavior of the prey species and they start avoiding certain places like valleys where they can easily be trapped. This allowed the vegetation to regenerate and trees started to grow. This resulted in birds moving in. Beavers started to increase, and the dams they built provided habitat for a bunch of other species of animals. And so on and so forth. The regeneration of the vegetation provided stability to the banks of the rivers so they collapsed less often."},{"id":27851786,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2021-07","story":"Young, Dumb, and Broke: Why Outdoorsy Types Suck at Money","stance":"reference","why":"Creatures have specific ecological roles maintaining balance.","quote":"Every creature has a specific role.","text":"Nature does not exist for you to \"enjoy\" it. Nature seeks balance by evolving creatures that have a reciprocal relationship with the environment. Every creature has a specific role. [VIDEO] Humans have no predators. We are effectively an invasive species that does not seek balance with the environment and thrive at the expense of the balance that nature has created in an unsustainable manner."},{"id":23170025,"author":"floatrock","month":"2020-05","story":"Moths have 'secret role' as crucial pollinators","stance":"reference","why":"Wolves in Yellowstone as the well-known keystone species example.","quote":"reintroducing wolves into Yellowstone changed the course of rivers, made them less eroding, and generally increased ecosystem diversity","text":"Keystone species are fascinating and show how we really need more systemic thinking. The one that tons of people have seen is how reintroducing wolves into Yellowstone changed the course of rivers, made them less eroding, and generally increased ecosystem diversity: [VIDEO] Beavers are another one: [LINK] Remember how we learned a lot of westward expansion in US colonial days was led by mountainmen trapping beavers to use their pelts in fancy european hats and we kept going further west because we kept killing off the beavers while demand for fancy hats kept going up? Turns out when you remove all those beaver ponds that would slow down water flow, you end up with a lot more erosion, incision, and flooding -- the landscape we have now. Fisherman think beaver ponds decrease fish populations because they tend to have warmer water, but this doesn't quite pass the thousands-years-of-coevolution-before-beaver-extermination sniff test... some experiments have showed that beaver ponds actually increase fish stock because they create lots of fish nurseries, and trout and salmon can instinctively swim up through beaver dams. Then when you consider how things like aquaponics incorporate fish because fish poop makes great fertilizer, it starts to become clear how important beaver ponds can be to nutrient cycling in these ecosystems. One of the most fascinating fish-based nutrient flows is salmon migrations... they're hatched upstream, put on most of their growth and biomass in the ocean"},{"id":19394821,"author":"michaelwilson","month":"2019-03","story":"28,000-year-old mammoth cells have shown reactivity in mouse egg cells","stance":"reference","why":"Counterintuitive ecological effects, wolves changing Yellowstone rivers.","quote":"the re-introduction of wolves actually changed rivers in Yellowstone","text":"It defies logic but case in point: the re-introduction of wolves actually changed rivers in Yellowstone: [VIDEO] . Not claiming this proposal will have the same impact, or even a positive one."},{"id":18926858,"author":"briantakita","month":"2019-01","story":"Climate Concerns Are Pushing Oil Majors to Look Beyond Fossil Fuels","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The obsession I'm mentioning is based one's worldview & presuppositions...How we reify & approach the problems determine the solutions. Appeal to authority will ensure policies that enrich the authority, with the associated conflicts of interest, confirmation bias, & transfer of power from the population to the authority. Remember, that plant growth is promoted with Carbon Dioxide. A principle of Permaculture is turning the problem into the solution. Elevated CO2 is plant food, if we properly take advantage of the opportunity. Here's an anology re: agriculture. Conventional monoculture petro-chemical till agriculture optimizes for yield & only yield. Unfortunately, there are pests, molds, blight, disease, weather issues, drought, soil conditions that reduce yield. In response, pesticides, herbicides, GMOs, mass irrigation (disrupting watersheds), petro-chemical fertilizers are all sold to the farmer. This leads to soil erosion, destruction of ecosystems, pollution, & lower quality (less nutrition) food. Instead, one can practice regenerative agriculture, that optimizes the ecosystem & soil remediation. Earthworks, such as swales, terraces, & ponds are used to capture rainfall & cycle the water through the property for as long as possible. Polycultures of plants that compliment each other are planted together. Animals are allowed to graze, which brings in fertilizer. Predatory insects & birds are introduced into the habitat to control pests & molds. And you get a higher overal"},{"id":15516497,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2017-10","story":"Iceland's attempts to replant its forests","stance":"reference","why":"Wolf reintroduction reduced deer and altered the ecosystem.","quote":"reintroduced wolves into the ecosystem, reducing the dear population, altering the ecosystem for the better","text":"There's a really interesting article about how Yellowstone (a national park in the US) reintroduced wolves into the ecosystem, reducing the dear population, altering the ecosystem for the better: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15521157,"author":"atmosx","month":"2017-10","story":"Iceland's attempts to replant its forests","stance":"reference","why":"Wolves pushed deer from areas, changing the landscape.","quote":"The introduction of wolves changed the entire landscape by pushing the deers out of the some areas.","text":"Somewhat relevant video[1]. The introduction of wolves changed the entire landscape by pushing the deers out of the some areas. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14017665,"author":"itchyjunk","month":"2017-04","story":"Urban Evolution: How Species Adapt, or Don't, to City Living","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"The moths evolved to become dark in order to match the background of these trees.\" \"Those guppies evolved to be very colorful, over the course of just two years.\" \"Over ten years they evolved significantly shorter legs.\" Remember, the variation already existed. There were already white and dark months in the population and natural selection caused increase or decrease in the ratios. The black moth did not evolve into white. The variation for short and long legs already existed. Short legs probably increased reproductive fitness in once case and not the other. Causing the number of individuals with certain trait to increase or decrease. My professor has repeated this the entire semester and she wants us to never forget that mutation is the only source of variation. If the variation is not already present, natural selection cannot trigger it. This is how some species go extinct. Natural selection is one source of evolution. Also, distinction between micro vs macro evolution is important here. [0] \"But there's no reason to believe this. Why shouldn't they be adapting to our conditions just like any other evolutionary situation?\" Because natural selection doesn't cause variation. Mutation is the only source of variation. Variation must already exist in a population for natural selection to work on it. \"What she found was that in each case they had changed. The lizards in the city had evolved longer legs—presumably useful for hanging onto broad surfaces, like walls—and they had e"},{"id":13306857,"author":"partycoder","month":"2017-01","story":"Forget Survival of the Fittest: It Is Kindness That Counts (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Species relate in unexpected ways within ecosystems.","quote":"Each species have a specific role in the ecosystem and sometimes they're related in very unexpected ways.","text":"Well, I would rather call it organization rather than just care. While the highest level of animal organization is called eusociality, humans are not hardwired to be eusocial but can display very advanced division of labor. Now, I would not really say advantage or disadvantage. Each species have a specific role in the ecosystem and sometimes they're related in very unexpected ways. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12491690,"author":"astrodust","month":"2016-09","story":"Can Anyone Stop the Man Who Will Try Just About Anything to Stop Climate Change?","stance":"reference","why":"Single species can alter whole landscapes and water flow.","quote":"Wolves, once reintroduced to Yellowstone, have changed the entire park, altering water flow on a massive scale","text":"Rabbits have completely transformed the Australian continent, one so dramatic you can see the effects from space. ( [LINK] ) Cane toads have a smaller but measurable effect since they utterly ruin entire ecosystems. If you think it's impossible for a single organism to have an effect on climate you're mistaken. Wolves, once reintroduced to Yellowstone, have changed the entire park, altering water flow on a massive scale: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11363629,"author":"briantakita","month":"2016-03","story":"Could Harvesting Fog Help Solve the World’s Water Crisis?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of ecosystem restoration via wolf reintroduction.","quote":"Reintroducing wolves to Yellowstone caused the river to flow again","text":"This solution would have negative disruptive impacts. Harvesting fog would effect dependent microclimates & ecosystems, by taking water out of the air. This also has an affect of causing a greater concentration of the water to be held by big institutions (corporations & government). This creates a social power imbalance, concentrates the economy to global institutions, & reduces autonomy of the people who live on the land. Plundering & profiteering off the commons & reducing autonomy in the guise of humanitarian efforts. --- re: better solutions, after all harvesting fog is a \"solution\". Regenerate the ecosystems. Encourage small scale regenerative agriculture, that utilizes native plants in polycultures to restore ecosystems and stimulate local health, novelty, & economy. Restoring local ecosystems has a positive effect on the local watershed. Reintroducing wolves to Yellowstone caused the river to flow again & had positive fundamental impacts on the local ecosystem. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10249047,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2015-09","story":"Elephants Shot with Poison Arrows Travel to Humans for Help","stance":"reference","why":"Shows dramatic impact of reintroducing wolves to Yellowstone.","quote":"dramatic impacts of reintroducing wolves to Yellowstone","text":"Loss of species diversity, 50 million years of evolution, and potentially profound impacts on ecosystems. Look for the story in the past year or so about dramatic impacts of reintroducing wolves to Yellowstone. How Wolves Change Rivers: [VIDEO] [LINK] Destroy a species and it's gone forever. Moreover, if you're looking to eliminate suffering, it's humans and their rampant overconsumption which are driving that. Not the elephant."},{"id":8342382,"author":"schiffern","month":"2014-09","story":"Can the World Really Set Aside Half of the Planet for Wildlife?","stance":"reference","why":"Wolves in Yellowstone as example of ecosystem services.","quote":"the services provided by wolves in Yellowstone, which were never fully understood until they were removed and then re-introduced","text":">In what way? Because we are all completely dependent on ecosystem services provided by those species, such as erosion control (without which your country erodes away to desert), rainwater buffering (without which you experience catastrophic flooding), and transpiration (without which you lose almost all inland rainfall[1]). Loss of individual species is just the symptom. The real problem—and what this article discusses—is wholesale destruction of wilderness. Don't imagine that just a handful of species can provide these services either. Since ecosystems are complex adaptive systems, a particular species' role is almost always subtle and interconnected. See: the services provided by wolves in Yellowstone, which were never fully understood until they were removed and then re-introduced[2]. So why is wilderness important? Fundamentally, wilderness is arranged (and so, it functions) in completely different ways than human-tended landscapes. Now it's obvious that a suburb is different from a forest, but what's less obvious is that the way wilderness works is much more efficient . It's not dependent on a constant stream of material extracted from \"somewhere else\", but on average it produces far more economic value when ecosystem services are accurately accounted for. Surprise, surprise: economics seems to suggest that those living on a spaceship shouldn't take a sledgehammer to the life support system… (of course, the ultimate trick would be to design human landscapes that also fu"}],"r":[["iHzzSao6ypE",1],["2Q26XIKtwXQ",1],["Ztc6QPNUqls",1]]},"5MxH1sfJLBQ":{"wy":"Commenters repeatedly recommend it in JWST threads as a great explanation of clever mechanical design that is accurate and long lasting.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend","i":30731480,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30731480,"author":"infogulch","month":"2022-03","story":"First images from James Webb telescope exceed expectations","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains JWST flexure actuators with a 3D-printed replica","quote":"I highly recommend","text":"The actuator is a genius flexure design that is both mechanically simple and accurate. Breaking Taps 3d-prints a working replica and describes the design. I highly recommend: [VIDEO] The actuator breaking wasn't as concerning after seeing the design."},{"id":46562099,"author":"bigiain","month":"2026-01","story":"The (likely?) cheapest home-made Michelson interferometer","stance":"rec","why":"Explains JWST mirror actuators and offers a 3D printable version","quote":"there's a great youtube video explaining how the actuators to align the James Webb mirrors work","text":"I have a related question, how far are you turning the thumbscrews when making adjustments? I think M3 standard thread pitch is 0.5mm, so to a first approximation that almost 1000 wavelengths (if I have the SI units right in my head, and I'm not 3 orders of magnitude out?). I suspect the left/right and up/down adjustments have as fairly high lever ratio, but I can't imagine you could successfully adjust the in/out distance with any precision (not in the 690nm sense anyway)? Is the in/out distance not important so long as the beams are aligned? Dunno if you've seen it, but there's a great youtube video explaining how the actuators to align the James Webb mirrors work: [VIDEO] including a 3D printable version of them: [LINK] \"This is a replica of JWST's mirror actuators. Six of these actuators are paired into a hexapod / stewart platform arrangement and used to control 6 degrees of freedom of each mirror segment (tip, tilt, roll, x, y, z translation).\""},{"id":31074400,"author":"mywacaday","month":"2022-04","story":"James Webb telescope's coldest instrument reaches operating temperature","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how JWST actuators work","quote":"It's a great watch.","text":"This video [VIDEO] was posted here [LINK] about how the actuators work on the James Webb telescope. It's a great watch."},{"id":30732283,"author":"blenderdt","month":"2022-03","story":"First images from James Webb telescope exceed expectations","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how one motor aligns each JWST mirror segment","quote":"Here a nice video that explains the mechanism that aligns each mirror","text":"Here a nice video that explains the mechanism that aligns each mirror: [VIDEO] It is amazing that this is done with only a single motor. It works something like this: one direction of the motor sets which axis to align, the other direction sets the alignment of the chosen axis."},{"id":30778277,"author":"mywacaday","month":"2022-03","story":"Mars helicopter Ingenuity completes 22nd flight","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how JWST actuators achieve accuracy and longevity","quote":"It's a great watch and shows how well thought out design can produce accurate and long lasting parts.","text":"This video [VIDEO] was posted here [LINK] about how the actuators work on the James Webb telescope. It's a great watch and shows how well thought out design can produce accurate and long lasting parts."},{"id":33342263,"author":"RicoElectrico","month":"2022-10","story":"Achieving 3-axes of microscopy imaging with just a motor and a linear actuator","stance":"reference","why":"JWST actuators overload a single axis for coarse and fine control","quote":"Reminds me of the JWST actuators which also","text":"Clever. I wonder how the mechanical realization looks like, as I can't really wrap my head around it. Reminds me of the JWST actuators which also \"overload\" a single axis for coarse/fine control: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lix-vr_AF38",1],["Q3oItpVa9fs",1],["2A2NY29iQdI",1],["j5v8D-alAKE",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["p8u_k2LIZyo",1],["Ecy1FBdCRbQ",1],["htYh-Tq7ZBI",1],["Y9FZ4igNxNA",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["ne99laPUxN4",1],["ImoQqNyRL8Y",1],["hVimVzgtD6w",1]]},"d3kfEeMZ65c":{"wy":"Treated as the definitive explanation of Android's audio latency problem, showing it spans the entire stack.","q":{"x":"is a must-watch","i":10677072,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10677072,"author":"on_and_off","month":"2015-12","story":"Android Audio Latency","stance":"strong_rec","why":"In-depth explanation of the challenges of Android audio latency","quote":"is a must-watch","text":"If you want an in-depth explanation (AP mostly skims the surface) : [VIDEO] is a must-watch. Obviously, it does not cover the work accomplished these past two years, but it gives a good idea of the challenges of audio latency on Android"},{"id":10680460,"author":"Polyphonie","month":"2015-12","story":"Android Audio Latency","stance":"rec","why":"Insight into why Android high performance audio is hard","quote":"It probably provides the best insight to the problem Google engineers were and probably is still facing","text":"You can watch this I/O session from a few years back: [VIDEO] It probably provides the best insight to the problem Google engineers were and probably is still facing in regards to high performance audio on Android. Even when the latency is brought down, there's still other audio related issues/glitches (like buffer underruns). Even now that Android is measurably better than it was previously, things like buffer underruns can still affect the overall usability. Increasing the buffer (to fix the underruns) render the device less suitable for such things as using it with external MIDI controller. You can see from Superpowered's audio latency measurements: [LINK] that the buffer for iOS is half that of the best Android (the dual-core Nexus 9)- 64 instead of 128 for Android. I"},{"id":9390409,"author":"dottrap","month":"2015-04","story":"Android’s 10 Millisecond Problem explained","stance":"rec","why":"Shows audio latency problems across the whole Android stack","quote":"Google I/O 2013 did a pretty good talk on the problem and shows how there are problems across the entire stack.","text":"This is a great description of the lowest level problem. But for those wondering why iOS doesn't have the problem or why only 10ms is such a difference, the answer is that this is only the beginning of the problem of Android audio latency. There are still additional problems that add latency that span the entire Android stack from the actual hardware and drivers, to the kernel and scheduler, to the Android implementation of their audio APIs. Anybody who has tried to do any serious audio on Android knows the infamous Bug 3434: [LINK] Google I/O 2013 did a pretty good talk on the problem and shows how there are problems across the entire stack. Glenn Kasten pretty much carries the brunt of all the audio problems with Android. I find it telling that he had to handcraft his own latency measurement device using an oscilloscope and LED because there were no actual tools built into the OS to help them analyze performance. [VIDEO] Audio has been terrible since Android's inception. It has improved a little over time, but unfortunately, 7 years later is is still pretty much unacceptable for any serious work."},{"id":7231619,"author":"fidotron","month":"2014-02","story":"Show HN: Fiddlewax","stance":"rec","why":"Google I/O talk focused on Android audio latency","quote":"They gave a good talk at I/O on just audio latency","text":"They gave a good talk at I/O on just audio latency: [VIDEO] The touch latency alone is generally in the >80ms ballpark as well though, and varies a lot between devices."},{"id":16819680,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-04","story":"Fuchsia is not Linux","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There was even a Google IO 2013 talk about it.","text":"Sure. Originally Android only had the Java media APIs, with a quite complex state. [LINK] Android 2.3 introduced OpenSL ES into the NDK and Android 4.0 got OpenMAX. All of them weren't up to the game of realtime audio due to the Android architecture. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] There are plenty of other articles online when you search for audio issues on Android. There was even a Google IO 2013 talk about it. [VIDEO] Meanwhile Samsung had customized their Android versions to properly support real time audio and offered an SDK for their handsets. [LINK] At Google IO 2016 the problem was again discussed, with the Samsung team from Professional Audio being called on stage to discuss their collaboration. [LINK] Finally last year at Google IO 2017, the new real time audio was announced, as C only lib. [LINK] [LINK] The announced C++ wrapper ended up being an external project on github, detached from the NDK, that you need to integrate yourself, without any guarantees how long Google will care to maintain it. Oh, to make things even better, it is already clear from Android P draft documentation that new audio APIs are on their way."},{"id":6334456,"author":"aufreak3","month":"2013-09","story":"Android is for startups","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Even Google folks admit that [1].","text":"> Besides, have you tried Android recently? You might be suprised to discover that Android is better. .. not yet for music apps. Even Google folks admit that [1]. This really can't wait any longer. Where is Garage Band for Android please? ... and to those saying \"you can't complain only about music, look at the games, todo lists, blah blah\", sorry music IS my domain and that's the only thing I care about. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6097615,"author":"jordanthoms","month":"2013-07","story":"What's new in Android 4.3","stance":"reference","why":"Google talk on Android audio latency improvements","quote":"Android 4.3 has halved the audio latency on the Nexus 7 (2012) and Nexus 4.","text":"This is something which is being worked on, but it's a complex problem and takes time to improve. Android 4.3 has halved the audio latency on the Nexus 7 (2012) and Nexus 4. See here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6098908,"author":"kllrnohj","month":"2013-07","story":"Google Unveils The New Nexus 7 Android Tablet","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> So 4.3 fixes neither the ad-hoc WiFi issue #82 nor the audio latency issue #3434. Low latency audio is \"fixed\" on the Android side, it needs better device support though. Apps need to actually support the fast path as well, which I don't think many do. [VIDEO] I don't know if the new Nexus 7 supports the low latency audio path, though. But Android itself does have low latency audio, it's a device-specific thing though."}],"r":[["5PmHRSeA2c8",1],["5kj5ApnhPAE",1],["yQ16_YxLbB8",1]]},"giYbq4HmfGA":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the best detailed explanation from the language's creator of how Elixir's new type system works and why.","q":{"x":"If you're into watching talks, this will be well worth your time.","i":40657484,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40657484,"author":"gregors","month":"2024-06","story":"Elixir 1.17 released: set-theoretic types in patterns, durations, OTP 27","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Valim on the foundations of the Elixir type system","quote":"If you're into watching talks, this will be well worth your time.","text":"If you're into watching talks, this will be well worth your time. ElixirConf 2023 - José Valim - The foundations of the Elixir type system [VIDEO]"},{"id":38919993,"author":"H12","month":"2024-01","story":"Elixir is now a gradually typed language","stance":"rec","why":"Details how the new Elixir type system works","quote":"Jose's elixirconf keynote this year did a great job providing details on how how this new type system works","text":"My understanding is \"yes, but only type errors that violate guards and pattern matching\". Jose's elixirconf keynote this year did a great job providing details on how how this new type system works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38215262,"author":"whalesalad","month":"2023-11","story":"Switching to Elixir","stance":"rec","why":"Language creator on achieving typing goals without static typing","quote":"the creator of the language has a fantastic talk on this","text":"static typing is a means to an end. if you can achieve the same end without static typing, good right? that is the goal of spec. the creator of the language has a fantastic talk on this [VIDEO]"},{"id":48391416,"author":"aeonfox","month":"2026-06","story":"Elixir v1.20 released: now a gradually typed language","stance":"reference","why":"Valim explains why Elixir should adopt types, nuanced","quote":"He gives a lot more nuanced take than 'types are useless'","text":"OP might be referring to Jose Valim's 2023 ElixirConf talk where he's explaining why Elixir should go down the path of types. He gives a lot more nuanced take than 'types are useless', which is more like 'types are less useful than people think in the context of Elixir development'. (Which makes sense because he's in the middle of implementing a type system for Elixir.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":48398251,"author":"mechanicum","month":"2026-06","story":"Elixir v1.20 released: now a gradually typed language","stance":"reference","why":"One of several presentations on the type system","quote":"","text":"Short answer: “a type system centered on the use of set-theoretic types (unions, intersections, negations) that satisfy the commutativity and distributivity properties of the corresponding set-theoretic operations”. Long answer, well, there are blog posts[0], the Design Principles of the Elixir Type System paper[1] and related presentations[2, 3, 4] that talk about it at length. Giuseppe Castagna’s site has many more related papers: [LINK] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44661068,"author":"_acco","month":"2025-07","story":"Why Elixir? Common misconceptions","stance":"reference","why":"Valim's balanced view on type systems","quote":"José Valim giving his balanced view on type systems","text":"It doesn't matter as much as you think. I believe this is in part due to how assertive most Elixir code tends to be. [1] These assertions not only aid the LSP and can cause compiler warnings/errors, they also help LLMs just like types do. Still, every release now contains new type system features. Next up is full type inference. [2] After that will be typed structs. [1] José Valim giving his balanced view on type systems: [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":43515465,"author":"rapind","month":"2025-03","story":"Koto Programming Language","stance":"reference","why":"Pointer to Elixir's gradual types work","quote":"Elixir is working on gradual types, which is something I would keep an eye on.","text":"I'd keep an eye on Roc [LINK] Also, Elixir is working on gradual types, which is something I would keep an eye on. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37637893,"author":"pawelduda","month":"2023-09","story":"Unpacking Elixir: Resilience","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Elixir types being on the roadmap","quote":"Types in Elixir are on the roadmap","text":"Types in Elixir are on the roadmap [1] but I don't think there's concrete ETA For now you have typespecs and dialyzer that tries to infer types and detect incorrect assumptions in code but I found it cumbersome to work with. Testing in Elixir is first class so I prefer that for now. You get doctests (documentation that is also a runnable test), like in Python, which I find very useful when looking at some package source. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["r8RxkpUvxK0",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["IxuU5L2MEII",1],["d2ogGZXmepY",1]]},"rCMh7srOqvw":{"wy":"HN users recommend the series to newcomers learning Emacs, Doom, org-mode, projectile and magit.","q":{"x":"To get started I highly recommend this series by Zaiste","i":22885570,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22885570,"author":"gtpedrosa","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: Resources to grok Emacs and use it well?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series on setting up Doom and org-mode, projectile, magit, dired.","quote":"To get started I highly recommend this series by Zaiste","text":"I have started using emacs in 2016 because of org-mode. It was powerful enough to make me use emacs on a daily basis since them. However, I have been using RStudio/Sublime as IDE/editor, not emacs. Curiously last weekend I have found a video about org-roam and the presenter mentioned he was using Doom emacs. I was curious enough to try it. It uses vim key bindings by default. To get started I highly recommend this series by Zaiste [0], he explains how to set up the environment and digs into org-mode which were my main concerns. I found the sections on projectile, magit, dired and avy very helpful. Now I am tempted to give it a try in one of my projects. The downfall so far is I didnt find an in depth explanation on how to grok it with python. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23210040,"author":"chairfield","month":"2020-05","story":"Making Emacs Popular Again","stance":"rec","why":"Zaiste makes Doom videos useful regardless of config.","quote":"Zaiste Programming makes Doom videos[4], but I find them useful regardless of my config","text":"I think there's a lot to learn from starting from scratch, and it's something I hope to do too. Gladly! I'll keep the list to what I've studied so far. Here goes: - If you ever want to explore, say, Doom (Spacemacs is likely too bloated for someone who gravitates to a vanilla config), chemacs[1] is a nifty, simple profile switcher - I think there's a lot of value in studying what they've done with their mnemonic keybinding systems (I love being able to narrow to an org-mode subtree and widen again with =, n= and =, N=, respectively, as but one of many examples) - Sasha Chua is a good source, as she's very knowledgeable and put out a drawn 1-pager[2] on starting Emacs - it's geared towards standard Emacs keybindings - Personally, I'm a huge fan of Evil-mode for Vim keybindings, as they're powerful and portable and I had basic familiarity with Vim before picking up Emacs - I haven't found a rough edge in Evil-mode yet - it seems very refined - Dired is worth getting a handle on early since any improvement in how you can navigate Emacs translates - Magit is pure magic, and I now have my full ~/org under version control with what feels like near-0 overhead - Seorenn makes mostly Spacemacs videos[3] and Zaiste Programming makes Doom videos[4], but I find them useful regardless of my config - you may just want to skip to the videos on packages that interest you - If you haven't yet, choosing either Helm or Ivy is huge - Personally, I'm happy with helm in Spacemacs and I was happy w"},{"id":19279462,"author":"zaiste","month":"2019-03","story":"A community-driven port of Emacs to Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Short Doom Emacs screencast for newcomers by the commenter.","quote":"I've recorded a short Doom screencast for newcomers","text":"There is also Doom Emacs [1] which provides Vim experience out-of-the-box. It also comes with a fantastic theme. I've been using Vi/Vim for more than two decades now. Doom made me feel at home. I used to envy Emacs all these nice features such as Org Mode, Magit and even the ability to use Lisp to configure your environment. Now it's the best of both worlds. I just wish Emacs' onboarding process / cohesiveness was as good as in VS Code. It would be a dream come true. ;) PS. I've recorded a short Doom screencast for newcomers [2] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23696383,"author":"taude","month":"2020-07","story":"How Did Vim Become So Popular?","stance":"reference","why":"One of the videos watched while learning Doom Emacs.","quote":"I've only watched a couple of these for the topics I'm interested","text":"I've recently blown away my several years old custom Emacs config and started over with vanilla Doom Emacs (it's supposedly a faster version of Spacemacs, but I don't really know, I just took a stab and grabbed one of them). I spent a weekend doing some VIM tutorials and writing down common key strokes of things I do all the time, and then on a Monday, it was all VIM navigation. A lot of your regular Emacs meta-stuff will still work, and for that, you can slowly start replacing with the spacebar-b key stroke for your buffer management, spacebar-p for projectile, etc.... Here's a couple of the videos I watched: 1) [LINK] 2) I've only watched a couple of these for the topics I'm interested: [VIDEO] 3) for common VIM, I went with this: [LINK] 4) Recently, I found this guys short six-part series that gets you navigating around VIM fast, but he's an acquired taste: [LINK] There's still some things I like better about Emacs navigation and there's a lot I like about VIM navigation. I prefer Emacs crtl-a and ctrl-e for navigating to front and end of the line (instead of $ and ^, which is an awkward shift+number key on my keyboard). However, in Doom Emacs (likely Spacemacs too since I believe they both support Evil-mod) if you're in Insert Mode, most of your old Emacs navigation will still work, so this makes it a decent crutch for transitioning."}],"r":[["qd_hEja6bzE",1],["huEtJw7pfLk",1]]},"rv-mFSoZOkE":{"wy":"HN readers recommend it as the best explanation of what nuclear waste really is and how much of it is reusable fuel.","q":{"x":"This talk from 2010 is really amazing at understanding what 'nuclear waste' actually is and what it will be.","i":31129779,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31129779,"author":"panick21_","month":"2022-04","story":"Turning nuclear waste into diamond batteries","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains what nuclear waste is and will become","quote":"This talk from 2010 is really amazing at understanding what 'nuclear waste' actually is and what it will be.","text":"A much more practical use of nuclear waste is to make traditional RTG. Nuclear waste could also be used for medical isotopes. There are lots of other interesting things in nuclear waste. This talk from 2010 is really amazing at understanding what 'nuclear waste' actually is and what it will be. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22141881,"author":"quindecagon","month":"2020-01","story":"Rolls-Royce plans mini nuclear reactors by 2029","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on nuclear waste of related interest","quote":"This talk on nuclear waste may also be of interest to you[2].","text":"Interestingly, this post on an application for Nuclear waste was on the front page[1]. This talk on nuclear waste may also be of interest to you[2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15554815,"author":"nickik","month":"2017-10","story":"Thruster for Mars mission breaks records","stance":"rec","why":"Explores what nuclear waste actually contains","quote":"Here is some exploration of what is contained in nuclear waste","text":"While this is true it is just another reflection of the backwards nuclear policy of NASA and the US government. With different reactors, creation of more Pu-238 would not be a huge problem. The issue is just that there seems to be very little interest in pushing forward any areas and as so often the case many of these technologies are mutually supporting. I would really love a real push forward on Molten Salt reactors that would give you the ability to filter out a number of important substances that have a hole host of different uses. It would also go a long way in solving the nuclear waste problems. Here is some exploration of what is contained in nuclear waste: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19897077,"author":"petre","month":"2019-05","story":"Zapping Nuclear Waste in Minutes Is Nobel Winner’s Holy Grail Quest","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that waste is mostly reusable fuel","quote":"","text":"More like 300 years. Most of what remains of the waste is americium and plutonium which can be reused as nuclear fuel. [VIDEO] Most of the issues with nuclear waste are proliferation related. That's why it's stored and not given away to let's say Iran."}],"r":[["vvZA9n3e5pc",1],["hgN8pCMLI2U",1],["040ejWnFkj0",1],["4zG0uecYSMA",1],["c52QhiXsmyI",1],["EczW2IWdnOM",1],["YAtLTLiqNwg",1],["2y5Pv4yN0b0",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["cidchWg74Y4",1],["-4FXLQoLDBA",1],["ROor6_NGIWU",1],["GNG6ZzDh9C8",1],["GTQnarzmTOc",1]]},"uTRUQ-RKfUs":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an engaging analysis of how the Hobbit films were made, in threads on CGI and Tolkien.","q":{"x":"Lindsay Ellis has a three-part video essay series","i":30182343,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":20943734,"author":"muzzio","month":"2019-09","story":"Postmortem: Every Frame a Painting (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Postmortem on the Hobbit films, a Hugo nominee.","quote":"Lindsay Ellis has some great film/TV analysis videos.","text":"Longer form than EFaP, but Lindsay Ellis has some great film/TV analysis videos. She's usually more focused on writing than anything else, though. Here's a postmortem she did on the Hobbit films, which was recently nominated for a Hugo award: [VIDEO] Other solid works by her include a postmortem on the last season of Game of Thrones and an analysis of themes in Michael Bay's Transformers. (I know that last one sounds weird, but it's really well done.)"},{"id":30182343,"author":"scott_s","month":"2022-02","story":"Over-reliance on CGI in movies","stance":"rec","why":"Video essay series on how the Hobbit films were made.","quote":"Lindsay Ellis has a three-part video essay series","text":"Agreed agreed. The problem with The Hobbit was not the visual effects. The problem was that it was made without a plan, under duress. Lindsay Ellis has a three-part video essay series ( [VIDEO] yes, I know it says 1/2, I promise there is a 2/3) exploring this."},{"id":22068682,"author":"Apocryphon","month":"2020-01","story":"Christopher Tolkien has died","stance":"rec","why":"Series on the Hobbit films' troubled production.","quote":"Here's the first video in a small YouTube series on it","text":"The Hobbit films had a troubled production and were terribly rushed. Here's the first video in a small YouTube series on it: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["VVp8UGjECt4",1],["Ik1AK56FtVc",1],["UWEjxkkB8Xs",1],["5HbYScltf1c",1]]},"zz4YVJ4aRfg":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a science-backed overview of sulforaphane's health effects, particularly for cancer.","q":{"x":"Here is a science-backed video talking about the benefits of a substance found in broccoli sprouts w.r.t. cancer.","i":13686212,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":22029075,"author":"TheAceOfHearts","month":"2020-01","story":"Ask HN: What were your most impactful podcast episodes from 2019?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Case for isothiocyanates and sulforaphane health effects.","quote":"There's two FoundMyFitness podcasts that positively impacted my life.","text":"There's two FoundMyFitness podcasts that positively impacted my life. I've been telling everyone I know to try a broccoli sprout smoothie because of the outstanding effect it's been having on my health. I suspect a fair number of HN readers might also benefit from giving it a try. Since this is still an active research topic I think it prudent to remind readers to take all health or medical claims with a grain of salt. My first smoothie caused a physiological reaction and it lit up something in my brain. A while after drinking it I messaged a few friends to tell them that I value our friendship. It appears to make you less autistic, or something along those lines. There appear to be additional positive effects, although I hesitate to describe them because they even sounds like bullshit to my own ears. \"Sulforaphane and Its Effects on Cancer, Mortality, Aging, Brain and Behavior, Heart Disease & More\" [0]: > Isothiocyanates are some of the most important plant compounds you can get in your diet. In this video I make the most comprehensive case for them that has ever been made. Short attention span? Skip to your favorite topic by clicking one of the time points below. Full timeline below the fold... \"Jed Fahey, Sc.D. on Isothiocyanates, the Nrf2 Pathway, Moringa & Sulforaphane Supplementation\" [1]: > Dr. Jed W. Fahey is a nutritional biochemist with broad training and extensive background in plant physiology, human nutrition, phytochemistry and nutritional biochemistry. He is t"},{"id":28658087,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2021-09","story":"Ask HN: Blogs/YouTube Channels on Cancer?","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting content on sulforaphane and cancer.","quote":"Here are a few I have found interesting.","text":"Sorry to hear your are going through this. Here are a few I have found interesting. I am not a doctor and by no means is any of this something I am suggesting. It's just interesting content on your topic. - Prof. Thomas Seyfried - 'Cancer as a Metabolic Disease: Implications for Novel Therapies' [LINK] - A New Cancer Strategy [LINK] - The MOST Important Cancer Prevention [LINK] - Antiangiogenic Therapy [LINK] - Sulforaphane and Its Effects on Cancer, Mortality, Aging, Brain and Behavior, Heart Disease & More [VIDEO] - Q&A with Dr. Jed Fahey on Sulforaphane, Moringa and Chemoprotection [An authoritative discussion!] [LINK] - [LINK] Also watch this site if your doctors run out of ideas. Look for newer and more targeted methods. [LINK] Some people that were written off by doctors had some success with prolonged fasting. Also important would be to get opinions by many doctors inside and outside of your medial group."},{"id":13686212,"author":"amelius","month":"2017-02","story":"Can Changing When and What We Eat Help Outwit Disease?","stance":"rec","why":"Science-backed video on broccoli sprout compound benefits for cancer.","quote":"Here is a science-backed video talking about the benefits of a substance found in broccoli sprouts w.r.t. cancer.","text":"Here is a science-backed video talking about the benefits of a substance found in broccoli sprouts w.r.t. cancer. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30460858,"author":"loudtieblahblah","month":"2022-02","story":"Soybean oil affects hypothalamus, causes genetic changes in mice: study (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There's 2 ways to look at protein. There's a life-span way and a health-span way. Do you want to live longer? or do you want to feel and potentially look better? When it comes to protein, these two things seem to be at odds with one another. High protein is correlated with shorter lifespans. - [LINK] - [LINK] You'll notice the first link shows higher lifespan when protein intake is increased after a certain age (65). it's assumed this is largely due to the number of deaths from injuries (falls) and complications from injuries that happen to the elderly and that muscle mass at this stage is vital. But protein tends to active mTor. You want that to not be activated and you want IGF-1 to be low. These are correlated with longer lifespan. - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] And it looks like studies in humans and animal models seem to reflect correlative data for \"Blue Zone diets\" (more or less). ( [LINK] ). Most of the regions in the Blue Zones are high in veggies, low in meat, often with a bit of seafood, and virtually zero processed foods. How you define a little meat really varies when going by \"blue zones\". Some might say 4-6oz a day, some might say 1-2x a week. The low amounts of meat and obtaining incomplete proteins with more narrow amino acid profiles is beneficial for health in the long term, largely due to mTor activation being avoided or reduced. The body evolved (it seems) to an extent, to struggle for certain nutrients (but that doesn't mean do without them entirely"},{"id":16725080,"author":"JoshMnem","month":"2018-03","story":"Nicotinamide riboside effects on aging similar to caloric restriction","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A few quick ideas from my experiments are below. I'm not a doctor so these aren't recommendations -- just things that I've experimented with. It requires a change in perspective. A quote I like is, \"food is not a recreational drug.\" Food is fuel for a fermentation tank (digestive system). If you want the machine to run well, figure out what creates ideal fermentation environment. (Watch out for things like sugars and flours.) I do meal prep by steaming a lot of vegetables in advance[1] and storing them in containers in the refrigerator. Then I can quickly make a variety of meals by combining the vegetables in various ways. Legume (bean, lentil, split pea) and vegetable soups are also good to have on hand at all times. Tahini is a base for a good sauce. I think 2 tbsp will provide about 10% of daily required calcium for most people. When out of the house, I bring along some chunks of raw red cabbage and raw broccoli.[2] They are cheap, filling, and actually pretty good, especially when you're hungry. I think it's okay to skip meals when healthy food isn't available. Restaurants can also usually provide steamed, plain vegetables on request, if you're eating out with people. Drink a quart of cold water before starting breakfast and sufficient water throughout the day. If you need to reduce calories, you can make a cutoff time for eating -- like 4, 5, or 6 pm. That also provides 14-16+ hours of fasting per day to rest the digestive system. Another way to do it is to have reduced "}],"r":[["06e-PwhmSq8",1],["-f-CFQxaUY4",1],["sWN13pKVp9s",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["9nXop2lLDa4",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["aDejwCGdUV8",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["xc8epam4NyY",1],["G0Cp7DrvNLQ",1],["64cEmjtwRgw",1],["HyzD8pNlpwI",1],["Cyya23MPoAI",1],["ycPr5-27vSI",1],["mPqWstVnRjQ",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["1TKSfAkWWN0",1]]},"S-Xm7s9eGxU":{"wy":"Recommended as an accessible entry to Satie and calm ambient classical music, and mentioned as a famous influence on game soundtracks.","q":{"x":"perhaps putting a name to it will give you some appreciation for why his work is valued","i":44405710,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44405710,"author":"pcthrowaway","month":"2025-06","story":"Unheard works by Erik Satie to premiere 100 years after his death","stance":"rec","why":"Familiar Satie piece to help appreciate his work","quote":"perhaps putting a name to it will give you some appreciation for why his work is valued","text":"Certainly both, but in your question, I'm suspecting your unaware of how much of this music you're familiar with it since it lives rent-free in the general zeitgeist. For example, I suspect you'd recognize Satie's work Gymnopedie no. 1[1] and perhaps putting a name to it will give you some appreciation for why his work is valued [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14171879,"author":"coldtea","month":"2017-04","story":"Automated snare drum made to the order of Aphex Twin (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"One entry in a list of similar piano artists","quote":"","text":"Philip Glass piano works, as someone suggested are somewhat near (through more angular and less sentimental). Also: Wim Mertens: [LINK] [LINK] Max Richter: [LINK] [LINK] Yann Tiersen: [LINK] [LINK] Pascal Comelade (more childlike): [LINK] [LINK] Eric Satie: [VIDEO] Yiruma: [LINK] Harold Budd (more abstract): [LINK] [LINK] Joep Beving: [LINK] Bach: [LINK] Gabriel Yared: [LINK]"},{"id":11958635,"author":"jean_claude","month":"2016-06","story":"Ask HN: Productivity","stance":"rec","why":"Satie piano piece familiar from Minecraft","quote":"If you like classical minimalism/ambient music, check out Erik Satie's work","text":"No worries. If you like classical minimalism/ambient music, check out Erik Satie's work, specifically, his three Gymnopédies compositions for piano. If you've played Minecraft, you'll instantly recognize No.1[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35580990,"author":"cma","month":"2023-04","story":"Super Mario Bros. theme part of the 2023 Library of Congress registry","stance":"reference","why":"Claimed inspiration for Zelda Ocarina of Time opening","quote":"Zelda Ocarina of Time opening: Gymnopédie No. 1","text":"The Mario theme intro may have been lifted from here: T-Square Adventures - Sister Marian [LINK] Guile's theme, same album (from the song \"Travelers\"): [LINK] -- Underworld theme intro likely taken from here: Friendship (ft. Lee Ritenour) - Let's Not Talk About It [LINK] -- Mario star powerup music: Piper - Summer Dream [LINK] -- Mario 3 water theme/Zelda Fairy theme: Tatsuro Yamashita - Morning Glory [LINK] -- Hammer Bros. Mario 3 theme and Dr. Mario theme seem to have taken from Lady Madonna though it is also may just be a common form: [LINK] -- Mario Galaxy theme: Barbra Streisand - Evergreen (Love Theme from, \"A Star Is Born\") [LINK] -- Bob-omb Battle field: Steely Dan - Kid Charlemagne (coda) [LINK] -- Zelda 1 dungeon music: Deep Purple - April [LINK] -- Zelda Ocarina of Time opening: Gymnopédie No. 1 [VIDEO] [LINK] -- Zelda Ocarina of Time Gerudo Valley: Heavily lifted from an anime song that was metal instead of acoustic. Link appears to have gone dead but I think it is the one mentioned in here: [LINK] -- Zelda's Lullaby: Mort Garson - Concerto For Philodendron And Pothos (Plantasia) [LINK] -- This video goes over several of them: [LINK]"}],"r":[["AJdEqssNZ-U",1],["u404SLJj7ig",1],["Uo08uS904Rg",1],["mGQLXRTl3Z0",1]]},"thqbjA2DC-E":{"wy":"HN links it whenever airline route rules, cabotage or loopholes come up, as a concise background explainer.","q":{"x":"This video has a lot of good info on it","i":20952242,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20952242,"author":"graton","month":"2019-09","story":"A Low-Fare Loophole for International Travelers","stance":"rec","why":"Good info on the five freedoms of aviation.","quote":"This video has a lot of good info on it","text":"The five freedoms of aviation are: 1) The right to fly over a foreign country without landing. 2) The right to refuel or carry out maintenance in a foreign country without embarking or disembarking passengers or cargo. 3) The right to fly from one's own country to another country. 4) The right to fly from another country to one's own. 5) The right to fly between two foreign countries on a flight originating or ending in one's own country. This video has a lot of good info on it: [VIDEO] And wikipedia page: [LINK]"},{"id":19378587,"author":"lagadu","month":"2019-03","story":"US refuses to ground Boeing 737 Max","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how Norwegian operates across the EU and US.","quote":"Check out this video about it","text":"Norwegian is technically an EU airline because despite the name they're also headquartered in EU countries. This is so because being EU-based they're free to use all EU airports to base flights out of, which they use to good extent to make flights between the US and the French Caribbean islands, something that would be impossible had they been solely HQd in Norway :) Check out this video about it (Norwegian starts at about 10 minutes in): [VIDEO] edit: turns out they're registed in several countries, not just Denmark."},{"id":16052173,"author":"crikli","month":"2018-01","story":"A personal ode to the Boeing 747","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the politics of aviation and five freedoms.","quote":"from my new favorite YouTube channel, Wendover Productions","text":"I was rolling my eyes at the subtitle. The article devolved from there. Some great (sometimes indirect) information on why the 747 and to a lesser extent the A380 are being phased out, from my new favorite YouTube channel, Wendover Productions: The Economics of Long-Haul Flights: [LINK] ETOPS: Explained [LINK] Politics of Aviation / 5 Freedoms of Aviation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37738861,"author":"laverya","month":"2023-10","story":"Major airline faces backlash after using ghost flights to exploit legal loophole","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant explanation of international and domestic flight rules.","quote":"There's a Wendover video from years ago that's relevant","text":"You can't legally sell domestic flights in a foreign country. (for these purposes, the EU is one country) This is true for Australia, for India, for the USA, for the EU - an airline like Delta can fly to Sydney from LA but not sell domestic flights from Sydney to Melbourne. (Or, for that matter, international flights between Sydney and Singapore) However, there is a catch! Airliners did not used to have quite the range they do today, and so the treaties allow things like refueling stops. And they (sometimes!) allow passengers to embark/disembark at those stops - but those passengers still have to be flying the international leg, or else it would be a domestic flight. There's a Wendover video from years ago that's relevant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19144270,"author":"thomaslangston","month":"2019-02","story":"Lufthansa Sues Hidden City Ticketholder for Throwing His Ticket","stance":"reference","why":"Background on route agreements between countries.","quote":"Some background on the subject","text":"My guess is this might be similar to how company's sue people for trademark violations, even when they are not doing any direct damage to the brand i.e. to protect themselves from future legal liability. If too many customers use the hidden city in a foreign airline flight, they might be found not be in compliance with route agreements. Some background on the subject: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NlIdzF1_b5M",1],["HSxSgbNQi-g",1],["QLUIExAnNcE",1]]},"QAr-uHd8h8o":{"wy":"Cited in climbing knot threads to counter the claim that figure 8s jam, by showing the correct tying method.","q":{"x":"Apparently there is a wrong way of tying a figure 8, that leads to a version that is much harder to untie.","i":26471062,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":32412021,"author":"imurray","month":"2022-08","story":"Essential Climbing Knots","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Experiments showing two look-alike figure 8 variants differ in untying.","quote":"This is a great video.","text":"This is a great video. A summary for those who don't want to wade through it: The main message is that if the knot is slightly loose, or messy, the strands pinch unevenly in a fall, and it can be really hard to untie (depending on the rope). Most of the video is experiments to show that. The knot only had to be slightly loose to cause problems. The part that surprised me is that there are two different ways to tie the knot that basically look the same. They only differ in which of the two outgoing strands is the one that's loaded. They're both perfectly safe, but one can be a lot harder to untie than the other after a fall. I actually find it hard to end up with the \"wrong\" version of the knot, but I might just be lucky with what my muscle memory has latched on to. I certainly wasn't taught to watch out for the difference. The difference between the two knots is shown at 13m 24s: [VIDEO] His demo of one way to get the knot the right way around is at 7m 41s of another long video: [LINK]"},{"id":26471062,"author":"roessland","month":"2021-03","story":"Learn How to Tie Knots","stance":"rec","why":"Shows a wrong way of tying a figure 8 that is hard to untie.","quote":"Apparently there is a wrong way of tying a figure 8, that leads to a version that is much harder to untie.","text":"Apparently there is a wrong way of tying a figure 8, that leads to a version that is much harder to untie. Personally I've never had issues untying a figure 8, so it might just be that my default way of tying it happened to be the correct way. It's described in this video: [VIDEO] Skip to 13:20 for the interesting part."},{"id":32408859,"author":"panzerklein","month":"2022-08","story":"Essential Climbing Knots","stance":"reference","why":"Tying the figure 8 correctly makes it easy to untie.","quote":"Figure 8 knot is not that hard to untie after a fall, you just have to tie it in a particular way","text":"Figure 8 knot is not that hard to untie after a fall, you just have to tie it in a particular way [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30641269,"author":"kerkeslager","month":"2022-03","story":"The Most Useful Rope Knots for the Average Person to Know","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A few adjustments from a rock climber and camper here: 1. When joining identical ropes, the reef knot is adequate for any situation where the sheet bend is adequate. The rolling over and jamming under load described above usually happens when joining different ropes. Deconstructed, the sheet bend consists of a loop in one rope and a bight in the other: you want the loop to be in the 1) thinner or 2) higher-friction of the two cords (in that order of priority). There doesn't seem to be any downside to using the sheet bend over the reef knot in identical ropes (besides perhaps complexity) so I tend to agree with the implication above that the sheet bend is the superior knot. Neither knot is recommended for life support applications. 2. The bowline coming undone under non-continuous load is very real and has resulted in some climber deaths. However, I think the half hitch addition suffers from the same issue--it merely takes longer to come undone this way. A more reliable finishing method is to tie a double overhand in the tail either around the loop (see [1]) or around the standing end (this is common and effective but not my preference for purely aesthetic reasons). The double overhand binds under load, but load will be intercepted by the bowline's turn and the overhand should never come under load. A double bowline will extend the life of your rope by widening the bend that comes under pressure, but isn't appreciably safer in adequately strong ropes. 3. The clove hitch coming"}],"r":[["JxBg4sMusIg",1]]},"tF4DML7FIWk":{"wy":"HN uses it as the benchmark for state-of-the-art humanoid robot mobility in discussions of Tesla Bot and robotics progress.","m":[{"id":33911523,"author":"SXX","month":"2022-12","story":"Xiaomi’s Humanoid Drummer Beats Expectations","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Latest Atlas demo looks like science fiction.","quote":"it look like sci-fi to me","text":"I can say I'm much impressed. At the same time I haven't watched Boston Dynamics videos for last couple of years. When I checked latest Atlas demo from 2021 it look like sci-fi to me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28242963,"author":"sergiotapia","month":"2021-08","story":"Tesla Announces a Humanoid Robot “Tesla Bot”","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how far robot movement has come, so advanced it seems fake.","quote":"Just ridiculous how far things have come, it seems fake it's so far along.","text":"We said the same thing about movement in robots -- and today we have: [VIDEO] Just ridiculous how far things have come, it seems fake it's so far along."},{"id":44912783,"author":"kohsuke","month":"2025-08","story":"AI-induced dehumanization (2024)","stance":"reference","why":"Robot shown with low socio-emotional capability in a dehumanization study.","quote":"that highlights the low socio-emotional capabilities of a robot","text":"So they run 5 different experiments to test the hypothesis, and they were nothing like what I imagined. For example, in one study, they divide participants into two groups, have one group watch [LINK] (that highlights the high socio-emotional capabilities of a robot), while the other watches [VIDEO] (that highlights the low socio-emotional capabilities of a robot) They are then asked if they agree or disagree with a (presumably hypothetical?) company's proposal to reduce employees' welfare, such as replacing a meal with a shake. Two groups showed a different preference. This makes me think about that old question of whether you thank LLM or not. That is treating LLMs more like humans, so if what this paper found holds, maybe that'd nudge our brain subtly toward dehumanizing other real humans!? That's so counter intuitive..."},{"id":41847105,"author":"ahiknsr","month":"2024-10","story":"The Retreat to Muskworld","stance":"reference","why":"Boston Dynamics robot dexterity demo, older than Tesla's robots.","quote":"Tesla's robots don't have as much dexterity as the robot from the video","text":"> If anyone is going to make self driving work, the odds are stacked in Tesla's favor. How disconnected from reality are you to make this statement? [LINK] Waymo self-driving has been open to general public since June 2024 and Telsa still doesn't have full self driving technology and yet here you are claiming odds are stacked in Tesla's favor. > I feel similarly excited for Tesla when they demonstrate robots with incredible dexterity. Did you never hear about Boston dynamics? [VIDEO] This is 3 years old video and Tesla's robots don't have as much dexterity as the robot from the video. And unlike Tesla's robot, Boston Dynamics robot is fully automated."},{"id":37828016,"author":"johnthesecure","month":"2023-10","story":"Extreme Parkour with Legged Robots","stance":"reference","why":"Atlas robot doing parkour and mid-air somersaults.","quote":"the Boston Dynamics Atlas could do mid-air somersaults","text":"Two years ago, the Boston Dynamics Atlas could do mid-air somersaults - that's better than most 10-year-olds. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36119802,"author":"rglover","month":"2023-05","story":"Paper Money Diehards Refuse to Fold","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Not yet [1]. Total symbiosis of corporations and government—to the point of making Mussolini blush—is the ultimate goal. Sure, that sounds paranoid, but look at how far we've come on that spectrum in the last 30 years. Proliferation of technology (due to its enabling of surveillance and weakening of the state) all but guarantees that future. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34496397,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: How long until ChatGPT is given a robot arm?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of existing walking robots, expensive and not humanlike.","quote":"There are already walking robots but they are very expensive prototypes and look nothing like humans.","text":"May we finally have walking humanoids robots in our lifetime? I think people are already getting ideas [1] and I am looking forward to everything that follows. From a safe distance There are already walking robots but they are very expensive prototypes and look nothing like humans. [2] Given how many things ChatGPT confidently gets wrong I am not sure how that would play out in a robot. [1] - [LINK] [video][movie trailer] [2] - [VIDEO] [video][1 min]"},{"id":31360156,"author":"edouard-harris","month":"2022-05","story":"DeepMind: A Generalist Agent","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of robotic parkour progress.","quote":"","text":"That Tim Urban piece is great. It's also an interesting time capsule in terms of which AI problems were and were not considered hard in 2015 (when the post was written). From the post: > Build a computer that can multiply two ten-digit numbers in a split second—incredibly easy. Build one that can look at a dog and answer whether it’s a dog or a cat—spectacularly difficult. Make AI that can beat any human in chess? Done. Make one that can read a paragraph from a six-year-old’s picture book and not just recognize the words but understand the meaning of them? Google is currently spending billions of dollars trying to do it. Hard things—like calculus, financial market strategy, and language translation—are mind-numbingly easy for a computer, while easy things—like vision, motion, movement, and perception—are insanely hard for it. The children's picture book problem is solved; those billions of dollars were well-spent after all. (See, e.g., DeepMind's recent Flamingo model [1].) We can do whatever we want in vision, more or less [2]. Motion and movement might be the least developed area, but it's still made major progress; we have robotic parkour [3] and physical Rubik's cube solvers [4], and we can tell a robot to follow simple domestic instructions [5]. And Perceiver (again from DeepMind [6]) took a big chunk out of the perception problem. Getting a computer to carry on a conversation [7], let alone draw art on par with human professionals [8], weren't even mentioned as examples"},{"id":29574886,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2021-12","story":"Research Notes on Humanoid Robots","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Boston Dynamics doing quite well in humanoid robots.","quote":"Meanwhile Boston Dynamics is doing quite well.","text":"The robot I am most personally impressed with is Agility Robotics Digit. It is a real human like form factor (small human not huge!). If I recall correctly it is an energy efficient walking system too. [LINK] This long continuous shot of walking from 2019 is nice too. They have improved their software a lot but the hardware was already in good shape. [LINK] Meanwhile Boston Dynamics is doing quite well. [VIDEO] Best path to success is Tesla buys Agility Robotics, revs the design for Digit to integrate the Tesla AI system, and spins up a manufacturing line. They could focus immediately on the high level AI problem rather than worrying about design. Could slap their AI system on digit while mechanical teams rev the new design so they get moving quickly. Disclosure: I do not work for any of these companies or any related company. I am making a farming robot (see my profile)."},{"id":29574965,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2021-12","story":"Research Notes on Humanoid Robots","stance":"reference","why":"State of the art Atlas, not using a static walk.","quote":"Neither of those robots use a static walk.","text":"I agree with the neighbor comment, 2015 was a long time ago. See the state of the art today: Agility Robotics Digit: [LINK] Boston Dynamics Atlas: [VIDEO] Neither of those robots use a static walk. That is obvious enough in the above Boston Dynamics video, but Agility Robotics also just released a little video explaining their (very robust) dynamic walking system: [LINK] It seems clear the basic hardware to do humanoid walking is technologically achievable today. Personally I would think Tesla would be better suited by copying or buying Agility Robotics as Digit is a smaller robot and it looks cheaper than Atlas too. Better for working in a warehouse alongside people as shown in the top video link in this post, and they seem a smaller company than BD and so cheaper to buy. Musk is very happy to buy firms that produce tech his companies need. As far as the software problem, it is a big one to be sure. I don't think they have all the pieces to the puzzle, but I do think Tesla's Dojo AI system has promise for training a machine like a humanoid. Their system is designed to ingest experience and produce neural nets which can navigate in the real world. They may be willing to take a bet, despite the clear risks, on using this system for humanoids. If they buy Agility Robotics, rev the design to add their AI system (which is extremely powerful AI hardware with their own custom processor), they could be in production on prototypes within a year. This all has the disclaimer that succe"},{"id":28958439,"author":"goldenkey","month":"2021-10","story":"Artificial muscles robotic arm with full range of motion [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an example of what Boston Dynamics achieved.","quote":"","text":"Reusable rockets weren't practical either. But someone was maverick enough to attempt it anyways. [1] You provide valuable information but I just hate how you succumb to easy thinking, an appeal to tradition [2]: \"well it hadn't been done then so it can't possibly be done now.\" It's a form of anachronistic gatekeeping. It doesn't belong in the age we currently reside in. We have totally different computational abilities today, powerful beyond reason. Our hardware is smaller and better performing. We can perform digital simulations billions of times before we even execute one physical experiment. I've seen the same attitude with age-old mathematics problems and it makes even less sense in that domain. The ability to perform computational experiments that our predecessors couldn't, changes the viability of almost every unsolved problem. Boston Dynamics would have never created Atlas or Spot if they clung to common tropes about robots. And now, due to battery technology, the once tethered robots are dexterously mobile. [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28211289,"author":"retSava","month":"2021-08","story":"Atlas robot does parkour","stance":"reference","why":"Video accompanying blog post; stutters show it is not fully scripted.","quote":"I like to see when they stutter or slightly-fail","text":"This is the video with the blog post: [VIDEO] To me, I like to see when they stutter or slightly-fail, since that to indicates the dynamics and real-time part of the thing, that it is not a 100% scripted thing (although the blog post mentions the engineers fine-tuning the celebratory arm-pump, to what extent is that scripted then?)"},{"id":28212189,"author":"rkagerer","month":"2021-08","story":"Atlas robot does parkour","stance":"reference","why":"Question about dripping fluid and foam landing surfaces in the footage.","quote":"","text":"What's that dripping from the right robot's right thigh near the end? [VIDEO] Also are the layers of foam over the landing surfaces they backflip onto needed for dampening?"},{"id":28255373,"author":"chronic2703","month":"2021-08","story":"An inside look at the custom CPUs in Tesla's Dojo Supercomputer","stance":"reference","why":"Impressive Boston Dynamics release that took spotlight from Tesla.","quote":"Boston Dynamics released an impressive video this week [1], which took the spotlight away from Tesla.","text":"> i cant stop thinking about the tesla bot. was he he outright lying? Elon is jealous of Boston Dynamics. Boston Dynamics released an impressive video this week [1], which took the spotlight away from Tesla. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RIctzAQOe44",1],["GWQh_DmDLKQ",1]]},"xR7XMkjDGw0":{"wy":"Shared in productivity and writing threads for King's simple daily-quota approach to producing a lot of work.","q":{"x":"One of my favorite videos is when George RR Martin asks Stephen King on stage","i":34089924,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34089924,"author":"komali2","month":"2022-12","story":"Show HN: Write 500 Words a Day","stance":"strong_rec","why":"King describes breaking a book into daily page goals and focused hours.","quote":"One of my favorite videos is when George RR Martin asks Stephen King on stage","text":"One of my favorite videos is when George RR Martin asks Stephen King on stage, \"How the fuck do you write so many books so fast?\" [VIDEO] Stephen King answers basically with the same advice he's always given, as you said, he tries to write 6 pages a day. In the video, he says he tries to get 3-4 hours of work a day, and get to that 6 pages. He breaks it down, \"if the manuscript is x length, it will take y months to finish.\" George RR Martin goes on to ask essentially, \"but what if writer's block? What if internet distraction? What if something comes up?\" He goes on to tell a story about Rowling that basically resolves with \"nobody really understands what writers do,\" but based on King's own \"On Writing,\" I think he does claim to understand, and his tips are not really any different from how you generate content in any other medium, methods that those of us terminally online HN types are at this point experts via headlines alone, we see so many posts on the subject. The method is demonstrated in the \"6 pages\" quote that gets thrown around: define the objective (write a book), break the objective into estimable and achievable goals (write 6 pages a day: this allows for book length x to be finished in y months), and set dedicated, distraction-free time to achieve the day's goal (as I recall from \"On Writing,\" King has a dedicated writing office and doesn't bring his phone into it, and doesn't have internet access in there. I may be remembering incorrectly or mixing up my absurd "},{"id":19363768,"author":"nybblesio","month":"2019-03","story":"Ask HN: How do I 3x my performance?","stance":"rec","why":"King explains his daily six-pages writing routine.","quote":"I love what Stephen King says on this topic: \"I try to get six pages a day.\" [1]","text":"I love what Stephen King says on this topic: \"I try to get six pages a day.\" [1] He works 3 to 4 hours per day, every day. He most likely works the same time block every day. As he says in the video, life happens. Don't stress life happening. Attend to things that come up and return to your \"six pages a day\". The number of pages isn't important. King even qualifies his answer by saying, \"...if things go well.\" The key component here is: - every day; - preferably same time block each day; - concentrated effort; - attainable goal with a bit of a stretch each day. I adopted this model several years ago and my productivity went through the roof. (From my own perspective, measuring against myself; not others.) I can say I'm overjoyed at how much work I can get done using this approach. Five hours each day, starting at 4am, is the sweet spot for me. Note, though, that I wake up at 2am to be ready by 4am and I go to bed, uh, about now. Here's the rub: it will always appear that you can do more. However, as others have said here, it's an illusion. Finally, I would make the following observation: if one feels secure and has a stable environment, productivity and motivation don't seem to be elusive. If you're chasing after dollars and you're about to go bust if-it-doesn't-get-done-by-this-Friday , you've already lost. It's been my experience that you cannot start from a foundation of anxiety. It can't be \"I have to do this or I'm screwed\". I realize this creates a paradox for many folk"},{"id":46286020,"author":"theshrike79","month":"2025-12","story":"Ask HN: How can I get better at using AI for programming?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It's not fully apocryphal, there's video of it","text":"It's not fully apocryphal, there's video of it: [VIDEO] - not those exact words, but the gist of it is there. (Full video here: [LINK] )"},{"id":17307920,"author":"Reedx","month":"2018-06","story":"Stephen King: Can a Novelist Be Too Productive? (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of when George RR Martin asked King, \"How the fuck do you write so many books so fast?\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bC-8y2R6IxI",1],["8An2SxNFvmU",1]]},"05WS0WN7zMQ":{"wy":"It is the standard reference whenever a thread turns to rote labels versus real understanding.","q":{"x":"Feynman also knows the difference \"between knowing the name of something and knowing something\"","i":1479180,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":4967928,"author":"ars","month":"2012-12","story":"Half the Facts You Know Are Probably Wrong","stance":"rec","why":"Learn the thing itself, not its name","quote":"Learn the thing itself, but the name tells you very little. Watch this","text":"> Is this something I should try to avoid with my son, or should I encourage it Avoid. I would avoid spending time learning the names of things. Learn the thing itself, but the name tells you very little. Watch this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1479180,"author":"ckuehne","month":"2010-07","story":"Where does a tree get its mass?","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman on knowing names versus knowing things","quote":"Feynman also knows the difference \"between knowing the name of something and knowing something\"","text":"Can't upvote you enough for this. By the way, Feynman also knows the difference \"between knowing the name of something and knowing something\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":8148377,"author":"bane","month":"2014-08","story":"Questions about Nasa's space drive answered","stance":"reference","why":"Models describe things but are not understanding","quote":"people often confuse understanding the model with understanding the thing.","text":"My point is that people often confuse understanding the model with understanding the thing. The model becomes a ritual, it becomes a kind of complex dogma, and when people master the model they claim they understand it. But it's not true, they don't understand the thing, they only understand our description of it. Feynman also addresses this, [VIDEO] The next Monday, when the fathers were all back at work, we kids were playing in a field. One kid says to me, “See that bird? What kind of bird is that?” I said, “I haven’t the slightest idea what kind of a bird it is.” He says, “It’s a brown-throated thrush. Your father doesn’t teach you anything!” But it was the opposite. He had already taught me: “See that bird?” he says. “It’s a Spencer’s warbler.” (I knew he didn’t know the real name.) “Well, in Italian, it’s a Chutto Lapittida. In Portuguese, it’s a Bom da Peida. In Chinese, it’s a Chung-long-tah, and in Japanese, it’s a Katano Tekeda. You can know the name of that bird in all the languages of the world, but when you’re finished, you’ll know absolutely nothing whatever about the bird. You’ll only know about humans in different places, and what they call the bird. So let’s look at the bird and see what it’s doing—that’s what counts.” (I learned very early the difference between knowing the name of something and knowing something.) Models are just a complicated way of calling something, not a way of understanding it. Getting experimental results just means you've used the cor"},{"id":7993499,"author":"clusterfoo","month":"2014-07","story":"The Last Days of the Polymath (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Distinguishing knowing something from knowing its name","quote":"Reminds me of this bit by Feynman on the difference between knowing something, and knowing the name of that thing","text":"Reminds me of this bit by Feynman on the difference between knowing something, and knowing the name of that thing: [VIDEO] There does seem to exist a particular breed of \"intellectual\" who not only confuses the two, but seemingly believes that conceptual understanding is merely an unpleasant side effect one must endure in order to achieve the ultimate goal, which is to grow one's vocabulary (and grow it beyond recognition, and, more importantly, beyond scrutiny from mere-mortals). Jargon creep is inevitable when we go expert-crazy, which, it seems to me, is more about marking territory and protecting one's clique than any legitimate concern for the advancement of knowledge. > \"It's simply no longer possible to be an expert in more than one field.\" This is a lazy straw-man argument (1). Of course, experts are necessary, and we'd only go so far before we start running in circles if not for dedicated individuals... But how does this negate the value of polymaths? This is like saying that wheels on an airplane are completely useless, because wings have been meticulously crafted for the sole purpose of flying. -- The two serve different purposes. The plane can't fly without the one, but it won't get off the ground without the other. A polymath will never acquire the depth of understanding of an expert, but neither can the expert have access to the more varied sources of inspiration / patterns of thought that a polymath acquires through breadth of experience. It seems obvious to me"},{"id":3902038,"author":"thebigshane","month":"2012-04","story":"Cargo Cults and Interview Questions","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman's bird-names anecdote","quote":"You can know the name of a bird in all the languages of the world, but when you're finished, you'll know absolutely nothing whatever about the bird...","text":"You can know the name of a bird in all the languages of the world, but when you're finished, you'll know absolutely nothing whatever about the bird... So let's look at the bird and see what it's doing — that's what counts. I learned very early the difference between knowing the name of something and knowing something. - Richard Feynman [LINK] and [VIDEO]"},{"id":2084801,"author":"bane","month":"2011-01","story":"Chinese Mothers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"(sorry if this is going to be rather reactionary and offensive) All day I've been getting emails and IM's from my East Asian friends about this article. As somebody who grew up in and around kids with parents like this I have to call hooey on it. All of my East Asian friends were miserable, tired, sad kids. A couple committed suicide, most had health problems, alcoholism, severe depression, drug additions, and the rest are today broken people on the inside who can't get through a simple phone call with their parents without breaking into sobbing tears after hanging up. All of them have spoken outwardly and quite vocally a wish for a Western upbringing, one that fostered creativity and the genesis of ideas - not conformance to a meaningless system of symbolic but useless achievements that have provided nothing in particular for their lives except the ability to get their parents to back off for a few minutes. Where this woman makes a mistake is in assuming that outward, public success (yes, all of them work good jobs, and have fine educations) is the most important thing in life. The question she has never thought to ask herself is \"so what?\" So what if little Xiao Yan or Jin Ho or Akiko can perform some dastardly complex piece on the violin on command? Congratulations, you've raised a music box. Can they write music of the same complexity? Can they create? Or was all of that destroyed when their soul was crushed just so they could perform that one more Caprice? So what if the"},{"id":8078297,"author":"mreiland","month":"2014-07","story":"How GoG.com is growing beyond a back catalog","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"and IBM is still supporting software and hardware from 40+ years ago, are they a back catalog company too? You're trying to redefine words to mean what you want them to mean, I hope you don't approach technical decisions in the same manner. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ALc8CBYOfkw",1],["5nW3nJhBHL0",1],["zcqZHYo7ONs",1]]},"4moyKUHApq4":{"wy":"Frequently cited for the claim that Photoshop's codebase could shrink dramatically with generic programming.","q":{"x":"Watch this Sean Parent video","i":14902789,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14902789,"author":"adamnemecek","month":"2017-08","story":"App sizes are out of control","stance":"rec","why":"Sean Parent on how generics could shrink Photoshop's codebase.","quote":"Watch this Sean Parent video","text":"They make code reuse possible. Watch this Sean Parent video [VIDEO] where he estimates that if Photoshop was rewritten using generics, the code size would go from 3,000,000 LOC to 30,000 LOC. You are right, during compilation, generics are specialized so you end up with code, however going all in on generics removes a lot of accidental complexity."},{"id":6415077,"author":"wwweston","month":"2013-09","story":"Edward C++Hands","stance":"rec","why":"Introduced Stepanov's Elements of Programming and better abstractions in C++.","quote":"which introduced me to Stepanov's \"Elements of Programming","text":"\"Ask any C++ guru and they will tell you: avoid mutation, avoid side effects, don’t use loops, avoid class hierarchies and inheritance. But you will need strict discipline and total control over your collaborators to pull that off because C++ is so permissive. \"Haskell is not permissive, it won’t let you — or your coworkers — write unsafe code. Yes, initially you’ll be scratching your head trying to implement something in Haskell that you could hack in C++ in 10 minutes. If you’re lucky, and you work for Sean Parent or other exceptional programmer, he will code review your hacks and show you how not to program in C++.\" For those who aren't familiar with Parent, watch this talk: [VIDEO] which introduced me to Stepanov's \"Elements of Programming\" and the idea that I could be working with better/more reliable/provable abstractions in C++. That said... after a few weeks of shallowly digesting the material, I started thinking pretty much like Milewski says above: it's great if I can learn to write better C++, but if what I really want is to escape from its specific dangers and general common programming pitfalls, something like Haskell seems like a better bet."},{"id":20441869,"author":"adamnemecek","month":"2019-07","story":"Elements of Programming (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Photoshop code size estimate from the book's ideas.","quote":"estimated that if PS codebase followed ideas from this book, the codebase could be reduced from 3,000,000 LOC to 30,000 LOC.","text":"This book is really good, really dense but the ideas have inspired a generation. In this video, Sean Parent, at the time working at Adobe on Photoshop, estimated that if PS codebase followed ideas from this book, the codebase could be reduced from 3,000,000 LOC to 30,000 LOC. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13407627,"author":"adamnemecek","month":"2017-01","story":"Ask HN: What's the best computer science book you've read recently?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in book recommendation for Photoshop code reduction estimate.","quote":"estimated that the PS codebase could be reduced from 3,000,000 LOC to 30,000 LOC (=100x!!)","text":"I'll give you a couple. Note that some of these are rehashes of my earlier comments. # Elements of Programming [LINK] This book proposes how to write C++-ish code in a mathematical way that makes all your code terse. In this talk, Sean Parent, at that time working on Adobe Photoshop, estimated that the PS codebase could be reduced from 3,000,000 LOC to 30,000 LOC (=100x!!) if they followed ideas from the book [VIDEO] Another point of his is that the explosion of written code we are seeing isn't sustainable and that so much of this code is algorithms or data structures with overlapping functionalities. As the codebases grow, and these functionalities diverge even further, pulling the reigns in on the chaos becomes gradually impossible. Bjarne Stroustrup (aka the C++ OG) gave this book five stars on Amazon (in what is his one and only Amazon product review lol). This style might become dominant because it's only really possible in modern successors of C++ such as Swift or Rust, not so much in C++ itself. [LINK] # Grammar of graphics [LINK] This book changed my perception of creativity, aesthetics and mathematics and their relationships. Fundamentally, the book provides all the diverse tools to give you confidence that your graphics are mathematically sound and visually pleasing. After reading this, Tufte just doesn't cut it anymore. It's such a weird book because it talks about topics as disparate Bayesian rule, OOP, color theory, SQL, chaotic models of time (lolwut), style-she"},{"id":13430139,"author":"adamnemecek","month":"2017-01","story":"Ask HN: Are we overcomplicating software development?","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that we write too much code.","quote":"estimated that the PS codebase could be reduced from 3,000,000 LOC to 30,000 LOC (=100x!!)","text":"There's this book that I've been mentioning around here called Elements of Programming [LINK] that makes exactly this claim, that we are writing too much code. It proposes how to write C++-ish (it's an extremely minimal subset of C++ proper) code in a mathematical way that makes all your code terse. In this talk, Sean Parent, at that time working on Adobe Photoshop, estimated that the PS codebase could be reduced from 3,000,000 LOC to 30,000 LOC (=100x!!) if they followed ideas from the book [VIDEO] Another point of his is that the explosion of written code we are seeing isn't sustainable and that so much of this code is algorithms or data structures with overlapping functionalities. As the codebases grow, and these functionalities diverge even further, pulling the reigns in on the chaos becomes gradually impossible. Bjarne Stroustrup (aka the C++ OG) gave this book five stars on Amazon (in what is his one and only Amazon product review lol). [LINK] This style might become dominant because it's only really possible in modern successors of C++ such as Swift or Rust that have both \"direct\" access to memory and type classes/traits/protocols, not so much in C++ itself (unless debugging C++ template errors is your thing)."},{"id":13268479,"author":"adamnemecek","month":"2016-12","story":"What Is Abstract Algebra? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for estimate that Photoshop code could shrink 100x.","quote":"estimated that the PS codebase could be reduced from 3,000,000 LOC to 30,000 LOC (=100x!!)","text":"If you are curious how does Abstract Algebra relate to software engineering, this book might be of your interest [LINK] The main selling point of this approach is that it can cut out a lot of code In this talk, Sean Parent, at that time working on Adobe Photoshop, estimated that the PS codebase could be reduced from 3,000,000 LOC to 30,000 LOC (=100x!!) if they followed ideas from the book [VIDEO] Another point of his is that the explosion of written code we are seeing isn't sustainable and that so much of this code is algorithms or data structures with overlapping functionalities. As the codebases grow, and these functionalities diverge even further, pulling the reigns in on the chaos becomes gradually impossible. Bjarne Stroustrup (aka the C++ OG) gave this book five stars on Amazon (in what is his one and only Amazon product review lol). [LINK]"},{"id":3432517,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2012-01","story":"A Programming Idiom You've Never Heard Of ","stance":"reference","why":"Neutral citation of a Google TechTalk with timing details.","quote":"this was hosted at googletechtalks","text":"Turns out I greatly exagerated my memories (sic), he was mentionning equivalence relation using c++ concepts then went into constraint/dependant interactive input. [LINK] page 18, 38 this was hosted at googletechtalks [VIDEO] (240p)"}],"r":[["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["tX4H_ctggYo",1]]},"5P5Mk_IggE0":{"wy":"HN cites it as Oxide's own explanation of how they ship hardware as a startup and why they picked illumos.","q":{"x":"We have talked quite a bit about what it's like to be making hardware as a startup","i":36605910,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":44895027,"author":"cnst","month":"2025-08","story":"OpenIndiana: Community-Driven Illumos Distribution","stance":"rec","why":"Episode with funny vendor support stories","quote":"Great episode!","text":"> [VIDEO] Great episode! The Dell PERC \"parity issue\" \"you're the only customer having this issue\" story is hilarious, reminds me of my own experience getting support over the years as a non-Windows user! Sadly, today, \"non-Windows\" is simply being replaced with \"non-Linux\", so, if you run BSD or Illumos, it's still the same issues. Oxide Computer is great, I really do hope they succeed, because it does seem like an uphill battle!"},{"id":36808237,"author":"faitswulff","month":"2023-07","story":"Oxide Computer, an efficient rack-scale computing system","stance":"rec","why":"Discussion of rack bootstrap time versus traditional setup","quote":"Pretty fascinating stuff.","text":"One benefit is that they're competing in an industry where the time to get a sled up and running for compute can be measured in weeks (they say they've heard up to 90 days) whereas their solution is basically plug and play - Bryan was trying to get Steve to admit set up took \"hours\" whereas Steve was hedging and saying customers could get started \"within a week.\" They go into more details on their podcast, and this section in particular covers the bootstrap time: [VIDEO] Pretty fascinating stuff."},{"id":44894816,"author":"ubedan","month":"2025-08","story":"OpenIndiana: Community-Driven Illumos Distribution","stance":"reference","why":"Oxide's reasoning for choosing illumos","quote":"They published their reasoning for choosing illumos here","text":"\"There’s a big difference between mostly dead and all dead. ... Now, mostly dead is slightly alive. Now, all dead…well, with all dead, there’s usually only one thing that you can do.\" I personally believe that illumos will survive for decades, and it very well could rise again for those users that want/need robust stability. One particularly notable design win was Oxide Computer Company as their hypervisor. They published their reasoning for choosing illumos here: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39180644,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2024-01","story":"Helios: A distribution of Illumos powering the Oxide Rack","stance":"reference","why":"Q&A explaining why an illumos derivative was chosen","quote":"we expanded on this a bit in our Q&A when we shipped our first rack[1]","text":"Keep in mind that Helios is really just an implementation detail of the rack; like Hubris[0], it's not something visible to the user or to applications. (The user of the rack provisions VMs.) As for why an illumos derivative and not something else, we expanded on this a bit in our Q&A when we shipped our first rack[1] -- and we will expand on it again in the (recorded) discussion that we will have later today.[2] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":36605910,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2023-07","story":"Ask HN: Any Hardware Startups Here?","stance":"reference","why":"Q&A about making hardware as a startup","quote":"We have talked quite a bit about what it's like to be making hardware as a startup","text":"We have talked quite a bit about what it's like to be making hardware as a startup in our Oxide and Friends podcast[0] -- and one might be especially interested in the Q&A we did on Monday[1] after our launch got quite a bit of attention here on HN.[2] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":36852642,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2023-07","story":"The IBM mainframe: How it runs and why it survives","stance":"reference","why":"Author's own VC pitch analogy to AS/400","quote":"I likened us to the AS/400 in a VC pitch[0]","text":"Yes! There are lots of ways in which we are nothing like these big IBM systems (we are, for example, entirely open -- like POWER, but very unlike AS/400 or Z series machines), but we definitely admire the robustness of these machines! Indeed, arguably to a fault, as I likened us to the AS/400 in a VC pitch[0] -- which (despite the potential accuracy of the historical analogue) is as ill-advised as it sounds... [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36898214,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2023-07","story":"A Day in the Life of an IPv6 Packet on Illumos","stance":"reference","why":"Background on the choice of illumos","quote":"it was certainly not the foregone conclusion that you might expect![0]","text":"Not unrelated, of course -- but it was certainly not the foregone conclusion that you might expect![0] [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ooNzRrHA9VY",1],["9QMGAtxUlAc",1],["aKLntZcp27M",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1]]},"9qi03QawZEk":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a detailed look at battery degradation, in threads on EV and grid storage lifetimes.","q":{"x":"This lecture (1h, 13m) goes into it in detail, including how it can be prevented, ways of testing, and more","i":9484923,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":15144734,"author":"RoboTeddy","month":"2017-08","story":"Tesla Model S battery degradation data","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth look at battery degradation by Jeff Dahn","quote":"Battery science is fascinating. Here's in an-depth look at degredation","text":"Battery science is fascinating. Here's in an-depth look at degredation: [VIDEO] The speaker (Jeff Dahn -- he now works with Tesla) pioneered a faster way to tell how fast a battery will degrade (which means you can iterate your design faster!)"},{"id":9484923,"author":"will_hughes","month":"2015-05","story":"Tesla battery storage will accelerate exit of coal generators","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed lecture on why lithium batteries degrade, prevention and testing","quote":"This lecture (1h, 13m) goes into it in detail, including how it can be prevented, ways of testing, and more","text":"It's a chemical reaction - there's a gradual degradation in the anode/cathode as a reaction with the electrolyte. There's environmental factors to it too - temperature has an effect, and if you can control the temperature over time, you can reduce the effect. This lecture (1h, 13m) goes into it in detail, including how it can be prevented, ways of testing, and more: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14449587,"author":"monkmartinez","month":"2017-05","story":"Electric Cars Soon Will Cost Less Than Gas Cars, Research Suggests","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture on why lithium batteries die","quote":"Why do Lithium batteries die? By Professor Jeff Dahn - WIN Seminar Series[0]","text":"Are you sure? Super fast discharge and charge cycles are only part of the story. I have seen that the Nissan Leaf battery problems stem from heat, choosing a poor mixture of electrolyte and additives in the Lithium. Why do Lithium batteries die? By Professor Jeff Dahn - WIN Seminar Series[0] [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"L2IKCdnzl5k":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it in EV and emissions debates for its numbers on when replacing a car pays off.","q":{"x":"Skip to 6 minutes in the video for when he starts showing compiled data of when its better to buy a new car vs keep old.","i":28633767,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28905650,"author":"widdershins","month":"2021-10","story":"The race to grab all the UK’s lithium before it’s too late","stance":"rec","why":"Provides numbers showing a new EV pays back its footprint in about five years.","quote":"the linked video provides lots of numbers","text":"A video I watched recently comes to a very different conclusion on almost every point you raised: that buying a new electric car is better for the environment within ~5 years of use, taking into account the 'new car' footprint. I'm not an expert, so I won't claim to know who's correct. But the linked video provides lots of numbers, and you didn't, so I lean in favour of that conclusion. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28633767,"author":"farisjarrah","month":"2021-09","story":"Tesla pushes U.S. to boost fuel economy penalties","stance":"rec","why":"Compiled data on when buying a new car beats keeping an old one.","quote":"Skip to 6 minutes in the video for when he starts showing compiled data of when its better to buy a new car vs keep old.","text":"Engineering Explained youtube channel tackled this question: [VIDEO] The verdict appears to be that the answer to this question is a bit more complicated then a blanket \"Its better to buy a new car for the environment\" but its highly dependent on gas milage and time of ownership, as well as new gas car vs new hybrid car vs new electric car. He does take into account carbon needed to build the cars too. It seems that in most cases a new EV will be better for the environment then your 10 year old honda in roughly 4 years. Skip to 6 minutes in the video for when he starts showing compiled data of when its better to buy a new car vs keep old."},{"id":44954435,"author":"cycomanic","month":"2025-08","story":"How much do electric car batteries degrade?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a new 35 MPG car emits less CO2 than keeping a 24 MPG one.","quote":"engineering explained [1] did a video about this","text":"> From an environmental perspective, I bet I could drive that truck for the rest of my natural life before I consumed more resources or exhausted more pollutants than would be required to manufacture a new one. Unless you drive much less than the avarage this is not true, engineering explained [1] did a video about this, and comparing a 24 MPG to a 35 MPG car you produce more CO2 with the 24 MPG car compared to a new 35 MPG car (including the manufacturing) considering your GMC has much worse fuel efficiency that break even point would be much earlier. This is not even considering the impact of recycling... So the best thing for the environment you could do is buy a new more fuel efficient car. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32018962,"author":"balls187","month":"2022-07","story":"9,000-pound electric Hummer shows we can’t ignore efficiency of EVs","stance":"reference","why":"Partly covers keeping current car versus buying a new EV.","quote":"Engineering Explained covered this in part","text":"I would be curious so see how the Hummer EV compares not based on national average, but in a markets where electricity is not produced via fossil fuels, and in a market where electricity is produced via fossil fuels. Engineering Explained covered this in part, in his video asking whether or not it is better for the environment to keep your current (presumably ICE) car, or buy a new EV. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29775697,"author":"djhn","month":"2022-01","story":"The Idiocy of Tiny Houses and Electric Vehicles","stance":"reference","why":"One of several videos with reasonable lifecycle estimates.","quote":"Engineering Explained has made several videos with reasonable estimates","text":"Though not a write up per se, Engineering Explained has made several videos with reasonable estimates: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":29123094,"author":"theluketaylor","month":"2021-11","story":"That Electric Car’s Not as Green as You Think","stance":"reference","why":"Concludes only a pretty efficient ICE car beats new on carbon.","quote":"Engineering Explained covered this is a recent video.","text":"Engineering Explained covered this is a recent video. tldr while it obviously depends on how much you drive, you need a pretty efficient ICE car for sticking to lower carbon overall. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28850066,"author":"eptcyka","month":"2021-10","story":"SUVs are offsetting EV car gains","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I'm mostly going off of this video","text":"The average age of cars on the road is above 10 years, if I recall correctly. This is plenty of time to rack up enough mileage to offset the carbon cost of manufacturing. Using averages, it seems like replacing a car with average MPG (25) with a car with 35 MPG, it only takes 7 years of driving to be better off CO2 wise. It takes even less for electric cars. I think most people overestimate how much CO2 is produced during manufacturing vs how much CO2 is produced by driving. I'm mostly going off of this video - [VIDEO] Moreover, I don't believe that there is a daily commute that's more than 120 miles each way, and this is well within the capabilities of even reasonably priced electric cars today. If you have a charger at your workplace, you could accommodate even longer trips, however colder weather might make a big dent in the range of most electric cars."}],"r":[["XA2WjJbmmoM",1]]},"NftT6HWFgq0":{"wy":"HN commenters reuse the capability-versus-suitability framing to discuss languages, restriction versus freedom, and tool design.","q":{"x":"Very nearly exactly what you're describing","i":36395974,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36395974,"author":"al2o3cr","month":"2023-06","story":"Ask HN: Enabling” vs. “Restricting” in Programming","stance":"rec","why":"2012 talk on enabling versus restricting technologies","quote":"Very nearly exactly what you're describing","text":"There was this talk (\"Capability vs Sustainability\") from 2012: [VIDEO] Very nearly exactly what you're describing; not sure if it's the same thing you were thinking of, or just part of a similar thought-wave at the time."},{"id":10379205,"author":"thristian","month":"2015-10","story":"Programming in a Mad Max Wasteland","stance":"rec","why":"Gary Bernhardt's capability versus suitability model of technology","quote":"See also the Gary Bernhardt talk, Capability vs. Suitability","text":"See also the Gary Bernhardt talk, Capability vs. Suitability: [VIDEO] Some technologies are radical new developments designed to give people some new capability they never had before. Other technologies are refinements of some previous capability technology, restricting the capabilities but more suitable for some particular problem-space or setting. It's not necessarily \"nobody has big ideas anymore\", it's just a natural cycle. On the other hand, if you want a less-charitable view, consider this: programming in the 1980s was dominated by ideas from C, a language developed in the 1970s. Programming in the 1990s brought the influence of Smalltalk, a language developed in the 1970s. The 2000s saw the introduction of functional programming ideas from Lisp, a language developed in the 1970s. These days, the hot new programming language features are being taken from Standard ML, a language developed in the 1970s. Someday the 1970s will be mined out, and then we can start coming up with new ideas again."},{"id":20325117,"author":"fnord123","month":"2019-07","story":"Absence of certain features in IRC considered a feature","stance":"reference","why":"Capability versus suitability model applied to IRC","quote":"This really comes back to the Capability vs. Suitability model that Gary Bernhardt has spoken about","text":"This really comes back to the Capability vs. Suitability model that Gary Bernhardt has spoken about: [VIDEO] IRC is capable. It is a box of nuts and bolts that lets us communicate and build awesome bots and so on. Bloating the protocol gives us suitability: features that are robust and can federate."},{"id":18343414,"author":"zachrose","month":"2018-10","story":"Create your own dysfunctional single-page app","stance":"reference","why":"Framework for viewing languages as capable or suitable","quote":"If you subscribe to Gary Benhardt’s Capability/Suitability theory of programming history [1]","text":"Using Elm, even a little bit, has given me a perspective on what a different language can do in the browser. If you subscribe to Gary Benhardt’s Capability/Suitability theory of programming history [1], JavaScript is the highly capable technology that can be used for any sort of architecture you can imagine, and Elm is a turn towards suitability that says, “some of those architectures are unsound or have a very low floor for quality.” You might want to abstract over DOM APIs for different reasons, but at their semantic core they are fine. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["FITJMJjASUs",1],["4PaWFYm0kEw",1]]},"ObiqJzfyACM":{"wy":"Cited for its well-known attempt to convey the size of 52 factorial.","q":{"x":"Vsauce has a fantastic animation of this too","i":11843006,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":42343951,"author":"redundantly","month":"2024-12","story":"Every V4 UUID","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how long 52! seconds would take","quote":"But it's worth watching the whole thing.","text":"The number you're thinking of is 52! (52 factorial) and how long it would take for 52! seconds to pass by. Vsauce has a great video on this, and might be where that example was taken from. [VIDEO] That example starts 16 minutes into the video: [VIDEO] But it's worth watching the whole thing."},{"id":11843006,"author":"Kortaggio","month":"2016-06","story":"To approximate 52! by hand, compute 54! and divide by 3000","stance":"rec","why":"Animation conveying the enormity of 52 factorial","quote":"Vsauce has a fantastic animation of this too","text":"Vsauce has a fantastic animation of this too: [VIDEO] Deal yourself five cards every billion years. When you finally deal yourself a royal flush, buy a lottery ticket. If the ticket wins the lottery, throw a single grain of sand into the Grand Canyon. As soon as the Grand Canyon is completely full of sand, remove one ounce of rock from Mount Everest. By the time Mount Everest is level: take a look at the clock. You'll have to do the whole thing all over again 256 more times before your timer reaches zero."},{"id":38125743,"author":"danbruc","month":"2023-11","story":"The 8000th Busy Beaver number eludes ZF set theory (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Well-known attempt to convey the size of 52!","quote":"Here [1] is a well known attempt to convey the size of 52!, the number of permutations of a deck of cards.","text":"A commonly quoted number for the number of particles in the observable universe is 10^80 give or take a few orders of magnitude an they have very roughly 10^10^10^2 permutations if you consider all particles distinguishable. The best known lower bound for BB(6, 2) seems to be 10^10^10^10^10^10^10^10^10^10^10^10^10^10^10 which is more like the number of permutations of permutations of permutations of permutations of permutations of permutations of permutations of permutations of permutations of permutations of permutations of permutations of the particles in the universe. Trying to comprehend or compare the size of BB(745) is just futile. Here [1] is a well known attempt to convey the size of 52!, the number of permutations of a deck of cards. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"S4O5voOCqAQ":{"wy":"HN uses it as a vivid illustration of how little sustained power a human body produces, even a champion's.","q":{"x":"Here's a neat video of an olympic cycle sprinter powering a toaster","i":24890083,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":33384659,"author":"toss1","month":"2022-10","story":"Mechanical Induction Cooker (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Impressive effort showing the scale gap of human power.","quote":"Anyway, worth the look","text":"Olympic cyclist working to toast a slice of bread. Insanely impressive effort by a maximally trained professional, and he's definitely toasted. This is like a cartoon technology — the general concept is right, but the scale is just waay off... Anyway, worth the look [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":24890083,"author":"michaelt","month":"2020-10","story":"Use Your Bike as a Backup to Your Backup Power Supply","stance":"rec","why":"Neat demo of a sprinter powering a toaster.","quote":"Here's a neat video of an olympic cycle sprinter powering a toaster","text":"Here's a neat video of an olympic cycle sprinter powering a toaster: [VIDEO] 1800 watt-hours, or 1.8 kilowatt-hours, is 20 cents worth of electricity. Using a cycle to power things makes for a neat demo, and might be good for educating people about electricity and suchlike, but anyone who expects to notice it on their energy bill or carbon footprint, or even for the installation to pay for itself, is off by a few orders of magnitude."},{"id":24891008,"author":"virtue3","month":"2020-10","story":"Use Your Bike as a Backup to Your Backup Power Supply","stance":"rec","why":"Intense toaster-powering sprint.","quote":"olympic (cyclist) sprinter powering a toaster! it's intense.","text":"that's not a bad sustained! Check this out: olympic (cyclist) sprinter powering a toaster! it's intense. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37727997,"author":"rzzzt","month":"2023-10","story":"MyPowerbank hacks Santander bikes so London's homeless can charge their phones","stance":"reference","why":"Vaguely recalled video of a cyclist's sustained power output.","quote":"weeeelll. I'm either thinking of a different one or completely messed up the numbers and duration","text":"There was a video a few years ago in which a professional cyclist is connected to a generator (bike included) and their continuous power output was around 100 W which they could maintain for a few hours. But it would be unreasonable to expect this amount of exertion from an untrained person. Edit: weeeelll. I'm either thinking of a different one or completely messed up the numbers and duration: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31846069,"author":"omnicognate","month":"2022-06","story":"A lightning bolt would be worth only about a nickel (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Toasting one slice takes everything the medallist has.","quote":"Olympic track cycling medallist powers toasting a slice of bread, which takes everything he's got and leaves him exhausted","text":"Olympic track cycling medallist powers toasting a slice of bread, which takes everything he's got and leaves him exhausted: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30592808,"author":"fuzzy2","month":"2022-03","story":"How to build a bike generator with control panel","stance":"reference","why":"Track cyclist powers a 700 W toaster for over a minute.","quote":"Absolutely insane.","text":"Reminds me of the toaster challenge video[1] where Robert Förstemann, German track cyclist, powers a 700 Watt toaster for over a minute on an exercise bike. Absolutely insane. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30060290,"author":"codethief","month":"2022-01","story":"Device wraps around hot surfaces, turns wasted heat to electricity","stance":"reference","why":"Related to power-generating gym bikes.","quote":"Very much related","text":"> I vaguely recall back in one of my engineering classes we did the math on turning all of the stationary bikes at the gym into power generating units. Very much related: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24888755,"author":"rzzzt","month":"2020-10","story":"Use Your Bike as a Backup to Your Backup Power Supply","stance":"reference","why":"Professional sustains 700W for a few minutes.","quote":"700W continuous output for a few minutes exerted by a professional","text":"700W continuous output for a few minutes exerted by a professional: [VIDEO] (For mere mortals, it will be indeed closer to 100W.)"},{"id":17300537,"author":"goldenkey","month":"2018-06","story":"Study confirms lifting weights reduces depression","stance":"reference","why":"Powerlifting cyclist as counterexample to the endurance myth.","quote":"Tell me this power-squatting cyclist lacks endurance","text":"It's a common myth that weightlifting only improves strength, not endurance. It is impossible to have strength and lack endurance. The myth comes from the unintuitive disconnect between muscular people and their actual weight - they clearly have to expend more energy to move larger, heavier body parts. So it may seem like their endurance is impaired but it isn't the case. Tell me this power-squatting cyclist lacks endurance: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13096252,"author":"ohyoutravel","month":"2016-12","story":"Sisyphus’s train set","stance":"reference","why":"Olympic cyclist powers a toaster to toast bread.","quote":"An Olympic cyclist powering a toaster to toast some bread.","text":"Contained in them. It's shocking to see the difference. I saw this video the other day which is relevant. An Olympic cyclist powering a toaster to toast some bread. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10358402,"author":"mcbain","month":"2015-10","story":"Creator of 5-hour Energy Wants to Power Homes with Bikes","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how strong an Olympic cyclist must be to toast bread.","quote":"are they as strong as Robert Förstemann (hint: no, they are not)","text":"You can ask people if they want some toast, are they as strong as Robert Förstemann (hint: no, they are not): [VIDEO]"},{"id":32512363,"author":"mrguyorama","month":"2022-08","story":"Tesla Virtual Power Plant","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory cyclist powering a toaster with their bonkers thighs","text":"Obligatory cyclist powering a toaster with their bonkers thighs: [VIDEO] IMO people often don't take the time to think about energy enough. By some quick math, I've found that the energy taken out of one 0 degree celsius ice cube to make it freeze (latent heat of fusion) is roughly equal to the energy in a ubiquitous AA battery. About 4 watt hours"}],"r":[["VYhAGnsnO7w",1],["NlIdzF1_b5M",1],["vtWp45Eewtw",1]]},"aBBuoD9eL5k":{"wy":"HN points to Geller's essays as accessible companions to literary works and as a good summary of a story.","q":{"x":"Jacob Geller provides a fun and effective summary within this video at 9 minutes in","i":44367178,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44367178,"author":"haiku2077","month":"2025-06","story":"A Mysterious Website I Stumbled Upon","stance":"rec","why":"Fun, effective summary of a story by Jacob Geller","quote":"Jacob Geller provides a fun and effective summary within this video at 9 minutes in","text":"For those who don't have time to... \"read\"... this great story, Jacob Geller provides a fun and effective summary within this video at 9 minutes in [VIDEO] In his words: \"It is in my opinion one of the best sci-fi stories ever written.\""},{"id":28776901,"author":"bsanr2","month":"2021-10","story":"Borges: The Library of Babel [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Jacob Geller video essay related to Borges' work","quote":"Jacob Geller did a few videos which mentioned this work.","text":"Video (game) essayist Jacob Geller did a few videos which mentioned this work. [LINK] [LINK] They're quite interesting, I think. The abyss really gets him going. And a few more I really like: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":38570266,"author":"cyrialize","month":"2023-12","story":"Ask HN: Books you read in 2023 and recommend for 2024","stance":"reference","why":"Video essay that introduced the commenter to Invisible Cities","quote":"I was introduced to this book by Jacob Geller's video essay","text":"\"Southern Reach Trilogy\" by Jeff VanderMeer [0]. I absolutely loved every book. \"Annihilation\" is probably my favorite. What hooked me about the series were two things: 1) It was very, /very/ weird and 2) it often times felt like the characters had no power. \"Bliss Montage\" by Ling Ma [1]. The short stories felt dreamy - drifting in perspectives, places, and times. Many of the stories had a supernatural aspect to them. \"Invisible Cities\" by Italo Calvino [2]. This book is a puzzle. You could read it front to back, or you could read it in the various different orders. There is no intended order. Again, very dreamy and weird. This book is a conversation, where Marco Polo is telling Genghis Khan of the cities within his kingdom. I was introduced to this book by Jacob Geller's video essay \"Cities without people\" [3]. \"Ring\" by Koji Suzuki [4]. One of my first horror book reads, and also the first in the Ring series. The \"Ring\" movies are based off of this book. I also read the 2nd one, which provides a very different view on the series and gives the horrors in the book a scientific backing. This year for me has been the year of strange novels. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO] [4]: [LINK]"}]},"cYsVvV1aVss":{"wy":"HN points to it as an illuminating look at what containers really are and as a video companion to running desktop apps in Docker.","q":{"x":"If you prefer video format, Jessie also has a talk at DockerCon SF 2015","i":25547739,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25547739,"author":"karlding","month":"2020-12","story":"Run More Stuff in Docker","stance":"rec","why":"Jessie Frazelle's DockerCon talk on running desktop apps in containers","quote":"If you prefer video format, Jessie also has a talk at DockerCon SF 2015","text":"The blog post links to Jessie Frazelle's GitHub repo [0]. Jessie also has a blog post about this [1] from back in 2015. If you prefer video format, Jessie also has a talk at DockerCon SF 2015 [2]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20060765,"author":"hardwaresofton","month":"2019-05","story":"Billions wasted on Hadoop startups, the same will eventually be true of Docker","stance":"rec","why":"Building containers from scratch shows they are just kernel flags","quote":"have given talks on how to make containers from scratch that are very illuminating","text":"This is precisely why I said the fact that someone would gloss over this is a red flag. The point is super critical . VMs are literally so hard to do correctly and in a performant fashion that parts of CPU instruction sets[0], and kernel subsystems (KVM[1]) were created to make them easier to run. Containers, in contrast are literally a few flags and a bunch of in-kernel antics. A few people, notably Liz Rice and Jessie Frazelle have given talks on how to make containers from scratch that are very illuminating for those that are interested: [LINK] [VIDEO] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":32201394,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2022-07","story":"How to advertise to developers: deep dive into paid developer marketing","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I really like nothing on my host... I am not going to freakin' install that","text":"Talking about marketing to devs, in the immortal words of Jess Fraz, \" I really like nothing on my host... I am not going to freakin' install that .\" (: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["B5NULPSiOGw",1],["o2HBHckrdQc",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["iukBMY4apvI",1],["sK5i-N34im8",1],["HPuvDm8IC-4",1]]},"enHF9lMseP8":{"wy":"Used as a primary source for 6502 origins, pricing strategy and Commodore history.","q":{"x":"You might also like the Computer History Museum interview w/Bill Mensch","i":27424218,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27424218,"author":"vidarh","month":"2021-06","story":"6502 “Illegal” Opcodes Demystified","stance":"rec","why":"Peddle describes 6502 origins, price targets, and sizing the chip.","quote":"From about 50 minutes in he talks about his meetings w/Motorola customers that told him the 6800 was too expensive.","text":"Most of what I know about that comes from Brian Bagnall's books (e.g. Commodore: A company on the edge). It's covered in the first chapter. I don't know of any preserved documents - if they exist they'd belong to Motorola, and I'd expect the leavers would have preferred to ditch any such documents (though part of the reason for the settlement with Motorola was that one of the engineers stupidly taking 6800 documentation with him). This [1] summarizes it. Here [2] is an interview by Computer History Museum of Chuck Peddle. It matches up with the first chapters in Bagnalls book very closely. From about 50 minutes in he talks about his meetings w/Motorola customers that told him the 6800 was too expensive. From about 58m in they talk about basically setting a size metric for the chip to meet a low enough price point. So the starting point was what they could fit in small enough space. You might also like the Computer History Museum interview w/Bill Mensch [3] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":21849310,"author":"ChuckMcM","month":"2019-12","story":"In Memoriam of Charles “Chuck” Peddle","stance":"rec","why":"Peddle's interview on computation, from someone who met him.","quote":"really was impressed at how he understood and expressed computation.","text":"Agreed. I was sad to hear of Chuck's passing. His MOS Technology was a huge thorn in Intel's side in the early days. That company punched way above its weight class because the chip was so simple it made a huge number of things possible. I got to meet him when he did this interview ( [VIDEO] ) with the Computer History Museum and really was impressed at how he understood and expressed computation."},{"id":41082705,"author":"LarryMade2","month":"2024-07","story":"Ask HN: Why did Commodore have a better BASIC line editor than Apple?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Chuck Peddle has claimed credit for the PET's (and later Commodore 8-bit) user friendly screen editor.","text":"Chuck Peddle has claimed credit for the PET's (and later Commodore 8-bit) user friendly screen editor. I have heard it in some interviews with him. The computer History Museum has one interview where he mentions it - [VIDEO] Radio Shack's TRS-80 editor is also not that great either."},{"id":37687156,"author":"dingosity","month":"2023-09","story":"Ask HN: Embedded Scripting Options for Microcontrollers","stance":"reference","why":"Peddle discusses the 6502 design process and its intended market.","quote":"In it he discussed their design process.","text":"Yup. Chuck Peddle (one of the guys largely responsible for the design of the 6502) did an oral history interview with the CHM (Computer History Museum in Mountain View, CA.) In it he discussed their design process. They thought the market for the 6502 was as a controller (cars, HVAC, instrumentation, etc.), not the CPU of a microcomputer. But then Tiny BASIC came along at exactly the right time and hid the messier 6502 design decisions (zero page, weird addressing modes, 256 byte stack, etc.) from a whole bunch of users. So yes... BASIC is absolutely a contender for small systems. But so are MicroLisp and FORTH. Personally, I prefer Lisp, but completely agree Lisp's learning curve is waaay steeper than BASIC's. Same for FORTH. Here's the video, btw: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21853078,"author":"pjbk","month":"2019-12","story":"In Memoriam of Charles “Chuck” Peddle","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The Computer History Museum interviewed him back in 2014 and posted the video on YT earlier this year","text":"So sad. I learned to program with my Atari 130XE, mainly in 6502 Assembly. Truly respected him and his constant engineering and entrepreneurship efforts. The Computer History Museum interviewed him back in 2014 and posted the video on YT earlier this year: [VIDEO] Scene World Magazine also did an extensive interview 6 years ago: [LINK]"},{"id":20670488,"author":"rasz","month":"2019-08","story":"Commodore 900","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Those two videos touch on the Commodore culture of curb stomping until only the weak and dumb survive","text":"Tramiel had a habit of firing good engineers 3 months after product release. Either directly, or by screwing them by moving to useless positions or taking bonuses away until people 'got the message'. Those who didnt get what was going on or didnt care about money/dignity were the ones who stayed as long term employees. Jack had an eye for suckers and people putting passion before common sense and self respect. Whole C64 design team was effed out of promised bonuses and fired/left. People like Bruce Crockett (manufacturing), Al Charpentier (VIC) and Robert Yannes (SID) went to start Ensoniq (later sold to Creative), Chuck Peddle went to Apple, Bill Mensch hung around for two years of abuse. Same with Amiga team (Jay,RJ,Needle). Those two videos touch on the Commodore culture of curb stomping until only the weak and dumb survive: Oral History of Chuck Peddle [VIDEO] Oral History of William David \"Bill\" Mensch Jr. [LINK]"}],"r":[["VVV4xeWBIxE",1],["Ne1ApyqSvm0",1],["bS5P_LAqiVg",1]]},"yCm9Ng0bbEQ":{"wy":"Cited as a data-backed counter to doom and pessimism about the state of the world.","q":{"x":"tl;dr: it's getting better. We're living longer, homicides are down, fewer wars and autocracies, etc..","i":27950391,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27950391,"author":"blippage","month":"2021-07","story":"Americans' life ratings reach record high","stance":"rec","why":"Numbers showing longer lives, fewer homicides, wars and autocracies.","quote":"tl;dr: it's getting better. We're living longer, homicides are down, fewer wars and autocracies, etc..","text":"\"Is the world getting better or worse? A look at the numbers | Steven Pinker\" [VIDEO] tl;dr: it's getting better. We're living longer, homicides are down, fewer wars and autocracies, etc.."},{"id":17151635,"author":"JoshCole","month":"2018-05","story":"Tesla that crashed in Autopilot mode sped up before hitting truck: police","stance":"rec","why":"TED talk arguing with data that the world is improving.","quote":"here are some cool TED talks [5][6]","text":"A quick google search puts the average number of deaths due to car crashes at 3,287 per day [1]. Another one gives me a list of four deaths attributed to autonomous vehicles [2]. Not four per day. Just four. Ever. Memory leads me to recall that statistical reports indicating that vehicles with assistive features like autopilot have a better than traditional safety record [3]. I especially disagree with your thought experiment, because I've watched YouTube videos of situations in which Tesla's autopilot avoided accidents both through stopping and through swerving out of the way [4]. I don't agree with your claim that robotic cars tend to have more severe accidents than traditional cars. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK] The short seller thesis for TESLA is that since the company is one which is valued based on its story, not its underlying fundamentals, it follows that its price will be volatile in response to negative press (accidents, missed projections, etc). There is a clear path from this observation to making money via negative press. If you're interested in hearing more about how the world is a lot better than you thought it was, but the truth of our situation is distorted by competing forces, here are some cool TED talks [5][6]. [5]: [VIDEO] [6]: [LINK]"},{"id":30197074,"author":"maerF0x0","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: General Mood of the Community","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as support for humanity's long-term success.","quote":"An example of support for success could be: (Steven pinker TED talk)","text":"if we look at the human race's success across time though we've survived many pandemics and many hard to adapt to events. I guess it entirely depends on your scale of perspective, from individual and short term to species and long term. An example of support for success could be: [VIDEO] (Steven pinker TED talk)"},{"id":25952687,"author":"vegetablepotpie","month":"2021-01","story":"Global real interest rates and the ‘suprasecular’ decline, 1311–2018 (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for declining wars, disease and poverty.","quote":"Wars, disease, and poverty have been on the decline [0]","text":"> there's been an increase in stability and decrease in risk throughout the centuries. Wars, disease, and poverty have been on the decline [0], which is great and makes things more stable and predictable, and therefore debt is less risky and less expensive. It would be great if these trends continue and don’t reverse. However I can’t help but think of the risks climate change will introduce to our world. A world with more extreme weather events is going to chip away at all this stability we have and push us into a less predictable world again [1]. [0] Is the world getting better or worse? A look at the numbers [VIDEO] [1] How climate change threatens to wreck the economy [LINK]"},{"id":18106399,"author":"pmorici","month":"2018-09","story":"Marriage Costs in China Are Out of Control","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as objective measurement that the world is better off.","quote":"Objective measurements show that the world better off than it was and still improving.","text":"\"Maybe people don't want to bring children into a world that will be worse off in many ways than the one they grew up in?\" Objective measurements show that the world better off than it was and still improving. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17803363,"author":"JoshCole","month":"2018-08","story":"After 60 Years, I-95 Is Complete","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as data showing the future has been better than the past.","quote":"The future can be better than the past. According to the data it has been [1][2][3].","text":"> at best a marketing gimmick Cynicism masquerades as wisdom, but it is not wisdom. A cynic is not merely one who reads bitter lessons from the past; he is one who is prematurely disappointed in the future. The future can be better than the past. According to the data it has been [1][2][3]. So optimism isn't a fluffy ideal to scoff at. Newton's laws make it clear that approaching vacuum allows for greater speeds. An object in motion will remain in motion unless an outside force acts upon it [4]. Therefore, the greater part of truth is out of reach of cynicism. What is more likely, that a man who heads a company that works in the space sector would think that moving through a near-vacuum was honestly more efficient [5]? Or that he would propose it merely as a marketing gimmick? And why can't it be both ? [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK] [5]: [LINK]"}],"r":[["g6bOwQdCJrc",1]]},"MOvfn1p92_8":{"wy":"HN cites it as a favorite demo of live coding and hot reload in a game engine.","q":{"x":"This is my favorite Unreal 4 demo","i":9133988,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9133988,"author":"archivator","month":"2015-03","story":"Unreal Engine 4 is now available to everyone for free","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Unreal 4 demo with live code swapping while in game.","quote":"This is my favorite Unreal 4 demo","text":"This is my favorite Unreal 4 demo - [VIDEO] (sadly missing the wonderful quote \"I light my level by dropping a sun in.\") I'm completely in awe of the feedback loop here. It even compiles and swaps code while you're in the game!"},{"id":13216361,"author":"binthere","month":"2016-12","story":"Choosing Functional Programming for Our Game","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates Unreal 4 live coding hot-reload.","quote":"jump to 9:40 if it doesn't load at this point automatically","text":"I believe that on Unreal Engine 4, you use C++ and it does have a feature that mimics \"live coding\" where it recompiles and hot-reload your game even while playing. edit: here it is [VIDEO] (jump to 9:40 if it doesn't load at this point automatically)"},{"id":10079603,"author":"amadeusw","month":"2015-08","story":"Working Toward Bret Victor's “Learnable Programming”","stance":"reference","why":"Shows changing gravity in Visual Studio with hot reload.","quote":"they hop to Visual Studio where they change gravity. After a few seconds of compilation, the game's code is updated.","text":"A few years ago (2012) I was taken aback by the Unreal Engine development process. Their IDE features a running game with all elements being selectable and interactively editable. In one video fragment [1] they hop to Visual Studio where they change gravity. After a few seconds of compilation, the game's code is updated. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"TitZV6k8zfA":{"wy":"Referenced as the definitive review explaining why the AI Pin failed.","q":{"x":"Essential viewing: Review of the Ai Pin","i":43096748,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43096748,"author":"light_triad","month":"2025-02","story":"HP Acquires Humane's AI Software","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why the AI Pin failed as a device","quote":"Essential viewing: Review of the Ai Pin","text":"Interesting they thought they could disrupt phones: devices with almost 20 years of iterative improvements, extremely mature app stores, tons of functionality, fast ubiquitous internet, etc. You couldn't even connect the Ai Pin to your phone ?! Lock-in makes sense but it was a very risky bet. Essential viewing: Review of the Ai Pin - The Worst Product I've Ever Reviewed... For Now ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":44137972,"author":"mattnewton","month":"2025-05","story":"Sam Altman and Jony Ive Will Force A.I. Into Your Life","stance":"reference","why":"Review argues smartphones dominate daily carry devices","quote":"Marques meant that, they are very good at the broad category of a daily carry consumer device [0].","text":"Marques meant that, they are very good at the broad category of a daily carry consumer device [0]. If you are competing against them, they are heavily favored to win. If you want to make a consumer device, as all these ai hardware companies like humane professed, you are competing against smartphones which have kinda evolved like a desire path, smooshing together all the previous personal electronics in a general form factor. [0] [VIDEO] if it’s helpful context, he has similar conclusions in his rabbit r1 review. I am quoting him because I think he’s saying it more clearly than the same vibe I get, that these devices are borne out of a company objective to own a platform and not a user need that necessitated a new platform."},{"id":42004345,"author":"m463","month":"2024-10","story":"Ed Roberts created the personal computer industry (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Review panning the Humane AI Pin","quote":"","text":"Thinking about this MVP, I wonder if in another 50 years people will describe products like the AI pin that MKBHD panned in the same way. EDIT: \"The Worst Product I've Ever Reviewed... For Now\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":41654404,"author":"nozzlegear","month":"2024-09","story":"Mkbsd: Download all the wallpapers in MKBHD's \"Panels\" app for free","stance":"reference","why":"Brownlee's harsh AI Pin review as example of influence","quote":"he reviewed the product so negatively that he received flak on social media for being too harsh","text":"How does what you said jive with, for example, his review of the Humane AI Pin¹, where he reviewed the product so negatively that he received flak on social media for being too harsh and careless with his influence. Another video of his, a review of the Fisker Ocean², lead to accusations that he killed the company behind the car due to the negative review. ¹ MKBHD: The Worst Product I've Ever Reviewed... For Now [VIDEO] ² Auto Focus: This is the Worst Car I've Ever Reviewed [LINK] ³ MKBHD: Do Bad Reviews Kill Companies? [LINK]"},{"id":40063103,"author":"ZiiS","month":"2024-04","story":"Humane AI – Pico Laser Projection – AI Twist on an Old Scam (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Brownlee's harsh Humane AI Pin review","quote":"The Worst Product I've Ever Reviewed... For Now","text":"They have got really positive (relatively) reviews though [VIDEO] \"The Worst Product I've Ever Reviewed... For Now\" at least a different league then the other scams listed."},{"id":40069575,"author":"bap","month":"2024-04","story":"Show HN: A self-published art book about Google's first 25 years","stance":"reference","why":"Brownlee's AI Pin review where the book appears on screen","quote":"Marques Brownlee recently published a video about the Humane AI Pin (device)","text":"Marques Brownlee recently published a video about the Humane AI Pin (device) [1] and I noticed this book in some of the device shots, found it curious and had (incorrectly it seems) assumed it was some kind of internal Google swag. Well played Mr. da Maia :) 1 [VIDEO]"},{"id":40069656,"author":"johndamaia","month":"2024-04","story":"Show HN: A self-published art book about Google's first 25 years","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamp where the book is visible","quote":"it also shows very clearly in minute 3:51","text":"Yes! Good catch, it also shows very clearly in minute 3:51 [0]. Thank you so much for your support! [0] - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["oFfVt3S51T4",1]]},"Uo3cL4nrGOk":{"wy":"Shared as a joke about JavaScript culture and tooling, usually without much explanation.","m":[{"id":44112970,"author":"tanseydavid","month":"2025-05","story":"At Amazon, some coders say their jobs have begun to resemble warehouse work","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"you're in for a treat","text":"Interview with a Senior JS Developer (satire) [VIDEO] (you've probably already seen it--everyone else has. But if not, you're in for a treat)"},{"id":35558029,"author":"solvencino","month":"2023-04","story":"Show HN: Extract clips from YouTube (without downloading whole video)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"demo command : deno run --allow-net --allow-run [LINK] \" [VIDEO] \" -s \"00:01:00\" -e \"00:01:15\" -r Usage: deno run --allow-net --allow-run [LINK] <yt_link> -s <start_time> -e <end_time> -r -o file_name Description: - Download clips from youtube without downloading whole video - To download whole video leave start and end args - Depends on ffmpeg Options: -h, --help - Show this help. -s, --start <type> - starting time of clip in hh:mm:ss format -e, --end <type> - ending time of clip in hh:mm:ss format -r, --re-enc [small] - re-encode video in ffmpeg -o, --output <type> - output file name"},{"id":31005759,"author":"runekaagaard","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: Where can we find the unsexy jobs?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I totally agree, the range in number of servers/services/technologies different companies use to solve similar problems are SO wide. After 12 years of growth we are still happily on: - 1 Ubuntu server - 1 MySQL db - 1 Django framework - Ansible for deployment And thats mostly it. Due to Hetzner missing the 27002 certification we moved from metal to Amazon EC2 instances which was pretty uneventful. We also moved to Aurora and the point-in-time restores actually makes me sleep better at night. Relative to Hetzner it's a lot more expensive. Not at all a problem in the absolute sense. Some years back I had a chance to talk with the CTO of a company very similar to ours, and their landscape was 400+ microservices, 50 servers managed by k8s on Azure Cloud, Service bridges, Workflow engines, 5 different DB engines, etc. It sounded very fascinating, but also quite complicated. Some of my worst decisions over the years has been not having the balls to say no to current best-practices. I've learned the hard way that even though the pressure of using shining-new-thingTM can be immense it's so important to realise that everything always has pros and cons. The most important thing is trying to really understand _my_ specific problem and _then_ using the simplest and most obvious solution that can work. Obligatory \"Programmers are also human\" link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30572255,"author":"drawkbox","month":"2022-03","story":"Building a web app with no framework","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The biggest crimes of web frameworks is they take you away into abstraction land away from the actual standards and they complexify simplicity, all of that under the guise of being more \"standard\" and more \"simple\". It is one of the greatest lies ever sold. I remember how .NET WebForms and lots of Java boilerplate frameworks did that early on, or browsers breaking standards, they wanted developer lock-in just like web frameworks of today. They wanted to keep developers dumb by being \"simple\" as long as you stay in their walled garden where they handle the standards. No thanks... I have been developing javascript for decades and I actually liked the original less Java like boilerplate of the previous iterations, and you can still do that. The people that push frameworks aren't always trying to make things more simple, they want control, lock-in and domain ownership. The last tool to really simplify in javascript was jquery and most of that is browser level now including selection the killer feature of jquery across all those document.all/document.layer days of pain. Those days are over, simplicity is being complexified now. Tools like jquery and even Flash or other plugins were platform pushers that got the web to the place we have now which is better than ever for web standards, yet we have all these bloated frameworks on top now. In a way it is a bit like the xkcd comic with so many standards, just to not do javascript people built thousands of frameworks on top to \"simplify"}],"r":[["IS5ycm7VfXg",1]]},"EczW2IWdnOM":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it for Google's reasons for backing open silicon and for background on the open-source chip design workflow.","q":{"x":"I suggest you watch this video from Google where they explain why they are doing what they are doing","i":41930969,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41930969,"author":"panick21_","month":"2024-10","story":"RISC-V is currently slow compared to modern CPUs","stance":"rec","why":"Google explains why it invests in open hardware ecosystems.","quote":"I suggest you watch this video from Google where they explain why they are doing what they are doing","text":"> The architecture doesn't offer anything actually new to the industry. There is more to live then technical specification. The change in the license and the development pattern and the business model does actually matter. I suggest you watch some talks by Krste Asanovic who created the RISC-V project, he explained exactly this point. > Open source software is unreasonably effective because of that trivial marginal cost of reproduction. Its open hardware isn't as good as open software. Yes we know. Sadly we don't have a universal 3d printer. But that doesn't mean its worthless. > Unlike the Linux kernel you can't compile a CPU core and reboot and get some performance gain. Have you never used an modern FPGA? > It just doesn't solve any of the very real problems of producing hardware. If you have a narrow point of you of only considering manufacturing but if you broaden your point of view and look at the whole value chain, it absolutely does. And you know who agrees with me, tons of companies who have invested in RISC-V and the ecosystem. If you don't believe me, I suggest you watch this video from Google where they explain why they are doing what they are doing: [VIDEO] You can find videos like that from other companies, including hardware companies. > It doesn't obviate the challenges or costs of developing new process nodes or chemistries. I didn't know that producing hardware was the same as developing knew nodes. In your mind, the only thing that matters is the cost of ne"},{"id":27778237,"author":"nickik","month":"2021-07","story":"Libre-SoC 180nm Power ISA v3.0 ASIC Submitted to IMEC MPW","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on the open-source manufacturable 130nm PDK.","quote":"There is a huge amount of great stuff going on this this area.","text":"Google has done a lot of effort in that direction. The first ever chips have already been produced that are fully open source from the tools used to make to the complete tool chain need to manufacture them. There is a huge amount of great stuff going on this this area. Tim Ansell - Skywater PDK: Fully open source manufacturable PDK for a 130nm process [VIDEO]"},{"id":25274488,"author":"KenoFischer","month":"2020-12","story":"Google partners with SkyWater to enable open source manufacturing of ASICs","stance":"reference","why":"Source for the topic being discussed around the one hour mark.","quote":"E.g. see from June around the one hour mark.","text":"It was talked about all over the place. E.g. see [VIDEO] from June around the one hour mark. As I said, I'm very far from an insider."},{"id":25043170,"author":"nickik","month":"2020-11","story":"What is a System-on-Chip (SoC), and why do we care if they are open source?","stance":"reference","why":"Overview of open-source chip design workflow with Google and eFabless.","quote":"Google, FOSSi and eFabless are working on a 100% open source chip design workflow","text":"Google, FOSSi and eFabless are working on a 100% open source chip design workflow that would allow you to use only open source tools to create a chip, and thanks to other efforts open source chips and ISA exist already: See: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23758536,"author":"cottonseed","month":"2020-07","story":"Google offers free fabbing for 130nm open-source chips","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Google's motives for open silicon ecosystem.","quote":"Tim directly addresses this in the talk","text":"Google wants to create an open, innovative ecosystem for silicon so it will be easier for them to build accelerators for their workloads to meet the growing demand for compute. TPU is only one example of the kind of accelerators they want to build. Tim directly addresses this in the talk: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":25274580,"author":"octoberfranklin","month":"2020-12","story":"Google partners with SkyWater to enable open source manufacturing of ASICs","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It's over an hour of somebody talking at a camera.","text":"> It was talked about all over the place. Really? Where can we read about it? > E.g. see [VIDEO] from June around the one hour mark. What are you referring to, where exactly in there? I poked \"around the one hour mark\" and didn't stumble across it, but am not gonna watch the whole thing. It's over an hour of somebody talking at a camera. Does an offhand comment buried in the middle of a 90-minute youtube ramble really strike you as appropriate notification of such an important requirement? Especially when this requirement doesn't appear anyplace where it can be read ?"}],"r":[["vvZA9n3e5pc",1],["hgN8pCMLI2U",1],["040ejWnFkj0",1],["4zG0uecYSMA",1],["c52QhiXsmyI",1],["rv-mFSoZOkE",1]]},"G-2dQoeqVVo":{"wy":"Cited for his plain-language explanation of how VR convinces the brain and for resolution and pixel-density figures.","q":{"x":"Michael Abrash of Valve now at Oculus giving a basic description","i":10009461,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10009461,"author":"mkeblx","month":"2015-08","story":"I Tried VR and It Was Just OK","stance":"rec","why":"Abrash's basic description of how VR convinces the brain","quote":"Michael Abrash of Valve now at Oculus giving a basic description","text":"Michael Abrash of Valve now at Oculus giving a basic description: [VIDEO] It's parts of your lower brain aligning to convince you you're in a virtual world, and while it is a spectrum you really need a high end headset to experience it. Abrash himself mainly dismissed VR's potential prior to experiencing it."},{"id":13394664,"author":"teach","month":"2017-01","story":"VR","stance":"reference","why":"Source for VR pixel density versus original Quake","quote":"According to Michael Abrash","text":"Yes. Even the best VR headsets still have incredibly low pixel density. According to Michael Abrash, \"the per-degree pixel density of a 1k by 1k, 110-degree VR display is... actually lower pixel density than the original Quake running at 320x200.\"[0] And when you call the graphics \"terrible\" do you mean they actually look bad to you from inside a VR headset, or just looking at the screenshot on some other device? Because what looks amazing in a traditional game can look cartoony and fake within VR, and incredibly simple things can look startlingly real. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7538979,"author":"leoc","month":"2014-04","story":"Alan Kay at Demo: The Future Doesn't Have to Be Incremental","stance":"reference","why":"Abrash talk discussing the same optical illusion as Alan Kay","quote":"the same optical illusion has been discussed by Michael Abrash","text":"It's amusing that the same optical illusion has been discussed by Michael Abrash [VIDEO] and Alan Kay [LINK] in talks on very different topics recently. > Thomas Paine said in Common Sense , instead of having the king be the law, why, we can have the law be the king. That was one of the biggest shifts, large scale shifts in history because he realised \"hey, we can design a better society than tradition has and we can put it into law; so, we're just going to invert thousands of years of beliefs\". Pfft, tell that to the 13th-century Venetians: [LINK] . Constitutionalism isn't that new an idea."}]},"GyV_UG60dD4":{"wy":"Mostly posted as a joke in threads about corporate-speak, buzzwords and meeting culture.","m":[{"id":22866580,"author":"bombledmonk","month":"2020-04","story":"The Weirdly Enduring Appeal of Weird Al Yankovic","stance":"rec","why":"Weird Al parody of corporate culture","quote":"should watch.","text":"I'm surprised the corporate culture parody song Mission Statement didn't come up. Every person who's ever been in a corporation should watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47410626,"author":"Freak_NL","month":"2026-03","story":"Kagi Translate now supports LinkedIn Speak as an output language","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"if you put the lyrics to","text":"Good idea for a unit test for this: if you put the lyrics to \"Weird Al\" Yankovic's Mission Statement in, it should return the exact same text as output. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34621561,"author":"bitwize","month":"2023-02","story":"Microsoft Teams Premium: powered by OpenAI’s GPT-3.5","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"If you train an AI on enough Teams meetings","text":"If you train an AI on enough Teams meetings, it will probably eventually generate the lyrics to \"Mission Statement\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":26231428,"author":"dsr_","month":"2021-02","story":"Unsuck It (2010)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"First verse: We must all efficiently Operationalize our strategies","text":"[VIDEO] (Weird Al Yankovic: Mission Statement) First verse: We must all efficiently Operationalize our strategies Invest in world-class technology And leverage our core competencies"},{"id":19757074,"author":"all2","month":"2019-04","story":"A former lead designer of Gmail fixes Gmail with a Chrome extension","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'll start my brainstorming session by watching this","text":"Now I want to try this. I'll start my brainstorming session by watching this: [VIDEO] I can make my website here: [LINK] Now, what am I missing?"},{"id":17873968,"author":"framebit","month":"2018-08","story":"Ask HN: What buzzwords or expressions do you dislike the most?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"using corporate-speak","text":"- Cognitive - Solutioning - Learning as a noun, as in \"these are some good learnings\" - Most things mentioned in Weird Al's brilliant lampoon of Judy Blue Eyes using corporate-speak: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17883777,"author":"dtech","month":"2018-08","story":"Mutiny at the big five is part of the future of work","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I could not distinguish this from a parody song like Weird Al Yankovic","text":"Oh my god, I did not know that Poe's law applied to corporate marketing now. I could not distinguish this from a parody song like Weird Al Yankovic's - \"Mission Statement\" [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15666278,"author":"csours","month":"2017-11","story":"Can Ford Turn Itself into a Tech Company?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Instead, I heard the Ford Motor Company described as a “mobility solutions provider” that engages in “multimodal journey planning.” People spoke about “whiteboarding” and “blue-skying” big ideas. I watched a video that described Ford’s efforts to build a “holistic, organic, interconnected system powered by a transportation operating system.” \"Weird Al\" Yankovic - Mission Statement: [VIDEO]"}]},"HLAzeHnNgR8":{"wy":"Shared after 2022 as evidence that McCain accurately predicted Russian aggression and the cost of showing weakness.","q":{"x":"In 2014, McCain absolutely nailed his prediction on Ukraine 2022.","i":43212576,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43915013,"author":"anonymars","month":"2025-05","story":"India launches attack on 9 sites in Pakistan and Pakistani Jammu and Kashmir","stance":"rec","why":"Short video of McCain's 2014 predictions after Crimea","quote":"Here's another short video I always share on this topic","text":"Here's another short video I always share on this topic, with John McCain laying things out in 2014 after Russia seized Crimea. We're about halfway through his predictions, I'd say. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43771363,"author":"anonymars","month":"2025-04","story":"America's cyber defenses are being dismantled from the inside","stance":"reference","why":"McCain was right that showing Putin weakness is perilous","quote":"So far John McCain had this one right. Show Putin weakness at your peril.","text":"Or worse, rifling through the victim's pockets (mineral deal) and demanding a thank you. So far John McCain had this one right. Show Putin weakness at your peril. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43455235,"author":"anonymars","month":"2025-03","story":"Germany is unlocking billions to supercharge its military at a seismic moment","stance":"reference","why":"2014 food for thought on Russian irredentism","quote":"Food for thought from 2014(!)","text":"Is there data in 2021 that would have led you to think Russia would attempt an outright invasion of Ukraine? Is it really so outlandish to think that Russian irredentism[0] will continue? Food for thought from 2014(!): [VIDEO] [0]: [LINK]"},{"id":43212576,"author":"smallmancontrov","month":"2025-02","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"McCain accurately predicted Ukraine 2022 in 2014","quote":"In 2014, McCain absolutely nailed his prediction on Ukraine 2022.","text":"> everyone who bought into that stupid ideology has been wrong about everything my entire lifetime In 2014, McCain absolutely nailed his prediction on Ukraine 2022. Did you? [VIDEO]"},{"id":42532238,"author":"2OEH8eoCRo0","month":"2024-12","story":"Cable-cutting tanker seized by Finland 'was loaded with spying equipment'","stance":"reference","why":"McCain's 2014 prediction of Russian empire rebuilding","quote":"McCain on Putin in 2014.","text":"[VIDEO] McCain on Putin in 2014. They want Eastern Ukraine and Crimea, and Moldova and the Baltics if they can get away with it. He is rebuilding the Russian Empire."}]},"KtHQGs3zFAM":{"wy":"HN points to it as a fun, substantive exchange between two experienced practitioners on TDD's value.","q":{"x":"this was a fun discussion about TDD between two professionals","i":12929237,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12929237,"author":"lobut","month":"2016-11","story":"TDD Doesn't Work","stance":"rec","why":"Fun discussion on TDD between two professionals.","quote":"this was a fun discussion about TDD between two professionals","text":"Yeah, I agree. TDD is also, you cannot write a single line of code without writing a failing unit test though. Well, I'm not a fan of that either. I mean, sometimes I like writing an acceptance test to begin with and work inwards. People sometimes interchangeably use \"TDD\" for \"testing\". Also, just because you do TDD, doesn't mean your code can be great, I've seen people assert pointless things and the unit has gotten so small that people now define them as methods in classes. Which I also think can lead to some crazy maintenance suite of tests. If you're interested, this was a fun discussion about TDD between two professionals: [VIDEO] Jim Coplien and Uncle Bob."},{"id":15467305,"author":"Ace17","month":"2017-10","story":"TerrariaClone – An incomprehensible hellscape of spaghetti code","stance":"reference","why":"Coplien's advice on short-sighted design versus refactoring effort.","quote":"I feel James Coplien's advice on the question is interesting","text":"I feel James Coplien's advice on the question is interesting: [VIDEO] TLDW: \"refactoring your way out\" from a simple but too short-sighted design might take a ridiculous amount of work, compared what would have been necessary if you knew what a \"correct\" design for this problem was."},{"id":7356575,"author":"lgunsch","month":"2014-03","story":"Why Most Unit Testing is Waste [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Debate between Coplien and Martin on TDD and unit testing.","quote":"James Coplien and Bob Martin have a debate of TDD and unit testing.","text":"James Coplien and Bob Martin have a debate of TDD and unit testing. There is a video of it on YouTube: [VIDEO]"}]},"L6A1Lt0kvMA":{"wy":"HN cites it as the first-hand account of video piracy on Facebook whenever the platform's treatment of creators comes up.","q":{"x":"","i":9855201,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9855201,"author":"pen2l","month":"2015-07","story":"Facebook’s Piracy Problem","stance":"rec","why":"Creator explains his views on Facebook freebooting convincingly.","quote":"Did you watch his video where he explains his views himself? I find it pretty convincing","text":"Did you watch his video where he explains his views himself? I find it pretty convincing: [VIDEO] Interestingly, a lot of people are getting very frustrated with Facebook for other reasons as well. Famously, there's Veritasium's video: [LINK] I can really appreciate what they're feeling. With piracy as we have traditionally known it, at least usually the pirate is not directly profiting from his act of piracy. With freebooting, Facebook is literally profiting from other people's work. It's directly diverting money flow from the creator (who REALLY NEED THE MONEY) to Facebook. I think it's plainly ridiculous this is happening, and obviously Facebook will turn a blind eye to it because it's making them money. This is one of the reasons why I encourage whitehat security researchers to turn a little gray when it comes to Facebook and do things to bring them down, it'll be for the greater good to bring the behemoth down."},{"id":20427734,"author":"wolph","month":"2019-07","story":"Facebook is embedding tracking data inside the photos you download","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's apparently a bit of a hassle to get it removed at times though. And in most cases far to late for it to really matter: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17164986,"author":"TAForObvReasons","month":"2018-05","story":"EU censorship machines and link tax laws are nearing the finish line","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It's absolutely insane that freebooting [1] on Facebook was allowed to persist for so long.","text":"It's absolutely insane that freebooting [1] on Facebook was allowed to persist for so long. It's easy to see the right to monetary remuneration as a method of addressing a massive loophole exploited by Facebook and certain users for years. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11738282,"author":"Ralfp","month":"2016-05","story":"Fox 'uses' a gameplay video from YouTube, and removes the original with DMCA","stance":"reference","why":"Story of a slow-mo clip ripped, reuploaded, then DMCA-claimed against the original.","quote":"told a story in one of his videos about slow-mo clip of tatoo machine in action being ripped","text":"SmarterEveryDay told a story in one of his videos about slow-mo clip of tatoo machine in action being ripped from one of his movies by the large press publisher, Bauer, and reuploaded on Bauer's Facebook page. Then Bauer moved on to claim DMCA on his original uploaded by him on his Facebook page: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10951516,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-01","story":"Facebook ad click numbers are inconsistent with other analytics","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence Facebook encouraged video fraud.","quote":"They also have a history of encouraging video fraud[2][3].","text":"Facebook has a history of fraud in their other services. Veritasium fell victim to FB's scams as a publisher a couple years ago[1]. I know first-hand that Facebook's advertising model is deeply flawed. When I paid to promote my page I gained 80,000 followers in developing countries who didn't care about Veritasium (but I wasn't aware of this at the time). They drove my reach and engagement numbers down, basically rendering the page useless. They also have a history of encouraging video fraud[2][3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":10653188,"author":"forrestthewoods","month":"2015-12","story":"Anger at 'stolen' online courses on Udemy","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I didn't say Udemy was responsible for flagging content. But I am saying there's no fucking way there are only 125 copyright infringing cases. It's a shit ton more. It may not be their responsibility to find them. But to imply that's the extent of the problem is wrong and a lie. \"nor can anyone other than the copyright holder reasonably be expected to\" I don't think this is a fundamentally true statement. For example I believe that Facebook has a moral obligation to provide tools to stop freebooting. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10659802,"author":"pen2l","month":"2015-12","story":"A letter to our daughter","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Facebook steals money from content creators who really need the money","text":"Consider as well that Facebook steals money from content creators who really need the money . See [VIDEO] & [LINK] Also, the idea of having Facebook at the cost of giving up your privacy to this behemoth, and more importantly, at the cost of this company exploiting your cognitive biases to get you to buy things hardly seems conducive to a spirit of bettering the world."},{"id":10770812,"author":"snthd","month":"2015-12","story":"Why we chose to move to HTML5 video","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">“Freebooting” is a silly but oddly appropriate word invented by YouTubers CGP Grey and Brady Haran that means “taking online media and re-hosting it on your website without permission.” [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10366596,"author":"pen2l","month":"2015-10","story":"Just Say No to Facebook's Internet.org, Says Inventor of World Wide Web","stance":"reference","why":"First-hand account of creators losing revenue to Facebook re-uploads.","quote":"See for a first-hand account of the situation.","text":"It's parasitic because of its particular approach. For example, it could embed youtube videos... so that the real creators get their pay, but no, Facebook would rather that the videos be Facebook videos, thereby the original creator getting zero profit from his hard work. See [VIDEO] for a first-hand account of the situation."},{"id":9854985,"author":"lips","month":"2015-07","story":"Facebook’s Piracy Problem","stance":"reference","why":"Creator describes trying to get Facebook to remove his pirated video.","quote":"Destin mentions in his \"intro to freebooting\" video his various attempts to contact Facebook regarding the piracy","text":"They're both at fault, to varying degrees, explicitly or implicitly. Destin mentions in his \"intro to freebooting\" video his various attempts to contact Facebook regarding the piracy, and that it was only removed after a Twitter campaign led by his viewers: [VIDEO] I don't know much, or have any experience with DMCA, but it's my impression that if there's a copyright violation on Youtube, the video can be taken down very quickly, and the burden of proof is then placed on the uploader to get it back up."}],"r":[["-DVyjdw4t9I",1]]},"UWacQrEcMHk":{"wy":"Seen as an inflection point in how Blender's UI was perceived, and as helping kick off the 2.8 redesign.","q":{"x":"I think Blender Guru's videos kicked off a lot of the 2.8 design work","i":22855897,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":13318763,"author":"mastax","month":"2017-01","story":"Inkscape Version 0.92 is Released","stance":"rec","why":"Improved Blender UI mock-up with many good ideas","quote":"Andrew Price's improved UI mock-up did look pretty amazing","text":"> Andrew Price's improved UI mock-up did look pretty amazing [VIDEO] if anyone's looking E: After watching it, I have to say it has a lot of good ideas, although the dynamic ribbon stuff is probably too much (how did I get here, how do I go back?)"},{"id":29436209,"author":"franciscop","month":"2021-12","story":"Blender 3.0","stance":"reference","why":"Inflection point in how Blender's UI was perceived","quote":"I believe this video was a big inflection point","text":"To be fair, Blender is better than 99% of the projects I've ever seen, commercial or not. AND they are open source, AND they have a great community behind, AND they are also great at promotional bits like this page itself, AND they are being backed by large companies. So IMHO it's one of the best executed desktop programs while also being in a massively difficult field, and they deserve the praise. In many places there are outliers like this; Stripe for Documentation, Wikipedia for encyclopedic knowledge, Google for search, etc. The two main criticisms that I've found is that first the interface is very confusing (but this has been improved constantly in the last 5-10 years!). As a personal opinion, I believe this video was a big inflection point (Blender seemed stubborn before the video, but this video at the very least softened the opinion of Blender): [VIDEO] The second one is that it's not \"industry standard\", but as more and more people are using it for hobbies and indie projects, this will def change eventually, so I'm not too worried about that as well."},{"id":22855897,"author":"wlesieutre","month":"2020-04","story":"Inkscape 1.0 Release Candidate","stance":"reference","why":"Videos that helped kick off the Blender 2.8 UI redesign","quote":"I think Blender Guru's videos kicked off a lot of the 2.8 design work","text":"I think you mean either 2.5 or 2.8. 2.49 was the last one with the really inscrutable old UI, 2.5 was a big overhaul but still had a lot of basic functionality buried behind hotkeys or menus, and 2.8 is the relatively recent one with a lot of that basic functionality exposed. I don't follow the blender development process too closely, but I think Blender Guru's videos kicked off a lot of the 2.8 design work: [VIDEO] Along with lots of discussion elsewhere as the actual redesign was underway [LINK]"}],"r":[["KkpqA8yG9T4",1]]},"YcVSgYz5SJ8":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as a source for Paine's continental-versus-maritime framework and her views on sanctions when discussing geopolitics.","q":{"x":"has some interesting ideas","i":38243716,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38243716,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-11","story":"The attack against Danish, critical infrastructure [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Sarah Paine on continental versus maritime power and why land grabs stopped paying.","quote":"has some interesting ideas","text":"> The value of the land in a long run tends towards infinity, especially if it is populated with taxable people and factories. The taxable people often end up dead and the factories destroyed. Since about the (Second) Industrial Revolution going after land really hasn't been a good way to gain wealth. Sarah Paine, Professor of History and Strategy at the US Naval War College has some interesting ideas in \"The Geopolitics and History of Continental and Maritime Power\": * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":45225436,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2025-09","story":"Ships are sailing with fake insurance from the Norwegian Ro Marine","stance":"reference","why":"Paine argues sanctions work by suppressing growth over generations, not changing behavior.","quote":"What they do is they suppress growth so that whoever's annoying you over time, you're stronger and they're weaker.","text":"> 25 years ago, IR scholar Dan Drezner wrote the book _The Sanctions Paradox_ which tried to explain, in an IR theory sort of way, why sanctions are used so often and achieve so little- they don't overthrow governments, they rarely even manage to make governments stop doing the things we don't like. Sanctions are a negative-rate compounding system. Sarah Paine from the US Naval War College: > People look at sanctions and go, “Oh, they don't work because you don't make whoever's annoying you change whatever they're doing.” What they do is they suppress growth so that whoever's annoying you over time, you're stronger and they're weaker. And the example of the impact of sanctions is compare North and South Korea. It's powerful over several generations. * [VIDEO] * [LINK]"},{"id":42749737,"author":"pas","month":"2025-01","story":"EFF statement on U.S. Supreme Court's decision to uphold TikTok ban","stance":"reference","why":"Continental versus maritime power competition explained by Paine.","quote":"Game theory is pushing continental powers against maritime ones.","text":"There's no need to demonize people, soulless systems will do just fine. Game theory is pushing continental powers against maritime ones. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42430511,"author":"pas","month":"2024-12","story":"US lawmakers tell Apple, Google to be ready to remove TikTok from stores Jan. 19","stance":"reference","why":"Paine's framework of maritime order versus continental order.","quote":"","text":"I think the useful model for this is that currently there's a competition between the naval powers and the land powers, which is rapidly heating up. (Due to the last decades of very high rate of economic growth of China.) [VIDEO] Maritime order (basically a trade alliance, if you join you have more chance to influence it, win-win) and the continental order (buffer zone, extractive/authoritarian, negative-sum). Of course as the competition is getting fierce one seems to borrow from the other. (Russia is funding itself and its war from trade. And China sold market access in exchange for technology.) And the US is now transitioning from soft-power to pay up or you are out. (Balance of trade, NATO contributions, etc.)"}],"r":[["fXW-QjBsruE",1],["QWWgr2rN45o",1],["LMxate9gegg",1],["qE7dYhpI_bI",1],["HNkLcD3PKyk",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1]]},"aJ4TJ4-kkDw":{"wy":"HN points to it as a demonstration of artificial daylight, especially for windowless or underground rooms, and to date the technology to 2014.","q":{"x":"If anybody wants to see a cool artificial light that mimics natural light well take a look at coelux","i":11747297,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11747297,"author":"internaut","month":"2016-05","story":"World's Largest Solar Plant Sets Itself Ablaze Due to Misaligned Mirrors","stance":"rec","why":"Shows a cool artificial light that mimics natural light well.","quote":"If anybody wants to see a cool artificial light that mimics natural light well take a look at coelux","text":"If anybody wants to see a cool artificial light that mimics natural light well take a look at coelux: [VIDEO] It is imperfect (costs too much) but it'll give a good idea of why I think heliostat/optic cable/natural light is something more people need in their lives."},{"id":24988660,"author":"amai","month":"2020-11","story":"Building an artificial sun that looks realistic [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Notes the technology is not new, dating to 2014.","quote":"This is not new. See this video from 2014","text":"This is not new. See this video from 2014: [VIDEO] This light is sold by [LINK] . They might also hold the patents on this technology."},{"id":21662309,"author":"edoo","month":"2019-11","story":"Rooms can be as bright as the outdoors","stance":"reference","why":"Shows smart LED skylight used underground to simulate daylight.","quote":"Basically a smart led version of a traditional roof sunlight","text":"They are using these underground to help simulate the true daylight experience. Basically a smart led version of a traditional roof sunlight: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11150262,"author":"kirk21","month":"2016-02","story":"CoeLux: Artificial Sunlight That’s Real Enough to Trick Your Camera and Brain","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses usage of the artificial sky light for underground buildings.","quote":"In this video ( ) they talk about the usage for underground buildings.","text":"In this video ( [VIDEO] ) they talk about the usage for underground buildings. It could be for underground buildings what the lift is for high buildings (eg make it practical). This was on Product Hunt a while ago: [LINK]"}],"r":[["tw-rxtwhzcY",1],["gmn7tjSNyAA",1]]},"bJ_seXo-Enc":{"wy":"HN cites it as an intuitive demonstration of the Coriolis effect and the practical problems of spinning habitats.","q":{"x":"I never really got it until I watched the Tom Scott video","i":34212553,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34212553,"author":"cm2187","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Concepts that clicked only years after you first encountered them?","stance":"rec","why":"Makes the Coriolis force finally intuitive.","quote":"I never really got it until I watched the Tom Scott video","text":"Coriolis force. It's pretty basic but I never really got it until I watched the Tom Scott video [VIDEO]"},{"id":44402725,"author":"MobiusHorizons","month":"2025-06","story":"Starcloud can’t put a data centre in space at $8.2M in one Starship","stance":"reference","why":"Shows spinning gravity makes movement harder and can cause sickness.","quote":"Makes many movements harder and can result in getting sick.","text":"Relevant Scott Manley video: [LINK] Relevant tom Scott video: [VIDEO] There is a lot of research, but it’s not as simple as operating under real gravity. Makes many movements harder and can result in getting sick."},{"id":37841651,"author":"inamberclad","month":"2023-10","story":"Microgravity-induced muscle atrophy in women/men in spaceflights to Moon/Mars","stance":"reference","why":"Shows non-intuitive effects of living in a rotating reference frame.","quote":"A rotating reference frame creates some pretty non-intuitive effects","text":"EMS = electro muscular stimulation? No, not 100%. Astronauts have very good resistance training onboard the ISS at this point. Some of them come back to earth stronger than they left. However, it turns out that your body also needs skeletal loading in order to produce strong bones. That kind of loading comes from having large forces distributed through the entire body. NASA tried a variety of shortcuts around resistance training, but none of them were satisfactory. A spinning drum is probably the best method short of artificial gravity, but it has a couple problems. A rotating reference frame creates some pretty non-intuitive effects ( [VIDEO] ). Research is ongoing about just how fast a human can be spun for a long duration, although data is hard to come by. We also don't know how low of a gravity a human can stay healthy in without intervention like serious exercise. If it turns out that you need 1G, that involves a much larger spinning drum than a G/3 (Mars) or G/6 (Moon) environment. The problem is that we really have no way to simulate those environments for a human."},{"id":35179753,"author":"vasvir","month":"2023-03","story":"Venus is volcanically alive, new find shows","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the difficulties of rotation-based artificial gravity.","quote":"Artificial Gravity via rotation is no walk in the the park either.","text":"Artificial Gravity via rotation is no walk in the the park either. See: [LINK] for the long explanation. and the youtube example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32668232,"author":"Retric","month":"2022-08","story":"134.5 kg Concrete Bike [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott tours a rotating lab showing practical problems of artificial gravity.","quote":"Here is a Tom Scott video about a more recent example","text":"The idea is much older for example 1948: [LINK] Various test chambers for astronauts where built to test how people respond to higher gravity / a rotating reference frame. Actually trying to do this runs into some unexpected issues in practice. Here is a Tom Scott video about a more recent example: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aMcjxSThD54",1]]},"fu3ETgAvQrw":{"wy":"Shared as a funny companion to logo-redesign and branding stories.","q":{"x":"The corporate PowerPoint visuals just adds so much.","i":36851624,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36851624,"author":"Nathan2055","month":"2023-07","story":"Twitter has officially changed its logo to ‘X’","stance":"rec","why":"Version of the logo parody song with corporate PowerPoint visuals","quote":"The corporate PowerPoint visuals just adds so much.","text":"There's a version of that with video that Neil reuploaded a while back as well: [VIDEO] The corporate PowerPoint visuals just adds so much."},{"id":43032909,"author":"wormslayer666","month":"2025-02","story":"YouTube's New Hue","stance":"reference","why":"Song inspired by the Pepsi design presentation","quote":"That song is directly inspired by the Pepsi nonsense","text":"That song is directly inspired by the Pepsi nonsense; the lyrics and backing video [1] have some direct quotes. Definitely the first thing that comes to mind each time a multi-billion company decides to change their logo and nobody likes it. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32064748,"author":"ianseyer","month":"2022-07","story":"Pepsi: Breathtaking Design Strategy (2008) [pdf]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"A funny parody song of this presentation by the legendary Neil Cicierega","text":"A funny parody song of this presentation by the legendary Neil Cicierega (potter puppet pals, ultimate battle of ultimate destiny, brodyquest, mouth sounds, etc…) [VIDEO]"}]},"g0TaYhjpOfo":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to illustration of Moravec's paradox and as a baseline for how far robotics has come since 2015.","q":{"x":"One of my favourite videos is this compilation of robots falling (failing) during the simplest tasks at the 2015 DARPA challenge","i":16153620,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16153620,"author":"FabHK","month":"2018-01","story":"Sussman anomaly","stance":"rec","why":"Compilation of robots failing at simple tasks in 2015 DARPA challenge","quote":"One of my favourite videos is this compilation of robots falling (failing) during the simplest tasks at the 2015 DARPA challenge","text":"Moravec's paradox - stuff we thought is hard (chess, derivatives) is easy, while stuff we think is easy (face recognition, getting out of a car, opening a door) is really hard. [LINK] One of my favourite videos is this compilation of robots falling (failing) during the simplest tasks at the 2015 DARPA challenge: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43436273,"author":"marcusverus","month":"2025-03","story":"Boston Dynamics shows off another major leap in humanoid mobility","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as slapstick state of humanoid robots in 2015","quote":"","text":"I remember thinking that the humanoid robotics revolution was imminent after seeing ASIMO walk around 2010[0], but after observing the slapstick comedy that was the DARPA Robotics Challenge around 2015[1], it seemed that we had actually made zero progress. But I haven't felt that way in years! My feeling at this point is that the best humanoid robots have, from a hardware perspective , surpassed the \"minimally viable product\" threshold. By this I mean that they are physically capable of completing enough tasks that would make them worthwhile a worthwhile product. They are physically capable of walking the dog, folding the laundry, picking up the living room, bringing in the groceries, etc. It's possible that there are reliability issues to address, but given that the newest generation of bots are fully electric, it's hard to imagine that this is a more difficult problem than those they've already solved. At this point, we're just waiting on the software to catch up. And surely this problem seems far more doable today than it did 20 years ago, given all the progress we've made in machine vision and machine learning! We'll know that the robotics revolution is truly imminent when the big players start pouring capital into development. Tesla has already joined the fray. I expect more will follow in the next few years. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40362817,"author":"numpad0","month":"2024-05","story":"Unitree G1 Humanoid Agent","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as 2010s state of autonomous humanoid robots","quote":"","text":"It was always software. Humanoid robots could walk up and down stairs and use basic tools, in preprogrammed lab conditions and/or by manual teleoperations since 90s[1], and in theory autonomously since 2010s[2]. Nowadays they just can, thanks to NVIDIA CUDA, but grabbing objects is still sketchy[3]. 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO] 3: [LINK]"},{"id":40066080,"author":"FabHK","month":"2024-04","story":"An electric new era for Atlas","stance":"reference","why":"Baseline for robotics progress since 2015","quote":"","text":"We've come a long way from the 2015 DARPA Challenge, where the robots succeeded only in falling down: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":40066146,"author":"FabHK","month":"2024-04","story":"An electric new era for Atlas","stance":"reference","why":"Baseline for robotics progress since 2015","quote":"","text":"The progress since the 2015 DARPA challenge (where robots succeeded mostly in falling down) is impressive. Less than a decade! [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":30876732,"author":"FabHK","month":"2022-04","story":"The singularity is close?","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates Moravec's paradox with robots failing at walking and doors","quote":"One of my favourite demonstration of that was the 2015 DARPA robot challenge","text":"That reminds me of Moravec's paradox: What we thought was hard and difficult (and indicative of intelligence), such as playing Chess and computing derivatives, is actually quite easy, while a lot of things that we consider trivially easy (distinguish a dog from a cat, pick up an egg, read some squiggly text) are quite hard. [LINK] One of my favourite demonstration of that was the 2015 DARPA robot challenge, where one robot after the other failed at such difficult tasks as walking and opening a door. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25886448,"author":"Judgmentality","month":"2021-01","story":"2 times 3 can sometimes equal 7 with Android's Neural Network API","stance":"reference","why":"Shows robots' limits in uncontrolled environments","quote":"","text":"> I'd trust a machine to drive more accurately than most people I see on the highway. Humans aren't special, in fact more often than not we're sloppy, subject to fatigue, and a whole bunch of other negative things. Humans are much, much, much more capable than the absolute state-of-the-art robots when it comes to doing things in an uncontrolled environment. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15353331,"author":"FabHK","month":"2017-09","story":"Real-Time Recognition of Handwritten Chinese Characters","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates Moravec's paradox with robots failing to walk and open doors","quote":"","text":"slightly off-topic, but the images in the article illustrate it beautifully: I think many people don't realise (I didn't) what an amazing skill it is to be able to read handwriting (cursive). I took it for granted to be able to read hand-written German, English, Spanish, and totally forgot that it must have taken me some time as a kid to acquire that skill (beyond reading and writing generally). However, when I studied Chinese for a bit, the (printed) characters are tricky to start with, but reading a fluent writer's handwriting (\"unconstrained\", as they call it in the article) is nigh impossible. I think stroke order is hugely important, in that it forms the conventions for fast abbreviated writing. For example, the \"mouth\" radical, a square, \"口\", when written quickly basically looks like a lower-case \"b\", because the left lower stroke comes first, then top and right in one stroke, then the bottom stroke. More complicated characters basically become a squiggle, and it's really magical, if you think about it, that you can just read it. I mean, look at figures 5, 6, 7 in that article. For me, a nice illustration of Moravec's paradox of AI [1]: what we think is difficult (chess, differentiation, ...) is easy, and what we think is easy (reading handwriting, putting an egg in the fridge, walking (!) and opening a door [2]) is hard... [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] (\"A Compilation of Robots Falling Down at the [2015] DARPA Robotics Challenge\")"},{"id":13471547,"author":"felippee","month":"2017-01","story":"Toyota's Gill Pratt on Self-Driving Cars and the Reality of Full Autonomy","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates missing low-level knowledge in robots","quote":"","text":"Yup, there is a boatload of low level knowledge missing from any AI that we build. It is painfully visible with robots (and autonomous cars are robots as well), see DARPA challenge video which was then lead BTW by Gill Pratt: [VIDEO] A lot of stuff we take for granted such a supporting oneself by a nearby wall when loosing balance, is completely out of reach of todays \"AI\"."},{"id":9674761,"author":"Vorcin83","month":"2015-06","story":"Korea’s Team KAIST Wins the 2015 Darpa Robotics Challenge","stance":"reference","why":"Shows robots with hardcoded motions failing at tasks","quote":"","text":"This [1] list of failures shows that some robots seem to \"just\" have hardcoded motions, for example for turning the valve. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"hsIpQ7YguGE":{"wy":"Cited in debates over pay equity law as Friedman's free-market argument on discrimination.","q":{"x":"Milton explains it best","i":14333453,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14333453,"author":"patrickaljord","month":"2017-05","story":"I spent two weeks delivering for Uber Eats and made $4.40 per hour","stance":"rec","why":"Friedman explains how markets penalize discrimination.","quote":"Milton explains it best","text":"> When an employer chooses a white man over a black/brown/fuschia man even though the wage it's the same then it's not the law that is racist. It's the employer. Yes, but where there is no minimum wage, the racist employer has to pay a price for being racist because people of color are selling cheaper labor than white people, so if racist employer wants to hire white people only he will have to pay a higher price. With minimum wage laws, racist employers don't pay a price for being racist because whites get the same minimum wage, this is why it encourages racism. Milton explains it best [VIDEO]"},{"id":25412816,"author":"trentnix","month":"2020-12","story":"Evidence that investors penalize female founders for lack of industry fit","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman on competitive advantage of hiring underpaid groups.","quote":"if one group of people could be every bit as productive as another at a fraction of the cost","text":"That's precisely the point Milton Friedman made regarding the 'wage gap' when asked about it 40 years ago. He noted that if one group of people could be every bit as productive as another at a fraction of the cost, that's a compelling competitive advantage. But as others became aware of the advantage, the overlooked group of people would find themselves more sought after. And that would drive up their wages and increase their opportunities. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15505866,"author":"jxramos","month":"2017-10","story":"New law bans California employers from asking applicants their prior salary","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman's exchange with students on equal pay.","quote":"this exchange Milton Friedman had with some college students","text":"That questioning of the gap being that big makes me think about this exchange Milton Friedman had with some college students in like the 70s or something regarding equal pay for equal work. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12823501,"author":"redthrowaway","month":"2016-10","story":"General questions about the Airbnb Community Commitment","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman's argument against equal pay laws.","quote":"Friedman's arguments against equal pay laws","text":"It's sad to see the tech community, which used to be very libertarian, move towards a hard-left progressive view of social issues. It wasn't that long ago that Friedman's arguments against equal pay laws[1] would have held sway here. The idea that the market punishes bigotry and so can be relied upon to advance freedom and equality used to be a core belief of the tech community. Now, it seems companies are falling over themselves to come up with ever more progressive and authoritarian solutions to problems that have only been complained about in the tech media--driven largely by people who care far more about culture wars than they do about tech. It's hard to see these developments and not feel that something important has been lost. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"uFvokQUHh08":{"wy":"HN people post it as a fun musical companion in threads on the Banach-Tarski theorem.","q":{"x":"i feel obliged to point everyone at this Banach-Tarski related video","i":4896449,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4896449,"author":"madmaze","month":"2012-12","story":"Banach-Tarski paradox","stance":"rec","why":"Music video about the Banach-Tarski paradox.","quote":"i feel obliged to point everyone at this Banach-Tarski related video","text":"On the topic of Banach-Tarski, i feel obliged to point everyone at this Banach-Tarski related video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40802127,"author":"nayuki","month":"2024-06","story":"The Point of the Banach Tarski Theorem (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"(music video)","text":"Vsauce: The Banach–Tarski Paradox (24m13s) [2015-08-01]: [LINK] (explainer video) Matematikrevyen: Matematikrevyen 2011: Banach-Tarski (3m42s) [2011-12-30]: [VIDEO] (music video)"},{"id":34484580,"author":"sshine","month":"2023-01","story":"The Point of the Banach Tarski Theorem","stance":"reference","why":"Student revue parody turning Barbara Streisand into Banach-Tarski.","quote":"Th student revue at University of Copenhagen made a music video ten years ago","text":"Th student revue at University of Copenhagen made a music video ten years ago: [VIDEO] Instead of saying \"Barbara Streisand\", they say \"Banach-Tarski\" and a number of oranges double:"}]},"w5ebcowAJD8":{"wy":"HN points to it as a recent, well-made explanation of QR code structure and error correction.","q":{"x":"Derek Muller just did an interesting take on QR codes and their history","i":41897912,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41897912,"author":"nhatcher","month":"2024-10","story":"The history of the barcode","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting take on QR codes and their history","quote":"Derek Muller just did an interesting take on QR codes and their history","text":"Derek Muller just did an interesting take on QR codes and their history on his popular channel veritasium: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44270466,"author":"gukov","month":"2025-06","story":"Show HN: Qrkey – Offline private key backup on paper","stance":"reference","why":"QR code degradation tolerance","quote":"Here's a Veritasium video on QR codes","text":"Yep, if I'm using a physical medium like paper I want to allow for some degradation. Here's a Veritasium video on QR codes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42168038,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2024-11","story":"Creating a QR Code step by step","stance":"reference","why":"Recent Veritasium video on QR codes","quote":"Recent Veritasium video on the topic","text":"Recent Veritasium video on the topic, \"I used to hate QR codes. But they're actually genius\": * [VIDEO]"}]},"-ccNkksrfls":{"wy":"HN uses it as footage of a famous officiating failure, linked in Maradona obituary and video-review threads.","m":[{"id":45355973,"author":"dylan604","month":"2025-09","story":"MLB approves robot umpires for 2026 as part of challenge system","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of the incident, analyzed for offside and officiating.","quote":"For those unfamiliar","text":"For those unfamiliar: [VIDEO] I just watched the replay, and I'm convinced VAR would rule it offside. So the hand of god would be irrelevant. The AR on the far side of the pitch is just out of frame at the crucial moment, so he was clearly behind the play. That's a position you never want to find yourself as an AR. I always found the AR role much more demanding than the referee. Keeping that offside line is difficult at best and nearly impossible if you're 30+ something trying to keep up with fit professional athletes in peak fitness in their 20s. Now, after that call was blown, again due to the framing, it is hard to see where the ref was looking. You can see his last position easily 30 yards from goal while Maradona makes contact about 8 yards from goal. There are at least 15 players in between the ref and the play at that point. Also, a very telling thing is that there was no conversation between the AR and the ref. On contentious plays, you'd see the ref back peddle to the AR (never take your eyes of the pitch) and have a chat about who saw what. I don't know if the ref crew had radios back in 1986, but the fact that the ref did not think it was worthy of a chat with his AR who would have had a different angle to view it says a lot about the ref's thinking. If the AR had seen it and wanted to chat with the ref, he would have stood still with his flag raised. If he felt the goal was good, he would keep his flag down and make a little run up the touchline towards midfield. "},{"id":25211823,"author":"aazaa","month":"2020-11","story":"Diego Maradona has died","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of the Hand of God incident.","quote":"the \"Hand of God\" incident is an entertaining read","text":"Not sure why this is here (don't follow soccer and never heard of Maradona), but the \"Hand of God\" incident is an entertaining read: [LINK] and watch: [VIDEO] On second thought, this was probably a hack of sorts."},{"id":9696246,"author":"smtddr","month":"2015-06","story":"Google: 90% of our engineers use the software you wrote (Homebrew), but...","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Disclaimer: GoogleFanBoy here, feel free to ignore or downvote. So, I've interviewed with Google twice. Once was 3 years ago, the other was like 2 weeks ago. They contacted me. The way I see Google's interviews is like referee in Football (soccer or \"Futbol\") . Sure, you need a certain amount of skill to play in the World Cup but you winning or losing can, and often enough does, come down to a controversial referee call. You end up losing out to a team that did a handball - [VIDEO] , but that's just how it is. What makes me like watching soccer so much is the same thing that excites me about Google's interview process. Yes, there is heartbreak and anger. Just like World cup fans get angry when their team loses because they were denied a point for an off-sides call even when the player was nowhere near off-sides. --- Is Google's interview process fair? Nope. --- Would I subject myself to their futbol-referee style of judging candidates again? You bet. --- Do I think they should make their process more fair? Nope, let the drama and _justified_ rant posts continue. Just like I want the unfairness in futbol to stay as is. I was one of the people against putting the microchip inside the ball to know for sure if it crosses the goal line. I want the drama of a ref having to call it and sometimes getting it wrong. I know people, especially on HN, love reliable & repeatable. I do too, except when it comes to dealing with humans."}]},"6bODiZ5bP84":{"wy":"Linked when retro hardware, 8088 or 386 machines, or oddball AliExpress computers come up.","m":[{"id":43773344,"author":"philips","month":"2025-04","story":"Restoring Old Software for Child Learning Safety","stance":"reference","why":"Weird retro hardware on AliExpress","quote":"","text":"I love this idea. There are some weird things showing up on Aliexpress but are limited in various ways: [VIDEO] It does seem like emulation is the way."},{"id":43522775,"author":"wcfields","month":"2025-03","story":"MS-DOS and Windows 3.11 still run train dashboards at German railway (Jan'24)","stance":"reference","why":"Oddball retro-equivalent hardware on AliExpress","quote":"","text":"Retro gaming enthusiasts have been creating mini-PCs first starting with industrial computers (386) built for CNC machines, and have recently been getting custom PCBs made like the Pixel x86: [LINK] You can find some oddball stuff on AliExpress that’s probably made to replace as close as possible 1to1 EOL equipment: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43055221,"author":"jsheard","month":"2025-02","story":"Msxbook OneChipMSX MSX2 Computer","stance":"reference","why":"Coverage of same company's portable 386/8086 machines","quote":"LGR covered them a while ago","text":"I thought this concept looked familiar and indeed it's by the same company which makes those portable 386 and 8086 machines. LGR covered them a while ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36370397,"author":"justsomehnguy","month":"2023-06","story":"They just pirated my open source 8088 BIOS","stance":"reference","why":"Review of the machine","quote":"LGR review of the machine [2].","text":"Additional context in [0], [1]. LGR review of the machine [2]. I would recommend reading [1] because it confirms the GPL violation but paints interesting picture of the reason. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] \"So I Bought Those Weird New AliExpress Retro PCs…\""}]},"9TIBeso4abU":{"wy":"Cited in autonomy debates as evidence that autonomous take-off aircraft exist and as an example of vision-based line following.","q":{"x":"Sure, no _commercial_ plane can take off with autopilot, but such planes exist[1].","i":31711568,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":45386795,"author":"rkomorn","month":"2025-09","story":"Demand for human radiologists is at an all-time high","stance":"reference","why":"Airbus ATTOL project showing autonomous takeoff work","quote":"this video is arguably the most fun one","text":"Not only that but they are even less capable of taking off on their own (see the work done by Airbus' ATTOL project [0] on what some of the more recent successes are). So I'm not sure what \"planes can land on their own\" gets us anyway even if autopilot on modern airliners can do an awful lot on their own (including following flight plans in ways that are more advanced than before). The Garmin Autoland basically announces \"my pilot is incapacitated and the plane is going to land itself at <insert a nearby runway>\" without asking for landing clearance (which is very cool in and of itself but nowhere near what anyone would consider autonomous). [0] [VIDEO] (among other things, but this video is arguably the most fun one) Edit: and yes maybe the \"pilots are basically superfluous now\" misconception is a pet peeve for me (and I'm guessing parent as well)"},{"id":31711568,"author":"l33t2328","month":"2022-06","story":"US moves closer to recalling Tesla’s self-driving software","stance":"reference","why":"Example that autonomous takeoff aircraft exist","quote":"Sure, no _commercial_ plane can take off with autopilot, but such planes exist[1].","text":"_sigh_ Yes. It can. You’re intentionally misrepresenting reality for what I suspect is some ideological fondness of Tesla. Sure, no _commercial_ plane can take off with autopilot, but such planes exist[1]. But, saying Cat IIIc is required for hands off landing is silly. It’s required for hands off landing in the absolute worst conditions. It’s needed only when conditions are so bad that you can’t safely taxi and you’ll be stuck on the runway. That’s why it’s not used. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28570373,"author":"rtkwe","month":"2021-09","story":"Video of Tesla FSD almost hitting pedestrian receives DMCA takedown","stance":"reference","why":"Airbus autonomous taxi test appears focused on line following","quote":"It looks like the cameras are entirely focused on line following.","text":"They're still in a vastly more controlled environment than cars and moving at much lower speeds as well. If an auto taxiing plane has to avoid another airport vehicle something has gone massively wrong. Judging by this video [0] I'm not sure they're even worrying about collisions and are counting on the combination of pilots and ATC ground controllers to avoid issues while taxiing. It looks like the cameras are entirely focused on line following. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"FL7yD-0pqZg":{"wy":"Only posted as a joke in threads about iPhone versus Android; no one recommends it for content.","m":[{"id":6061501,"author":"GoodIntentions","month":"2013-07","story":"All three major Russian carriers dump iPhone","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":">>But Nexus 4 is not an iPhone. You might appreciate this: [VIDEO] NSFW ( or as NSFW as foul mouthed teddy bears can be )"},{"id":1665873,"author":"slmichalk","month":"2010-09","story":"Is Android Only Surging Because Apple Is Letting It?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"But the iPhone does exist. And I simply can’t bring myself to use an Android phone when I know a superior device is out there.\" WTF? That line reminds me of this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1486267,"author":"martythemaniak","month":"2010-07","story":"The difference between iPhone + Android:  Great since day one ","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"You know why people make stuff like this? [VIDEO] Because you can't just get an Android phone and use it and be happy with it. There's always some hipster or yuppie or desperate-to-be-cool nerd that'll start going on and on and on about how amazingly great their iPhone is, was and always will be and how they have taste which you, as a non-Jobs product buyer, obviously don't. A lot of people like me switched from the iPhone to Android because we like it better, get over it. Also, there'll be more people that like and use Android than iOS, get over that as well. And since we're reaching for snarky old jokes (ie desktop linux), how about this one: What can Android users do that iPhone users can't? Shut the fuck up."}]},"G2y8Sx4B2Sk":{"wy":"Used purely as a joke reply when someone misuses a term.","m":[{"id":47803845,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2026-04","story":"30 Years of HPC: many hardware advances, little adoption of new languages","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> realtime computing Even with HDL defined accelerators, that statement may not mean what people assume. =3 [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46279667,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-12","story":"Adafruit: Arduino’s Rules Are ‘Incompatible With Open Source’","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Arduino 1kHz busy-loop and ISR designs is not real-time or necessarily guaranteed latency. RTLinux or FreeRTOS are guaranteed-latency OS, that can reduce timing jitter. \"Real-time\" is another term hijacked by people that think it means the same thing. It was a disservice to students, but thankfully FPGA or Raspberry Pico io DMA with mcu parallelism can teach people to understand clock domain crossing. Part of the Arduino appeal, was convincing people the mcu and application SoC problem domains were the same. They are not, and never were... Hence the new Arduino board dual chip layout is twice as bad in some ways. Cheers =3 [VIDEO] risc v -- a new hope, but 20 years too late stm32 -- a solid competitor to Microchip/AVR, but 14 years too late esp32 -- FreeRTOS with hardware flash paging can pose an issue, but at least it already doesn't need Arduino software lol"},{"id":38429730,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2023-11","story":"Brother [printers] have gotten to where they are now, by NOT innovating","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Most models are reliable, compatible with *nix network drivers, and don't pull shenanigans when you need them to work. The only issue I saw is the toner color match on laser models could be a bit better. Still highly recommended for anything except photos. \"Innovating\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":28554123,"author":"ChrisMarshallNY","month":"2021-09","story":"What's Wrong with Tech Hiring","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It's a funny thing being in an industry where people retire not because they can't do the work at a professional level, but because getting a job is such a PITA. I feel that this is a desired result. I am pretty convinced that folks like to have an \"ideal culture\" at their workplaces. I hear the term \"cultural fit\" so often, that I guess it means something that I'm missing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28367461,"author":"ChrisMarshallNY","month":"2021-08","story":"Software Development Waste","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Well, I'm not sure that I \"misunderstand\" \"MVP,\" because, after the dust has settled, and the smoke has cleared, a significant percentage of what is out there, and carries the label \"MVP,\" is duct-tape-and-baling-wire-last-checkin-five-seconds-before-ship dross. I know what I consider \"MVP\" to be, ideally , and that pretty much jives with your description. It's just that I don't think most folks actually do it. It's like \"Agile,\" in that respect. As someone that practices high-Quality development, I am regularly laughed at and told that I'm \"wasting my time,\" doing Quality coding. Words come to mean whatever their most common usage implies, so I think that both of these wonderful words now mean something other than what they used to mean. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13303599,"author":"rarrrrrr","month":"2017-01","story":"SpiderOakONE – Zero Knowledge Cloud Storage","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"SpiderOak founder here... A few cryptographers have noticed SpiderOak's marketing term Zero Knowledge is inconsistent with the academic definition. Maybe it doesn't mean what we think it means[1]? SpiderOak was one of the first companies to use this phrase commercially and the need has only grown stronger. At the heart of the issue is the difficulty for end users to decipher the terms cloud vendors use to describe their security. Doing so would require discrimination between transport encryption, data encryption, meta data encryption, encryption at rest vs. in motion, and then most importantly evaluate key management and access. This vocabulary is foreign to most folks. Vendors often exploit the inaccessibility of these topics to make a series of statements that, while often factually correct individually, together create a false sense of privacy. SpiderOak launched a online backup product for Linux, Mac, and Windows in 2007. The competitors were companies like Xdrive, Mozy, Carbonite and SugarSync. Each claimed that customer data was fully encrypted. Even the most credible journalists writing for well funded publications with fact checking budgets were fooled and repeated these misleading claims to end users. [2] In 2009 when Dropbox launched, they made misleading claims about the encryption of customer files and their internal ability to access customer's data or provide that data to 3rd parties, leading to a well publicized FTC deceptive trade practices complaint. [3] The "},{"id":8683860,"author":"peterwwillis","month":"2014-12","story":"CoreOS is building a container runtime, Rocket","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Two thoughts: 1. Competition? How can open source software be in competition with anything? It's free, its source code is there; if people want it they'll use it, if not they won't. Why would anyone care what other projects are doing or saying? Just build your tools how you want and go on with life. (Unless you're building your tools specifically to make money, in which case I guess PR and 'competition' does matter a lot) 2. On Twitter you suggested things should be ' composable to the extreme' ..... using plugins and drivers. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6261500,"author":"EGreg","month":"2013-08","story":"Wait.for: Sequential programming for Node.js (beta)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't get it, console.log(wait.for(fs.readfile,'/etc/passwd')); won't that execute console.log immediately, whereas fs.readfile will return the data later? \"I don't think it means what you think it means\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":3323079,"author":"swdunlop","month":"2011-12","story":"Thoughts on Python 3","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I had a similar response, reading the article. Cue Inigo Montoya voice: \"You keep using that word. I do not think it means what you think it means.\" [VIDEO]"}]},"H-gfxjAaZg0":{"wy":"Cited in threads on dating, loneliness and the shortage of eligible partners as a song that captures the dilemma.","m":[{"id":18564334,"author":"RJIb8RBYxzAMX9u","month":"2018-11","story":"‘I’m Broke and Mostly Friendless, and I’ve Wasted My Whole Life’","stance":"reference","why":"Song about loneliness and lack of prospects","quote":"So, basically this[0] and this[1].","text":"So, basically this[0] and this[1]. If the author just needed to vent and sympathetic ears, then the columnist's 2000+ words platitude is fine. However, if she really wants out of the situation, then a more pragmatic[2] -- tldr; treat finding a SO like a job; she already professed that she's found no fulfillment on her current job, so might as well -- approach would be more useful. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":16514955,"author":"Tade0","month":"2018-03","story":"Why Women Choose Differently at Work","stance":"reference","why":"Song illustrating shortage of eligible single men","quote":"This song[0] illustrates this point beautifully.","text":"You're ignoring a lot of subtelties here. > I'd love to live in a place where this were true. Which cities exactly have such thing going on? Warsaw is a great example, but ironically these womens' standards are pretty high - all in all they didn't leave their 30k-something-souls towns for the Big City to marry a man who doesn't enjoy a decent social status himself. > Or is there? Also, either there is stigma for being single woman or there are vast numbers of single women. Both at the same time cannot be true in even somewhat stable system, and it's hard to argue that Polish cities are in total chaos. Anecdata: my sister was born in 86' and is currently single and childless, just like a few of her friends. On one hand they're pressured to do something about this, on the other there aren't many eligible bachelors available because well, they're taken. This song[0] illustrates this point beautifully. Hell, the problem is so visible right now that a TV show dedicated to making couples out of (most of the time)well-off farmers and city-dwelling women[1] was created. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":14784764,"author":"technofiend","month":"2017-07","story":"The real reason women freeze their eggs","stance":"reference","why":"Song about women suddenly realizing they have no partner","quote":"cover the dilemma some women find themselves in related to suddenly realizing you have no partner and no prospects","text":"Garfunkel and Oates cover the dilemma some women find themselves in related to suddenly realizing you have no partner and no prospects [VIDEO]"}]},"Kr7AONv3FSg":{"wy":"Used purely as a joke or meme when threads touch on tracking and surveillance.","m":[{"id":28304721,"author":"nullc","month":"2021-08","story":"A catalog of naturally occurring images whose Apple NeuralHash is identical","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Like batman!","text":"So far the courts have determined that since providers invade their customer privacy of their own free will with no incentive or coersion by any government agency, that it is not a search by the goverment. It's just your friendly trillion dollar tech company putting on a mask and cape and engaging in a bit of vigilante fun. You know? Like batman! ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":27733656,"author":"Goety","month":"2021-07","story":"Underground fiber optics spy on humans moving above","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Paranoid memes below","text":"Agreed, old news. Also think about apartment buildings with coax [LINK] \"Human intruder detection with Leaky Coaxial cables for wide area surveillance system\" 2011 [LINK] Paranoid memes below [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":27126891,"author":"anonAndOn","month":"2021-05","story":"I mailed an AirTag and tracked its progress","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Batman showed us the future and Apple delivered.","text":"Anybody with $29 can track a target by leveraging everyone else's iPhone. Whether the target uses a phone or not is of no consequence. Batman showed us the future and Apple delivered.[0] [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"LXb3EKWsInQ":{"wy":"Used as a convenient high-resolution, high-framerate test clip for video decoders, YouTube format handling and power benchmarks.","m":[{"id":34338013,"author":"cb22","month":"2023-01","story":"Vulkan Video Decoding","stance":"reference","why":"4K 60 FPS test video used for power benchmarking","quote":"The test video was - which is 4k 60 FPS and got scaled to 1920x1080 for display.","text":"I just did some quick testing on my XPS 7390 2-in-1 (i7 1065G7) with Wayland: * Idle @ 50% brightness, WiFI on: 3.5W * mpv + hardware acceleration: 15W * Firefox + hardware acceleration: 19W * Firefox software decoding: 29W The test video was [VIDEO] - which is 4k 60 FPS and got scaled to 1920x1080 for display."},{"id":24835972,"author":"user5994461","month":"2020-10","story":"YouTube broke for 40% of the UK population after rolling out 60 FPS videos","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a 60 FPS-only video on YouTube","quote":"Check this for example, you should only have the choice between 60 FPS options.","text":"Videos that are submitted in 60 FPS are only available in 60 FPS. It's getting more common as the cameras as getting cheaper. Check this for example, you should only have the choice between 60 FPS options. [VIDEO] Videos that were submitted in 30 FPS remain as they were. It doesn't make sense to create new frames out of nothing indeed."},{"id":24844347,"author":"rasz","month":"2020-10","story":"YouTube broke for 40% of the UK population after rolling out 60 FPS videos","stance":"reference","why":"Example showing available formats including 4K 30fps via youtube-dl","quote":"","text":"30fps versions are still there on the server, its the default player hiding them from you. There is even 4K 30fps file available 313. D:\\_learning>youtube-dl.exe -F [VIDEO] [youtube] [VIDEO] Downloading webpage [info] Available formats for [VIDEO] format code extension resolution note 249 webm audio only tiny 57k , opus @ 50k (48000Hz), 1.89MiB 250 webm audio only tiny 75k , opus @ 70k (48000Hz), 2.53MiB 140 m4a audio only tiny 130k , m4a_dash container, mp4a.40.2@128k (44100Hz), 4.84MiB 251 webm audio only tiny 148k , opus @160k (48000Hz), 4.96MiB 394 mp4 256x144 144p HDR 88k , av01.0.00M.10.0.110.09.16.09.0, 30fps, video only, 2.34MiB 278 webm 256x144 144p 97k , webm container, vp9, 30fps, video only, 3.38MiB 160 mp4 256x144 144p 111k , avc1.4d400c, 30fps, video only, 2.87MiB 395 mp4 426x240 240p HDR 191k , av01.0.00M.10.0.110.09.16.09.0, 30fps, video only, 4.33MiB 242 webm 426x240 240p 233k , vp9, 30fps, video only, 6.89MiB 330 webm 256x144 144p60 HDR 243k , vp9.2, 60fps, video only, 7.96MiB 133 mp4 426x240 240p 245k , avc1.4d4015, 30fps, video only, 5.86MiB 396 mp4 640x360 360p HDR 403k , av01.0.01M.10.0.110.09.16.09.0, 30fps, video only, 8.67MiB 243 webm 640x360 360p 435k , vp9, 30fps, video only, 12.85MiB 331 webm 426x240 240p60 HDR 504k , vp9.2, 60fps, video only, 16.65MiB 134 mp4 640x360 360p 633k , avc1.4d401e, 30fps, video only, 15.49MiB 397 mp4 854x480 480p HDR 735k , av01.0.04M.10.0.110.09.16.09.0, 30fps, video only, 15.80MiB 244 webm 854x480 480p 857k , vp9, 30fp"},{"id":20003044,"author":"lousken","month":"2019-05","story":"Firefox brings smooth video playback with the fastest AV1 decoder","stance":"reference","why":"4K AV1 test video for checking decoder performance","quote":"since then they also did a 4k one if you wanna test it","text":"it was probably encoded at a time when they didn't do 4k av1 tests but only 1080p, but since then they also did a 4k one if you wanna test it [VIDEO]"}]},"NuAMczraBIM":{"wy":"HN cites it in right-to-repair and EV battery discussions as an example of independent Tesla repair.","q":{"x":"showing the silliness he has to go through to be able to repair salvaged Teslas","i":27815318,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27815318,"author":"mdaniel","month":"2021-07","story":"Tesla’s $16k Quote for a $700 Fix Is Why Right to Repair Matters","stance":"reference","why":"Hurdles repairing salvaged Teslas","quote":"showing the silliness he has to go through to be able to repair salvaged Teslas","text":"There's a related YT channel \"Rich Rebuilds\" showing the silliness he has to go through to be able to repair salvaged Teslas: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27627005,"author":"hardwaresofton","month":"2021-06","story":"Help All data in WD mybook live gone and owner password unknown","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla repair shop specializing in salvaged cars","quote":"He runs a channel called Rich Rebuilds[1]","text":"So this reminds me of the dude that was doing repairs on teslas from a few years ago -- The Rogue Tesla Mechanic Resurrecting Salvaged Cars [0]. He runs a channel called Rich Rebuilds[1] (I'm not a subscriber) a company in New Hampshire that specializes in repairing Teslas, and has a recent video on his relationship with Tesla (and the community of fans)[2] (which I haven't seen yet, but watching now). I don't follow this channel but I do not remember Tesla being particularly encouraging of the trend, similar to other defacto closed ecosystems. They're definitely not egregious but just a little bit closed-by-default. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":23481054,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2020-06","story":"Used EV Batteries Could Power Tomorrow's Solar Farms","stance":"reference","why":"Mechanic salvaging Tesla batteries and parts","quote":"This guy has salvaged batteries and tons of other parts from junkyards","text":"Used EV batteries could just be used in cars, but Tesla makes it incredibly difficult to fix their crashed vehicles. It's almost impossible for regular consumers to buy OEM parts unless it's for their specific purchased car. This guy has salvaged batteries and tons of other parts from junkyards: [VIDEO]"}]},"OXtq4a8829g":{"wy":"Linked as footage of the arrest of an Australian online journalist, in threads about press freedom and state power.","q":{"x":"they captured footage of the arrest itself. This is obviously only one side, but here it is","i":28023721,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28452289,"author":"grecy","month":"2021-09","story":"Australia’s new mass surveillance mandate","stance":"reference","why":"Anti-terror police arresting a young online journalist","quote":"","text":"> These new laws being introduced if used in the spirit they are intended could be good We already know they won't. Here's [1] the Australian anti-terrorism Police arresting a young online journalist at his house, because he was asking probing questions to a politician that said politician did not like. Video of the incident clearly shows the Politician lied multiple times in his police statement (which is a crime). Yet the anti-terrorism taskforce was used to arrest this person at his house. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28023721,"author":"RileyJames","month":"2021-08","story":"FBI Seized $900K from Safe Deposit Box on 'Pure Conjecture,' Federal Judge Says","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of the arrest itself, one side only","quote":"they captured footage of the arrest itself. This is obviously only one side, but here it is","text":"For those interested, they captured footage of the arrest itself. This is obviously only one side, but here it is: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27590186,"author":"9387367","month":"2021-06","story":"Australia opposes Great Barrier Reef ′in danger′ listing","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Australian Environmental Minister Sussan Ley: \"I agree that global climate change is the single biggest threat to the world's reefs but it is wrong, in our view, to single out the best-managed reef in the world for an 'in danger' listing,\" > warming waters led to the loss of half of its corals since 1995. Australia seems to be deeply corrupt. Last week a 21 year old YouTube editor for the Friendlyjordies channel was arrested by some sort of special anti terror unit after approaching the New South Wales Deputy Premier on the street, this was recorded and it’s shameful that a politician would have anyone arrested for simply approaching them. [1] [2] Just so happens that Friendlyjordies have been exposing potential corruption links between the New South Wales Deputy Premier and corporations and donors with link to his political party. [3] [4] > “MFS is a farming research cooperative that was established by Richard Taylor, who is the brother of the federal energy minister, Angus Taylor, and the brother-in-law of the NSW Nationals MP Bronwyn Taylor. Until 2019 Richard was the chair of MFS...MFS has been the recipient of numerous state and federal grants, totalling well over $800,000.” > “An estimates inquiry was told earlier this year that documents supporting another $200m community grants program had been shredded.” > “...Barilaro pushed the Department of Industry to support another Taylor family project, the Country Universities Centre, even though it failed a cost-benefit an"}]},"R2idkNdKqpQ":{"wy":"HN cites it as Crockford's main explanation of Misty and his take on the actor model.","q":{"x":"had explained in his famous talk","i":38823326,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38823326,"author":"brabel","month":"2023-12","story":"Misty Programming Language","stance":"reference","why":"Crockford's talk explaining the Misty language ideas.","quote":"had explained in his famous talk","text":"This is the spec for the language Douglas Crockford (author of the book \"JavaScript: The Good Parts\", the JSON specification[1], JSLint[2]) had explained in his famous talk: \"The Next Programming Language\"[3]. The \"big things\" in the language are the Actor model, favouring immutability and capabilities-based security. Presumably, there will be a flashy website later that actually motivates why you should use this language and what's cool about it. Notice this looks similar to the JSON spec website[4]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":38215523,"author":"zoogeny","month":"2023-11","story":"Switching to Elixir","stance":"reference","why":"Crockford on Misty language and message-passing actors.","quote":"see this recent video from Crockford on his new Misty programming language","text":"I have been watching Elixir YouTube videos pretty much every day for the last few weeks. I guess there was an Elixir conference recently and after I watched a couple, YouTube has been sending me a consistent stream of Elixir content. I really want to try out this language. I love the idea of Erlang but the few times I've had to deal with it (an ejabberd chat server was one) I found it to be a bit too quirky. Every video I've seen with Joe Armstrong leads me to believe he was an under-appreciated genius. Isolated processes and message passing (Actors) seem a good candidate for the future of distributed programming (see this recent video from Crockford on his new Misty programming language [1] - notice how the questions were about Elixir/Erlang). I love pattern matching and elixir uses this to a high degree. However, I am not 100% sold. Clearly this community punches above its weight, and the BEAM is obviously no slouch and is a brilliant choice for a platform to build upon. But I get concerned looking at the magic of LiveView (and LiveBook). My experience is that if something sounds too good to be true, it often is. A lot of the jargon is also totally unfamiliar to me - things like GenServers and other esoteric kinds of platform specific knowledge. It feels like stepping into a one-way portal, I'm not sure any knowledge in that universe is transferable. There is a huge risk in niche languages and platforms. Hard to hire for, hard to find libraries for, hard to find blogs/tutor"},{"id":34277684,"author":"justin66","month":"2023-01","story":"Carl Hewitt has died [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Crockford as torchbearer for the Actor model.","quote":"","text":"> Who is left to carry the torch to illuminate millions of developers who have no idea or have never heard of the Actor model? Douglas Crockford. [VIDEO]"}]},"RvZ-667CEdo":{"wy":"Cited as a prescient example of predicting the future, though AT&T did not deliver it.","q":{"x":"present both an optimistic and fairly accurate view of what the future would look like.","i":45805637,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":47127697,"author":"WorldMaker","month":"2026-02","story":"How close are we to a vision for 2010?","stance":"reference","why":"Prescient ad campaign, though AT&T did not deliver it.","quote":"It was very prescient but also the joke is that the commercials ended with","text":"A possible catalyst might have been the 1993 AT&T \"You Will\" Ad Campaign. It was very prescient but also the joke is that the commercials ended with \"and the company that will bring it to you will be AT&T\", which did not pan out in the ways that ad campaign thought. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45805637,"author":"michaelbuckbee","month":"2025-11","story":"AI's Dial-Up Era","stance":"reference","why":"Optimistic and fairly accurate view of future technology.","quote":"present both an optimistic and fairly accurate view of what the future would look like.","text":"To your point, AT&T's \"You Will\" commercials started airing in 1993 and present both an optimistic and fairly accurate view of what the future would look like. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43972623,"author":"mnky9800n","month":"2025-05","story":"The great displacement is already well underway?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It used to be that you wouldn't be aware of what is going on in RU-net [1] or PTT [2] because you simply were not a Russian speaker or you weren't someone living in Taiwan speaking Chinese (yes modern Taiwanese people use a CLI app to login to a BBS in 2025, you can also do this with a web browser but where is the fun in that). So you simply were unaware that they even existed probably or if you do, you are like me and can only kind of speak Russian and get some of the memes, or you simply know about PTT because your Taiwanese friend told you about it. But now, everything is bifurcated within languages because there is orders of magnitude more content being generated and that content is algorithmically delivered to your eyes and ears based on your interactions with whatever platform you use (e.g., instagram, reddit) and maybe even across multiple platforms. So you likely don't see anything related to Kim Kardashian because you aren't flipping channels anymore through what is essentially \"static\" content. Instead you are scrolling a feed designed for you and you have never indicated you wanted to keep up with the Kardashians based on what you like and dislike in your feed. And so I think this bifurcation is combined with this kind of oily, artificial interactions you are talking about, and that makes the internet feel dead. Because the second you have a live experience, like going to a jazz bar without your phone and just hanging out and listening, everything feels so alive an"},{"id":32966061,"author":"ctoth","month":"2022-09","story":"Are You Ready for the Internet? (1994) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"1993 ad campaign showing future tech already imagined.","quote":"Check out this ad campaign from 1993","text":"This comment thread started when you pushed back against someone who said: > when it comes right down to core \"mind blowing\" new tech, how much have we actually improved on 1994? You provided examples of core ideas that you thought did not exist in 1994, I showed you how several of them absolutely did. If I had to make a list of core technologies that didn't exist in 1994, it might consist of things like CRISPR-Cas9, deep neural networks, electronic cigarettes, self-driving cars, personalized genetics, artificial meat. Stuff that had either not been conceived of or required technology that didn't exist at the time. Even then, you can find precursors to all of those things since tech development is path-dependent. But seriously, dynamic websites and online social communities and the like existed then :) As for smartphones there's a pretty clear lineage that includes early bag phones, pagers, primitive handhelds, Nokia bricks, The Danger Hiptop/Sidekick, Blackberry, and so on. Check out this ad campaign from 1993[0]: [0]: AT&T - 1993 \"You Will\" Ad Campaign [VIDEO]"}]},"UP1YsMlrfF0":{"wy":"Cited critically as an example of someone vouching for Mt. Gox just before its collapse.","m":[{"id":7189386,"author":"tlrobinson","month":"2014-02","story":"Mt.Gox Withdrawals","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It's a little creepy though, like he's being held at gunpoint, forced to read a statement...","text":"\"But, you only had Goxes word for that.\" Roger Ver, who is well respected in the Bitcoin world, also posted this video: [VIDEO] It's a little creepy though, like he's being held at gunpoint, forced to read a statement... I agree it's risky to keep money in a Bitcoin exchange that's showing signs of trouble."},{"id":19760851,"author":"apo","month":"2019-04","story":"Things Got Weird for Stablecoin Tether","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"many people (who should have known better) insisted that the exchange was secure and fully capitalized.","text":"It may be baffling that Tether hasn't collapsed yet. But consider the drawn-out drama of the Mt Gox collapse. It took weeks for Mt Gox to unwind. In the time leading up to the finale, many people (who should have known better) insisted that the exchange was secure and fully capitalized. For example, this guy: [VIDEO] When incredible spreads started appearing between Gox and competitors, the gullible asked \"How can I arbitrage this?\" rather than \"Where are the exits?\" Then they became irate at their inability to withdraw the funds they deposited to make a quick buck. There were others who simply refused to believe the facts staring them in the face. I expect the same to happen here. Settle in, this is going to take awhile to unwind."},{"id":12945178,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-11","story":"Another crypto-currency is born","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Roger Ver, a convicted felon who claimed Mt. Gox was sound[1], is involved with ZCash.","text":"(Article date: Oct 29th 2016). This is so last week. Zcash has already crashed. Peaked around $5000, today $127. Not only that, the anonymity feature didn't work. You can't send to \"z addresses\" only. This is claimed by the developers to be a bug they will fix. Since that was the main feature of this altcoin, and they shipped with it broken, they're either incompetent or disabled it to avoid regulatory problems. Roger Ver, a convicted felon who claimed Mt. Gox was sound[1], is involved with ZCash. What could possibly go wrong? ZCash is from Tel Aviv, headquarters of \"binary option\" scams. The entire Tel Aviv based binary options business has been exposed as a scam. [2] (Short version: a binary option is a bet against the house, not other investors, and the house cheats. Online binary option companies claimed to be in lots of places, including Trump Tower, but they're almost always really in Tel Aviv.) People in the \"binary option\" industry are now looking for new scams. \"Forex\" is one. There's a lot of interest in \"blockchains\" there.[3] This is a worry. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":10970926,"author":"ufo","month":"2016-01","story":"Zcash, an untraceable Bitcoin alternative, launches in alpha","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Roger made a video saying everything was fine and then mtgox went under a couple of months later.","text":"His mtgox video is emblematic[1]. Mtgox was at the time the largest bitcoin exchange in the world but it was insolvent due to stealing/misplacing customer funds. Roger made a video saying everything was fine and then mtgox went under a couple of months later. He then tried to spin that he was just talking about \"liquidity\" and not \"solvency\" but thats a really shitty excuse. [1] [VIDEO] ."},{"id":7289636,"author":"pmorici","month":"2014-02","story":"Mt.Gox's tweets are all gone","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apparently liquidity problems and solvency problems are different problems, who knew.","text":"Check out the recent update to this YouTube video... [VIDEO] Apparently liquidity problems and solvency problems are different problems, who knew."}]},"_fNp37zFn9Q":{"wy":"Cited as a funny illustration of how lag wrecks hand-eye coordination.","q":{"x":"Or have a similar experience to the living with lag video","i":36425317,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36425317,"author":"alfonsodev","month":"2023-06","story":"Developer tools to create spatial experiences for Apple Vision Pro now availabl","stance":"reference","why":"Shows hand-eye coordination problems from lagged view","quote":"Or have a similar experience to the living with lag video","text":"I thought so but what about the camera position not being same as your eyes, would you be able to drink from a cup of coffee and coordinate? Or have a similar experience to the living with lag video, this time no because time lag, but eye hand not coordinating. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9392464,"author":"e12e","month":"2015-04","story":"Android’s 10 Millisecond Problem explained","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"but still quite hilarious (Swedish audio)","text":"I came across this (which is a commercial so exaggerates the lag) -- but still quite hilarious (Swedish audio): [VIDEO]"},{"id":8622769,"author":"prawn","month":"2014-11","story":"Show HN: Projects from “Stupid Shit No One Needs and Terrible Ideas” Hackathon","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I laugh so hard when he tries to make a pancake.","text":"I agree. Here's a clever promo showing people trying to live with a Rift showing them a lagged version of reality. I laugh so hard when he tries to make a pancake. [VIDEO]"}]},"f1Zvo8N3G94":{"wy":"Mostly cited as a reference to Greenwald's statements, such as his comments on press freedom, rather than recommended outright.","m":[{"id":7216986,"author":"amirmc","month":"2014-02","story":"Glenn Greenwald's news site, The Intercept, is up","stance":"reference","why":"Greenwald on press freedom in the US","quote":"Here's the point in the interview.","text":"Greenwald's been asked, given how things seem to be, whether he would set things up outside the US. ISTR he stated that the Constitution (and First Amendment) still exist and the US is the most appropriate place for the NewCo. I think it was in an interview with Kirsty Wark for Newsnight. EDIT: I misremembered the exact words as he actually comments on press freedom in the US. Here's the point in the interview. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6504728,"author":"logn","month":"2013-10","story":"Interview of Glenn Greenwald [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Is this link from the article to the video on youtube-nocookie.com one of those URLs that the NSA infects my computer with via their spyware server? Here's the standard youtube link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7037982,"author":"thisiswrong","month":"2014-01","story":"Bitcoin vault offering insurance is 'world's first'","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Both are establishment mouthpieces, while one is quite blatant and the other more subtle.","text":"> This is bbc news, not fox news. I expect better. Seeing how bbc 'news' have covered the NSA scandal so far [1] I would no longer say there's much difference between bbc and fox. Both are establishment mouthpieces, while one is quite blatant and the other more subtle. You could perhaps argue that fox 'news' spectators are mostly clueless brainwashed Americans while bbc 'news' still has a strong following among all levels of English society. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"jfPzUgzrOcQ":{"wy":"Cited as the source for the claim that Niemann had many 100% engine-match games; one commenter calls it a credible-seeming analysis.","m":[{"id":42460285,"author":"gradstudent","month":"2024-12","story":"Analyzing the World Chess Championship 2024: Empirical synthesized approach","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis says top players correlate about 70-75% with engines.","quote":"a credible-seeming youtube analysis from last year's Hans Niemann cheating scandal","text":"The analysis mentions the correlation with the played moves vs. engines is ~95% for both players. But I recall a credible-seeming youtube analysis from last year's Hans Niemann cheating scandal which said the best players only have a ~70-75% correlation on average. [VIDEO] I'm trying to cohere these two \"facts\". Does anyone know if the 2024 championship games simply played out along very well established lines?"},{"id":33284467,"author":"Maursault","month":"2022-10","story":"Hans Niemann Suing Magnus Carlsen, Chess.com, and Hikaru Nakamura","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Yosha's engine-correlation analysis of Niemann versus Fischer.","quote":"According to Yosha Iglesias[1] and her anonymous mathematician friend","text":"> Assume for a moment Hans did not cheat (aside from the times he admitted to cheating). In the scenario where he did not cheat - how amazing is his performance? ...In the scenario where he did not cheat, is he really on a Bobby Fischer-like ascendance? I think so, yes, and even considerably moreso. According to Yosha Iglesias[1] and her anonymous mathematician friend, for 5 tournaments in a row starting at the Philadelphia Open in July 2021 Hans Niemann played at an average engine correlation of 73%, beating Bobby Fischer's average engine correlation of 72% during his 20 game winning streak between 2 Dec. 1970 and 30 Sept. 1971. Fischer's peak rating was 2895 in October 1971 at 27yo. Niemann's peak rating was 2699 this month, October 2022, at 19yo. Also according to Yosha, in three years Niemann has played 10 games with a perfect 100% engine correlation and 23 games with engine correlations above 90%.[2] My own chess layman analysis of the experts' analyses is that the best chess players in the world right now generally play games with engine correlations ranging game to game from the mid-60%s to the high-80%s, with much rarer games in the 90%s, and games 100% engine correlated being a once or twice in a lifetime event. Magnus Carlson at his best has an engine correlation of 70%.[1] Unless he cheated, it is quite clear to me, at least, that Hans Niemann is the singular greatest chess player of all time. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":32988738,"author":"syncerr","month":"2022-09","story":"Dear Chess World","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Niemann's string of 100% engine-correlation games.","quote":"Hans has a string of games at 100% correlation[0]","text":"Hans is clearly cheating. Comparing his past games against what an engine would do is pretty damning. Chess engines are far superior to players and the best players in the world top out in the high 70s percent correlations (Magnus averages around 70%). Hans has a string of games at 100% correlation[0], meaning he's playing perfect games. Past players who achieved this later went on to admit to cheating[1]. Magnus knows this because he owns part of chess.com and presumably sees the data. Magnus has a lot riding on his statement. He wouldn't make it unless he was sure. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":32992278,"author":"otherme123","month":"2022-09","story":"Dear Chess World","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Niemann's ten 100% engine-match games in three years.","quote":"There is a video explaining how Hans played ten 100% games","text":"There is a video explaining how Hans played ten 100% games (his moves matching 100% moves of what an engine would do) in the last three years and a lot of >90%: [VIDEO] For reference, Magnus at his best does a 70% match with engines, and between 70-75% is historically enough to earn you the World Championship. This guy is consistently over 80%, come on. Reminds me of Lance Armstrong: a mediocre 90's cyclist that suddenly becomes the best in history, in such dominant fashion that I think everybody suspected something, but without proof you cannot do nothing about it."}]},"ji3Hii_LZOc":{"wy":"Cited in drone regulation discussions as evidence that quadcopters can injure people.","m":[{"id":8932184,"author":"Phlarp","month":"2015-01","story":"Drone carrying drugs crashes near US-Mexico border","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"These are injuries from 450mm sized quads.","text":"Less dangerous than a car? Perhaps. Although I'd point out that we require licensure and insurance for cars, and the threat of life in prison if you injure anyone through neglect while operating a vehicle Baseball? No. These are injuries from 450mm sized quads.[1][2][3] warning some blood / injuries [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":8131272,"author":"MindTwister","month":"2014-08","story":"Drones Outpacing Rules as Popularity Soars in New York","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is why we need regulation and rules","text":"This is why we need regulation and rules: “They started jumping for it,” he said. “I started taunting them, bringing it down and then taking it up. They wouldn’t leave until their mom dragged them away.” If he was flying a Phantom with carbon propellers somebody could have gotten seriously hurt: [VIDEO] The current rules in Denmark (which I believe to be slightly too strict). * No flying within 150m of city areas * No flying within 150m of larger roads * Maximum flying height 100m * No flying above crowded areas (including vacation houses etc) * No flying within 5 km of airports * No flying within 8 km of military airports * No flying over specially protected areas (think wildlife preserves) * Special care must be taken not to endanger lives and property"},{"id":8046937,"author":"spyder","month":"2014-07","story":"Beijing From Above, aka How I was police detained for flying a Quadcopter","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it shows that quadcopters can be dangerous in the wrong hands.","text":"Yea, here is a video about an injury caused by DJI Phantom 2 with carbon fiber blades (warning: some blood dripping): [VIDEO] Sure the propeller is not the original, but it shows that quadcopters can be dangerous in the wrong hands."}]},"t8NCigh54jg":{"wy":"HN rarely recommends it; it is mostly cited in critical threads on Soylent's quality control, including footage of a rodent at its warehouse.","m":[{"id":12895492,"author":"Alupis","month":"2016-11","story":"Soylent Thinks It Found What Was Making People Sick: Algae","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Soylent has practically zero quality control/testing","text":"> How might they acquire that knowledge, other than through the process they seem to currently be executing? Well, a lot of folks would like to see a bit more care paid attention to the entire process. This isn't the first time Soylent has shipped potentially dangerous product. Not long ago, they shipped their new bottled formula to folks, and it arrived covered in mold! Their snack bars have made people violently ill, and now their powder too. Every product Soylent makes, is making or has made people ill. Soylent has practically zero quality control/testing, and doesn't have on-site staff dedicated to ensuring their product is safe. They send out a few carefully selected samples to an external lab, but that's far from continuous testing of the product rolling off the line. Some might attribute it to the \"silicon valley\" mindset Soylent has. \"We'll disrupt the food industry - but we won't bother to learn what it takes to produce uniformly clean and safe product\". There's good reasons the \"big guys\" spend so much on lab personnel and equipment. If people all around the world started getting sick from Coca-Cola, it wouldn't be good - to say the least. Edit - Adding sources: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12895679,"author":"Alupis","month":"2016-11","story":"Soylent Thinks It Found What Was Making People Sick: Algae","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Soylent is classified as a nutritional supplement, which isn't the same as \"food\". It's the same umbrella things like body-building supplements enjoy being under, with far more relaxed requirements than \"food\". Sources are well known, a quick search turns them up[1][2][3]. The testing procedure has been disclosed by Soylent during their last debacle, and there was discussion here on HN[3][4]. I used to buy Soylent myself, started at version 1.5 (powder in the bag). But come on, this sort of thing is seriously not OK. You need better quality control, and you need better (and more rapid) testing of product as it rolls off the line. Hunt and pecking clearly isn't getting it done, and problems are slipping out under their noses. It's people's health - nothing to joke at. \"Move fast; make people sick\" isn't a good mantra. > FWIW, the ingredient in question is \"generally recognized as safe\" by the FDA Soylent has no real idea if that ingredient was the culprit (and from the FDA's classification, it seems reasonable to suspect that ingredient is not the problem). For all anyone knows, it could be a combination of ingredients, or not an ingredient at all, but rather contamination in the manufacturing process. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12895787,"author":"infecto","month":"2016-11","story":"Soylent Thinks It Found What Was Making People Sick: Algae","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I still have vivid memories of the rodents that ran around the original warehouse they mixed soylent in.","text":"Sorry for the strong language but bullshit. I am sure it has changed over the years but I still have vivid memories of the rodents that ran around the original warehouse they mixed soylent in. It was a joke. They had a bunch of randos mixing powders haphazardly and no sense of proper food prep/sanitation. It was scarier than some of the operations that supplement manufacturers run. I am sure its changed but it blows my mind that this is classified as an ambitious project. Edit: Here is the video. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12814719,"author":"vesinisa","month":"2016-10","story":"Soylent halts sales of its powder as customers keep getting sick","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"A TV crew caught on film a rat sneaking around in their","text":"Maybe the rats finally got in: [VIDEO] Explanation: at least in 2013 they were producing the \"food\" in an abandoned old factory with disgustingly little care for hygiene. A TV crew caught on film a rat sneaking around in their \"production facility\"."},{"id":11979360,"author":"cisstrd","month":"2016-06","story":"Introducing Soylent 1.6 Powder","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it certainly shows you how low the standards are","text":"I remember when the Soylent-Creator was on the Colbert Report, and he clearly marketed it as a 100% food replacement. Saying that you don't have to replace food with it completely is smart, it opens up additional customers, makes sense. Their Soylent promotional video however makes the claim that 1) it can replace any meal 2) while not having to replace every meal, it certainly insinuates it can (which is way different from other supplements). [LINK] I also do remember the piece by motherboard on Soylent [VIDEO] Fair, they were a small operation back then, but even if it might not tell you how they work now, it certainly shows you how low the standards are. Their facilities don't even have to be inspected, ... at all. So you are too tired to cook, but fit enough to run? And usually I have to get dressed, have to shower, pack up my things, work over my schedule, read some mails, activities where I can have something on the stove at the same time. If something needs 10 minutes to cook I am not staring into the pot for the duration. The composition of the ingredients does not tell me how they know, where they buy, anything in beyond. I rather see food, touch it, smell it, know where it's from, I don't really bother about whether the fish I just bought has 1 gram fat more or less, that's not the \"quality\" in food I am looking for."}]},"u48vYSLvKNQ":{"wy":"Posted as a joke in layoff threads, since the show already satirized exactly this kind of corporate layoff scenario.","m":[{"id":34451973,"author":"mrtksn","month":"2023-01","story":"Google Announces Layoffs: A difficult decision to set us up for the future","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"A CEO taking responsibility for firing people always reminds me of Gavin Belson's speech: [VIDEO] Did ever a journalist tried to ask how they took responsibility? Did they faced difficulties in their lives or careers or did they couldn't concentrate on Succession's finale and had to rewind? Maybe taking responsibility actually means they did something very positive for the company within the rules of the game and expect a bonus? Are they referring to the events as a mistake they made or are they saying that they were able to grab the talent pool and didn't miss the opportunity? After all, CEOs and workers have different responsibilities. Hiring and firing for the opportunity and change in conditions is CEOs job and maybe he takes responsibility for properly executing it?"},{"id":34459118,"author":"sireat","month":"2023-01","story":"Google Announces Layoffs: A difficult decision to set us up for the future","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Whenever I see CEO taking \"full responsibility\" and staying put I am reminded of Gavin: [VIDEO] Full responsibility that does not involve massive shakeup at the top is not full responsibility."},{"id":34527067,"author":"reducesuffering","month":"2023-01","story":"Macroeconomic Changes Have Made It Impossible for Me to Want to Pay You","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Make no mistake, though they are the ones leaving, it is I who must remain, and bear the heavy burden of their failure.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":33530875,"author":"sireat","month":"2022-11","story":"Meta lays off 11,000 people","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Typical Gavin Belson move: [VIDEO] Of course it has been done for millennia. How does a CEO with enough class B shares to control shareholder voting take accountability? Self flagellation perhaps?"},{"id":32324769,"author":"ChuckNorris89","month":"2022-08","story":"Robinhood lays off 23% of staff","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Silicon Valley (the satire TV show) already covered this exact scenario.","text":"Silicon Valley (the satire TV show) already covered this exact scenario. Mike Judge is a brilliant writer. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32423932,"author":"mrtksn","month":"2022-08","story":"CEO's LinkedIn crying selfie about layoffs met with backlash","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The moment I saw it, it reminded me the Gavin Belson speech[0] from Silicon Valley about firing a team in his company. Life imitates art I guess. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"ukgnU2aXM2c":{"wy":"Cited critically as an example of overhyped startup claims and of presentations by founders whose product never worked.","m":[{"id":35755906,"author":"rasz","month":"2023-04","story":"Presentations and pitch decks by failures and frauds","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Spoilers: She is a fraud, it never worked","text":"Missing Meredith Perry and her uBeam trying to sell idea of ubiquitous wireless charging. Tedtalk [VIDEO] Analysis [LINK] \"for each technological hurdle deemed insurmountable by the experts I would spend just a few hours thinking about the problem from a variety of approaches so I was able to solve problems when the PhD experts couldn't with just a few hours of really simple research every single argument over why the technology couldn't work has been indisputably wrong has taught me to be skeptical of experts the expertise represented a narrow way of looking at things engineers are inherently linear thinkers and tend to take a very binary approach to solving problems as a non expert I had an advantage because I could look at a problem from different angles because I just didn't know it was possible by thinking outside the box by thinking around corners you can out think the top thinkers and now eight months later I have four of the top ultrasonic engineers in the world working for me we're working with me it's going to work and it's going to be awesome and I can't wait to give the middle finger and smiled all the engineers that criticize the crap out of me\" Spoilers: She is a fraud, it never worked, one of the ultrasonic engineers she boasted about hiring even ran a blog debunking her claims [LINK] . >$20 million from Andreessen Horowitz/Mark Cuban/Marissa Mayer down the crapper. At least it didnt manage to IPO."},{"id":28084189,"author":"random314","month":"2021-08","story":"Yann LeCun on his start in AI, recent self-supervised learning research [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of this talk by the failed tech startup's non technical CEO","text":"I trust Hinton and LeCun who have actually created the trends and bigger picture and invented the now \"current\" techniques over commentators who show up years later and offer shallow non peer reviewed analysis and predictions. Reminds me of this talk by the failed tech startup's non technical CEO who apparently was able to see the bigger picture that scientist aspergers needs weren't able to see. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18271774,"author":"Afrotechmods","month":"2018-10","story":"UBeam CEO Meredith Perry steps aside amidst B2B pivot","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Does anyone know if she ever apologized for this awful speech?","text":"1) This is the blog by the former VP of Engineering that the article references: [LINK] 2) Does anyone know if she ever apologized for this awful speech? [VIDEO] 3) This uBeam shit is literally one of the reasons why I created a beginner's course in wireless power. It drives me nuts that investors have gotten ripped off so many times in this industry and the degree to which the media has been complicit in the hype train. If Mark Cuban or any other VCs happen to be reading this, please watch this course before putting any more money into wireless power. I created a coupon so you can watch it for free. [LINK]"},{"id":18274464,"author":"sambull","month":"2018-10","story":"UBeam CEO Meredith Perry steps aside amidst B2B pivot","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"she just believes she knows better","text":"Her TEDx video [0] makes it clear, she just believes she knows better and within an hour of any subject matter that has experts she could revolutionize their field by just thinking outside the box. The experts too limited in their thinking to see how awesome it could work lol [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18279090,"author":"nikanj","month":"2018-10","story":"UBeam CEO Meredith Perry steps aside amidst B2B pivot","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"One of the most ironic parts is Meredith Perry's TEDx talk","text":"One of the most ironic parts is Meredith Perry's TEDx talk, where she's proud about not having any education in the field. \"I was not an engineer, and I did not have Asperger's(!!)\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":14772191,"author":"gh02t","month":"2017-07","story":"Juicero Plans to Lower Prices and Cut 25% of Staff","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The talk by the uBeam CEO he links is also pretty infuriating.","text":"Here's Dave Jones ripping into it pretty hard. [LINK] The talk by the uBeam CEO he links is also pretty infuriating. [VIDEO] Her core hypothesis that experts can often get stuck \"inside the box\" is certainly valid, but on the other hand \"I sat down and solved complex engineering problems in a few minutes with pencil and paper\" (almost an exact quote from the talk) is just plain dumb. Not to mention the not subtle implication that good engineers are all socially retarded..."},{"id":12915902,"author":"maverick_iceman","month":"2016-11","story":"Walgreen Sues Theranos, Seeks $140 Million in Damages","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"delivered a TED talk[1] which was nauseating","text":"I looked into uBeam a while back. The technology is unproven and unlikely to work because of basic physics. (They are trying to transmit energy using ultrasonic sound and it usually has severe health consequences for any significant amount of power delivered.) The CEO, Meredith Perry, delivered a TED talk[1] which was nauseating. She was basically saying it doesn't matter what the experts say, if you're a plucky disruptor you should just follow your half-baked ideas. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"uvPbj9NX0zc":{"wy":"Used as a joke whenever a discussion has someone grudgingly listing the benefits of something they criticize.","m":[{"id":30463560,"author":"sterlind","month":"2022-02","story":"Why does Google still have the “I’m Feeling Lucky” button?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'm reminded of the \"what have the Romans ever done for us?\" scene from Life of Brian","text":"I'm reminded of the \"what have the Romans ever done for us?\" scene from Life of Brian: [VIDEO] I'm not sure the history with workspaces, but Google Docs etc. were very important. Google also gets points in my book just by scaling YouTube, Maps, Gmail etc. To the degree they've published their work it's been revolutionary to the field. Excellent engineering with terrible/conservative corporate strategy, imo."},{"id":25673320,"author":"netsharc","month":"2021-01","story":"Facebook Indefinitely Suspends Trump","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> DNC now has to spend most of the first half of Bidens term trying to rebuild the nations confidence. One hopes, but I'd imagine the people living in the alternate reality will just ask \"What have the Romans ever done for us?!\"[0] [0] Reference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22018586,"author":"seer","month":"2020-01","story":"Health and Wealth in the Roman Empire (2019)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the hilarious sketch from Monty Python","text":"The romans did envy the Greek culture quite a lot - Pompeii and Herculan are filled with “copies of greek statues”. And they did copy the mythology wholesale. But speaking about this I can’t not mention the hilarious sketch from Monty Python - [what did the romans ever do for us]( [VIDEO] ) TL;DR; Quite a lot given the relative cultures and civilizations of the time. Have in mind that a lot of the greeks were themselves a bunch of cruel slaving rednecks too - [LINK]"}]}});
